hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3001","2633 - And what Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim had narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Abd Allah ibn Muhayriz, from Abd Allah ibn al-Sadi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that this does not contradict anything we already reported in this chapter, for it may mean the disbelievers of Makkah who were fought at the conquest of Makkah until it was opened over them by what Allah Almighty opened. He said: Does this contradict?"
"3002","2634 - He mentioned what al-Harawi had narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Awf al-Jurashi, from Abu Hind al-Bajali, that he heard Muawiyah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Hijrah is not cut off until tawbah is cut off, and tawbah is not cut off until the sun rises from its west; he said that three times. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that this hijrah mentioned in this hadith is not the hijrah mentioned in the first hadiths; it is the hijrah of evil, not the other hijrah mentioned in the first reports. Do you not see him saying: until tawbah is cut off, that is, the hijrah by which what preceded it is abandoned, which tawbah cut off. What proves that is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguishing between these two hijrahs."
"3003","2635 - As Ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Damdam ibn Zurah narrated to us, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, from Malik ibn Yakhamir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Hijrah is two traits, one is to abandon evils, and to emigrate to Allah Almighty and to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Hijrah is not cut off while tawbah is accepted, and it remains accepted until the sun rises from its west; when it rises, every heart is sealed with what is in it, and people are spared deeds. It was also reported in this chapter."
"3004","2636 - What al-Rabi al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman narrated to me, Abu Jafar said: He is the son of Abd Allah ibn Hanzalah, washed by the angels, he said: Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd narrated to me, from al-Harith ibn Ziyad, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of al-Khandaq while he pledged people upon hijrah, and said: O Messenger of Allah, will you not pledge this one? He said: And who is this? I said: My cousin Hawt ibn Yazid. He said: No, O company of Ansar, you do not emigrate to anyone, but people emigrate to you."
"3005","2637 - And what Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Ghasil narrated to us, from Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd, from al-Harith ibn Ziyad, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: My cousin, without naming him, and added: By Him in whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, no man loves the Ansar until he meets Allah Almighty but he meets Allah Almighty loving him, and no man hates the Ansar until he meets Allah Almighty but he meets Allah Almighty hating him."
"3006","2638 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman ibn al-Ghasil narrated to us, he said: Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd narrated to me, whose father was a Badri, he said: al-Harith ibn Ziyad al-Saidi al-Ansari narrated to me that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of al-Khandaq while he pledged people upon hijrah, and said: This is Hawt ibn Yazid, or Yazid ibn Hawt, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This in our view, and Allah knows best, does not contradict anything we already reported in this chapter, for this was before the conquest of Makkah, a time of emigration, unlike what is after the conquest of Makkah. It was also reported concerning the second hijrah after the conquest of Makkah."
"3007","2639 - What Ibn Abi Dawud and Ibn Abi Maryam both had narrated to us, they both said: Abu Isa Fudayk ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salih ibn Bashir ibn Fudayk, he said: Fudayk went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, they claim whoever did not emigrate perished. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Fudayk, establish prayer, give zakah, abandon evil, and dwell where you wish of your people's land, you are an emigrant. In this hadith is the exposition of the hijrah entered by one who enters it after the conquest of Makkah, that it is by his abandoning evil, that it does not bar residing outside Madinah, and that it differs from the hijrah barring residence in the abode the emigrant had been from. In what we mentioned thereof is exposition of what we described. We found what is more proving of what we mentioned, namely the saying of Allah Almighty in His Book: And the foremost, the first of the emigrants and the helpers, and those who followed them in goodness, Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him, and He prepared for them gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever; that is the great triumph. He informed that the foremost He mentioned in this verse are the emigrants, and it was understood He meant thereby those who emigrated to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the abode they had been in among abodes of disbelief, of Makkah and others, to the abode of hijrah, namely Madinah. It was understood the helpers He mentioned therein are those to whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, with what was from them in his affair of belief in him, affirming him, and expending themselves and their wealth for him, until Allah Almighty opened through them the greatest of abodes wherein were disbelievers in him, averse to him, fighting him. It was understood those who followed them in goodness are those who entered Islam after that, after Makkah became an abode of Islam. What proves that is what we reported above in our book of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Mujashi when he came to him with his brother after the conquest to pledge him upon hijrah: No, rather he pledges upon Islam, for there is no hijrah after the conquest, and he shall be of the followers in goodness. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"3008","Chapter 418 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: Allah Almighty, when He wants good for a man, asalahu. 2640 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Kathir ibn Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from his father, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Amr ibn al-Hamiq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah wants good for His servant, He asalahu. They said: And how does He asiluhu? He said: He guides him to a righteous deed until He takes him upon it."
"3009","2641 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, he narrated to him from his father, from Amr ibn al-Hamiq, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When Allah Almighty wants good for a servant, He asalahu. Do you know what His asalahu is? They said: Allah Almighty and His Messenger know best. He said: Allah Almighty opens for him a righteous deed before his death, until his neighbors or those around him are pleased with him. He said: We sought the meaning of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and found the Arabs say: This is a spear in which is asal, meaning therein is quivering. He likened its speed, which is its quivering, to the quivering of other spears and others. So it may be his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When Allah wants good for a servant, He asalahu, means He inclines him to what He loves of righteous deeds, until that becomes a cause for entering him into His Paradise. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"3010","Chapter 419 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning providing divorced women. 2642 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, that he asked Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Amr ibn Hafs about the divorce by his grandfather Abu Amr of Fatimah daughter of Qays. Abd al-Hamid said to him: He divorced her irrevocably, then went out to Yemen, delegating Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah. Ayyash sent her some maintenance, and she disliked it. Ayyash said to her: You have upon us neither maintenance nor lodging; here is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so ask him. She asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what he said, and he said: You have neither maintenance nor lodging, but provision honourably; leave them. She said: Shall I go out to the house of Umm Sharik? He said to her: Her house is frequented; move to the house of Abd Allah ibn Umm Maktum the blind, for it is less so."
"3011","2643 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad, from Abu Salamah, from Fatimah daughter of Qays herself, with the like of the hadith of al-Layth from Abu al-Zubayr, letter by letter. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith among what is ascribed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is: You have upon them neither maintenance nor lodging, but provision honourably. It may be upon obligation, and may be upon recommendation and urging, not upon obligation. We reflected and found Allah Almighty mentioned providing divorced women in three places of His Book: His saying: And for divorced women is provision honourably, a duty upon the Godfearing; and His saying: Provision honourably, a duty upon the doers of good; and His saying Almighty: There is no blame upon you if you divorce women whom you have not touched nor fixed for them an obligation, but provide them, the verse. That may be like His saying Almighty: Prescribed upon you when death attends one of you, if he leaves good, is bequest for parents and near kin honourably, a duty upon the Godfearing. That was upon recommendation and urging, not upon obligation. Likewise His saying Almighty concerning provisions of divorced women, a duty upon the doers of good and a duty upon the Godfearing, may be upon encouragement and urging, applying to all divorced women, entered upon or not."
"3012","As was reported from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, among what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala had narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub and Musa ibn Ayyub al-Ghafiqi informed me, from their uncle Iyas ibn Amir, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, saying that, meaning: For every divorced woman is a provision. It may be upon recommendation and urging, and may be upon obligation for some of them apart from others, as was reported from Abd Allah ibn Umar in that."
"3013","Among what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, that he used to say: For every divorced woman is a provision, except one divorced with a dower fixed for her; half of what was fixed for her suffices her."
"3014","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, and he mentioned the like of it. In this of the saying of Ibn Umar is the exclusion of women divorced before consummation from the provisions we mentioned. Then we sought its ruling by reasoning, and found the obligatory compensation for intimacy becomes due by the occurrence and binding of marriages, not by other than that. Since provisions are not obligated by marriages with no divorce alongside, it is fitter that divorce after them does not obligate them. If an objector says: We saw divorce obligating maintenance and lodging in the waiting period, though they were not obligatory before that. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that the matter is not as he stated; rather they had been obligatory by marriage, an obligation the occurring divorce therein did not lift. This is proof for the obligation of provision for divorced women after consummation. As for women divorced before consummation, the people of knowledge differed concerning them, whether they have provisions judged upon their divorcers who had not fixed a dower for them or not. Some said: They have provisions upon them, though they differed in amounts of provisions. Some among them said: It is the amount sufficing in prayer of clothing. Among those who said that are many Kufans, among them Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri, and those holding their saying. Others among them said: The amount of provision here is half the dower of her like among her women, to whose dower-like recourse is to likes of dowers of their likes. Among those who said that: Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, and this is worthiest of what they said therein upon their principles on which they built this meaning. Other people of knowledge besides them said: Provisions here are urged, commanded but not compelled. Among those who said that: Malik ibn Anas. He contradicted the others we mentioned therein, for those obligate them, compel over them, and imprison for them. The worthiest of what was said therein in our view, and Allah knows best, was obligating them and imprisoning for them, for marriage occurring without naming a dower obligated for her the dower of her like upon her husband, as it obligated ownership of her intimacy to her husband. When divorce occurred before consummation, it dropped from the husband half of what was obligatory upon him before divorce, of what had been wholly detainable had he not divorced. When he divorced, half dropped from him by divorce, and the remaining half stays upon him as it had been upon him before that of his obligating it, his being taken by it and imprisoned for it, just as when he named for her a dower then divorced her before consummating with her, half of it leaves him, and the remaining half is hers upon him upon the ruling of all of it she had upon him before divorce, of its binding him for her and his imprisonment for her therein. Reports from the predecessors concerning provisions by divorce were reported; we shall mention them in this chapter, Allah willing."
"3015","Among them is what Sulayman ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam informed me that a man disputed to Shurayh concerning his wife's provision. Shurayh said: And for divorced women is provision honourably, a duty upon the Godfearing. If you are of the Godfearing, provision is upon you. And he did not judge with it."
"3016","And among them is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: For every divorced woman is a provision."
"3017","And what Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3018","And among them is what Yusuf had narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik informed us, from Ata, he said: For every divorced woman is provision, except one he divorced before consummating with her with a dower fixed for her; she has half the dower."
"3019","And among them is what Yusuf had narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim and Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, the like of it."
"3020","And among them is what Yusuf had narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir informed us, from al-Dahhak, that he said: For every divorced woman is provision, even the woman who ransoms herself. In what we mentioned above in this chapter is what proves the correct of what they said therein, among what we mentioned from them. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"3021","Chapter 420 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning leftovers of beasts and other animals, of purity and otherwise. Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah ibn Salamah al-Azdi al-Tahawi narrated to us, he said: 2644 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah Hammad ibn Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir al-Makhzumi, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Umar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about water frequented by beasts, and said: When water reaches two qullahs, it does not carry filth. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is proof that water below two qullahs carries filth."
"3022","2645 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Hannad ibn al-Sari and al-Husayn ibn Hurayth narrated to us, from Abu Usamah, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about water frequented by animals and beasts, and said: When water is two qullahs, it does not carry filth. In this hadith is the inclusion of animals with beasts in this ruling we mentioned."
"3023","2646 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked about desert pools drunk from by beasts, and said: When water reaches two qullahs, it does not carry impurity. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the like of what is in the hadith with which we began our report in this chapter. An objector said: How do you accept this hadith concerning leftovers of beasts and animals, while you report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein what contradicts what you reported in this chapter therein?"
"3024","2647 - He mentioned what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3025","And what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi had narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, then both together said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about pools between Makkah and Madinah. They said: O Messenger of Allah, beasts, dogs, and donkeys come to them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For them is what is in their bellies, and what remains is for us pure. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that this hadith he mentioned is not among hadiths argued with their like, for it turns solely upon Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, whose hadith according to hadith scholars is in the extremity of weakness. Then we sought this chapter's ruling in other than what we reported therein of what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"3026","2648 - We found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Qurrah ibn Khalid, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The purification of a vessel when a dog laps in it is to wash it seven times, the first with dust."
"3027","2649 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Qurrah ibn Khalid, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The purification of a vessel when a cat laps in it is washing once or twice. Qurrah doubted."
"3028","2650 - And we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi had narrated to us, he said: Sawwar ibn Abd Allah al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Ayyub narrating from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A vessel is washed when a dog laps in it seven times, the first of them, or he said: the first, with dust; if a cat laps in it, it is washed once. Abu Jafar said: In that is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing of the impurity of a cat's leftover, as his informing of the impurity of a dog's leftover, though he differed in what purifies them, making it for the dog seven, and for the cat once. An objector said: How do you accept this from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Hisham ibn Hassan reported it from Muhammad ibn Sirin halting it upon Abu Hurayrah, not passing beyond to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? And he mentioned."
"3029","What Bakkar had narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir al-Dubai narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The purification of one's vessel when a dog laps in it is to wash it seven times, the first with dust."
"3030","And what Bakkar had narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A cat's leftover is poured out, and the vessel is washed once or twice. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Majestic and Mighty, was that Ayyub is above Hisham in eminence and firmness, so his addition over him in this hadith's chain is accepted. Qurrah, even if not above Hisham in firmness and memory, was not below him therein, besides that Muhammad ibn Sirin, when he halted hadiths of Abu Hurayrah and was asked: Is it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? would say: Every hadith of Abu Hurayrah is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3031","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Ismail and Yahya ibn Atiq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that when he narrated from Abu Hurayrah and was asked: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? he said: Every hadith of Abu Hurayrah is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: That proves Muhammad raised this hadith once, and Ayyub and Qurrah took it from him likewise, while he halted it upon Abu Hurayrah once after informing people that every hadith of Abu Hurayrah is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Hisham heard it from him likewise, though in reality it is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). An objector said: Aishah reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the cat's leftover establishing its purity."
"3032","2651 - He mentioned what Bakkar had narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rijal narrated to us, from his mother Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe from one vessel, though a cat had drunk from it before that. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Majestic and Mighty and His help, was that this hadith is among Muammal's errors in its chain from al-Thawri; he reported it from him, from Abu al-Rijal, while the trustworthy reliable Abu al-Rijal, rather it is from Harithah ibn Abi al-Rijal, who is spoken of in his hadith and weakened to the extreme."
"3033","2652 - As Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Abi al-Rijal, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that. Then we considered whether anything besides this hadith was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or not."
"3034","2653 - We found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman had narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Salih ibn Dinar, from his mother, that a freedwoman of Aishah sent her with harisah to Aishah. She found her praying, and she gestured: Put it down. A cat came and ate of it. When Aishah finished, she said to the women: Eat, but avoid the place of the cat's mouth. Aishah circled it, then ate from where the cat ate, then said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not impure; it is of those going about you. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing ablution with its leftover."
"3035","2654 - And we found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Salih, from his mother, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a cat's leftover. We reflected on this hadith and found it goes back to the mother of Dawud ibn Salih, who is not of the narrators from whom the like of this is taken, nor known to people of knowledge. Then we considered whether other than this hadith was reported in this meaning proving the purity of a cat's leftover."
"3036","2655 - We found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Humaydah daughter of Ubayd ibn Rifah, from Kabshah daughter of Kab ibn Malik, who was under Ibn Abi Qatadah, that Abu Qatadah entered upon her and she poured for him ablution water. A cat came and drank of it, and Abu Qatadah inclined the vessel to it till it drank. Kabshah said: He saw me looking at him and said: Do you marvel, O daughter of my brother? She said: I said: Yes. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not impure; it is of those going about you, male or female. Abu Jafar said: His saying it is not impure may mean its being in houses and touching clothes, not the purity of its leftover. What proves the purity of its leftover in this hadith is the act of Abu Qatadah therein, his performing ablution with it. Two men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contradicted him therein: Abd Allah ibn Umar and Abu Hurayrah, holding its impurity."
"3037","As Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Yahya al-Ushnani narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Waqid ibn Muhammad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Do not perform ablution from a donkey's leftover, nor a dog's, nor a cat's."
"3038","And as al-Rabi al-Jizi had narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A vessel is washed from a cat as it is washed from a dog."
"3039","And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Khayr ibn Nuaym, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it. The view of Abu Qatadah therein was not worthier than their view therein. Those of the successors agreeing with them upon their view therein: Said ibn al-Musayyab, al-Hasan, and Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari."
"3040","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said."
"3041","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Said ibn al-Musayyab, in the hadith of Ibn Marzuq, he said: When a cat laps in a vessel, wash it twice or thrice; in the hadith of Ibn Khuzaymah, one of them said: He washes it once, and the other said: He washes it twice."
"3042","And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me that he asked Yahya ibn Said about animals whose leftover is not used for ablution. He said: The pig, the dog, and the cat. An objector said: In the hadith of Abu Hurayrah you reported, that a vessel is washed from a cat's lapping therein as it is washed from a dog's lapping therein, must they thereby be washed equally, with no preference in washing one over the other? Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Majestic and Mighty and His help, was that it may mean the vessel is washed from each of them with a washing differing in number from what it is washed of the other, combining them in that it is washed from both. This is Arabic, and such is found in the Arabs' language. Allah Majestic and Mighty said: There is no animal on earth nor bird flying with its wings but communities like you. He informed they are communities like us, not meaning they are like us in the creation wherein we and they differ, nor that they are like us in our being bound by what Allah Almighty brought us in worship, which He did not bind them with. Likewise His saying Almighty: And of the earth the like of them, meaning like the heavens, not meaning in what He created them upon, but that they are of the number as the heavens are of number. Likewise the saying of Abu Hurayrah: A vessel is washed from a cat as it is washed from a dog, not that it is washed from a cat seven times as it is washed from a dog seven times, but that it is washed as the dog is washed thereof, though they differ in number. What Ibn Umar and Abu Hurayrah said therein was confirmed by what was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the cat, that it is of beasts."
"3043","2656 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rabiah al-Kilabi narrated to us, from Isa ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cat is of beasts."
"3044","2657 - And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog and the cat. In the hadith of Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that the cat is of beasts, and in the hadith of Jabir from him is the forbidding of their price as his forbidding of the dog's price. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast and every taloned bird, which we shall mention with what was reported therein later in our book in a place worthier than this, Allah willing. In that is the forbidding of their meats, and it was understood that whatever of water touches a thing, that water takes that thing's ruling in purity and impurity. We found meats upon four aspects: of them pure eaten meat, namely camel, cattle, and sheep meat, so their leftovers are pure, for they touched pure meats. Of them pure uneaten meat, namely the meats of the children of Adam, so their leftovers are pure, for they touched pure meats. Of them forbidden meat, namely pig and dog meat, so the leftover thereof is forbidden, for they touched forbidden meat. These are three kinds of meats whose leftovers were judged by their ruling in purity and prohibition. Other meats remained, namely domestic donkey meats, meats of every fanged beast, including the cat and its likes, and meats of every taloned bird. The meats of those things were barred from eating by the Sunnah, and analogy upon the three kinds of meats we mentioned whose leftovers' ruling was referred to their rulings in purity and impurity is that these things' leftovers also be referred to their rulings. Just as their meats were in the Sunnah forbidden, barred, their leftovers are likewise forbidden, barred, as was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confirming that, as was reported from Abd Allah ibn Umar and from Abu Hurayrah in their agreement therein, as was reported from those below them among the successors we mentioned, namely Said ibn al-Musayyab, al-Hasan al-Basri, Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari, and those agreeing with them of the rank below theirs, among them Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"3045","Chapter 421 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his commanding openness and warning against secrecy. 2658 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Advise me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not associate anything with Allah Almighty, establish prayer, give zakah, perform Hajj and Umrah, hear and obey. Upon you is openness, and beware secrecy. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith to stand upon its intent, Allah willing. What occurred to our hearts as the worthiest of what it may bear is that openness is meant among people, that they be with one another upon what appears of them to them, not going beyond that to seeking their secrets, for they cannot reach their realities, since Allah Almighty concealed them from them, and since He barred them from it concerning them, by His saying Almighty: And do not pursue what you have no knowledge of; the hearing, the sight, and the heart, all of those shall be questioned thereof. Likewise what was reported from Umar ibn al-Khattab addressing people."
"3046","As Malik ibn Yahya Abu Ghassan al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, (Ha). And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, Malik said in his hadith: al-Jurayri informed us, and Yazid said in his hadith: Saeed al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Firas, he said: I witnessed Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressing the people; he praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: O people, we used to know you when revelation was coming down, and when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us, and when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, was informing us of your affairs. Revelation has now ceased and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has passed away. So I know you only by what I say: whoever we see good from, we think good of him and love him for it, and whoever we see evil from, we think evil of him and hate him for it. Your secrets are between you and your Lord, Mighty and Majestic. Similar to that is what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about commanding openness and leaving secrets alone, and similar to that is what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed to the one who killed the man after he said: There is no god but Allah, and after he excused himself for that to him, that he had only said it seeking protection: Did you not split open his heart, meaning: you cannot reach from him anything beyond what his tongue uttered and what you heard from him. And we ask Allah for success."
"3047","Chapter 422: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the fluid of the man and the fluid of the woman, and about the effect of each of them on the child created from them. 2659 - Muhammad ibn Abda ibn Abd Allah ibn Zayd al-Marwazi Abu Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abu Tawba al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Sallam narrated to us, from his brother Zayd, that he heard Abu Sallam saying: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to me that Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him: that a scholar of the Jews said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I ask you about the child. He said: The fluid of the man is white, and the fluid of the woman is yellow. When they combine, and the semen of the man predominates over the semen of the woman, they produce a male by the permission of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. And when the semen of the woman predominates over the semen of the man, they produce a female by the permission of Allah. The Jew said: You have spoken the truth, and you are indeed a Prophet. Then he turned and left. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He asked me about what he asked me, and I had no knowledge of any of it until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, brought it to me. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith, when the fluid of the man predominates it produces a male by Allah's permission, and when the fluid of the woman predominates it produces a female by Allah's permission. Then an objector said: You have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when the fluid of one of them predominates over the fluid of the other, it does something other than this meaning."
"3048","2660 - He mentioned what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Qutayba ibn Saeed narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida narrated to us, from his father, from Musab ibn Shayba, from Musafi ibn Abd Allah al-Hajabi, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Aisha, that a woman said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Does a woman wash when she has a wet dream and sees the fluid? He said: Yes. Aisha said to her: May your hands be dusty. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave her alone; whence does resemblance come except from that? When her fluid predominates over the fluid of the man, the child resembles his maternal uncles, and when the fluid of the man predominates over hers, he resembles him."
"3049","2661 - And what Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiya al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Zaynab, from Umm Salama, she said: Umm Sulaym came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah is not shy of the truth; is washing obligatory on a woman when she has a wet dream? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When she sees the fluid. Umm Salama covered her face and said: O Messenger of Allah, does a woman have a wet dream? He said: May your hands be dusty; how else does her child resemble her?"
"3050","2662 - And what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Saeed ibn Abi Saeed al-Maqburi, from Abd Allah ibn Rafi, the freedman of Umm Salama, from Umm Salama, that Umm Sulaym, the wife of Abu Talha, said: O Messenger of Allah, is washing obligatory on a woman who sees her husband in a dream having intercourse with her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, when she sees wetness. Umm Salama said: O Messenger of Allah, does a woman do that? He said: May your forehead be dusty; whence does maternal-uncle resemblance come except from that? Whichever of the two fluids reaches the womb first dominates in resemblance. He said: In this hadith, when the fluid of one of them predominates over the fluid of the other, the resemblance belongs to it, and this is different from masculinization and feminization. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is that this which he mentioned does not contradict what we mentioned in the first section of this chapter; because what is in the first section mentioned at the beginning of this chapter about masculinization and feminization is by the predominance of one fluid over the other in the womb, and what is in the second section is by the precedence of one fluid over the other, and resemblance and creation belong to it, yet it is not from it alone but from it and from the other fluid. When there is the other fluid, resemblance belongs to it, after Allah's prior decree of masculinization and feminization in it. Each of these two meanings is different from the other: one concerns the cause of masculinization and feminization, and the other concerns the cause of resemblance. And we ask Allah for success. If an objector says: In Aisha's hadith which you narrated in this section: When her fluid predominates over the fluid of the man the child resembles his maternal uncles, and when the fluid of the man predominates over hers he resembles him. It is said to him: So it is in this hadith, but the hadith scholars do not consider Musab ibn Shayba's hadith strong; rather what is in al-Maqburi's hadith: Whichever of the two fluids reaches the womb first dominates in resemblance, is the correct one according to them. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3051","Chapter 423: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the angel in the womb asking his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, about the creature from the drop, male or female, after the time that passed over the drop in the womb before that, and whether it contradicts what we mentioned in the previous chapter or not. 2663 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan saying: Amr narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Hudhayfa ibn Asid al-Ghifari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, or the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - the doubt is from Ibn Uyayna -: The angel enters upon the drop after it settles in the womb for forty, and says: O Lord, what, wretched or blessed? Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says, and they are written. Then he says: O Lord, male or female? Allah says, and they are written: his provision, his deed, his trace, and his calamity. Then the pages are folded, and nothing is added to what is in them nor decreased."
"3052","2664 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, that Amir ibn Wathila narrated to him, from Hudhayfa ibn Asid al-Ghifari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When forty-two nights pass over the drop, Allah sends an angel to it; he forms it, and creates its hearing, its sight, its skin, its flesh, and its bones. Then he says: O Lord, male or female? Your Lord, Mighty and Majestic, decrees what He wills, and the angel writes. Then he says: O Lord, his term. Your Lord, Mighty and Majestic, says what He wills, and the angel writes. Then he says: O Lord, his provision. Your Lord decrees what He wills, and the angel writes. Then he comes out with the page in his hand, neither adding to his command nor decreasing."
"3053","2665 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Waki narrated to us, he said: Ghiyath ibn Bashir narrated to us, he said: Khusayf narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, raising it, he said: When the drop settles in the womb for forty days and forty nights, the angel comes and says: What shall I write? He says: Write his lifespan, his term, his provision, his calamity, and wretched or blessed. Ibn Abi Dawud did not mention to us in his hadith anything besides this."
"3054","2666 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isa Ibn al-Tabba narrated to us, he said: Ghiyath ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the drop falls in the womb, an angel is entrusted with it; the angel says: O Lord, male or female? Wretched or blessed? And what is the provision and what is the term? He said: So it is written in his mother's belly. Then an objector said: In Hudhayfa ibn Asid's hadith which you narrated in this chapter, the creation from the drop, what is created from it of males and females, occurs only after the mentioned period passes. Does that contradict what you narrated in the first chapter in Thawban's hadith? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: each of Hudhayfa ibn Asid's hadith and Thawban's hadith is upon a meaning different from the other's. For what is in Thawban's hadith is what occurs from the semen before it becomes a drop, of what Allah decreed in it to be male or female, with the predominance of one semen over the other. Then its hearing and sight are fashioned, as in Hudhayfa's hadith, after the mentioned period, and the angel then asks his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, seeking knowledge of what preceded from Him concerning it, male or female, to write that in the page in which he writes it. Allah's prior knowledge of which of the two kinds it is has preceded that. And we ask Allah for success."
"3055","Chapter 424: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who sows in another man's land without his order, to whom that crop belongs, the owner of the land or the sower. 2667 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us. 2668 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us. 2669 - And al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us. They all said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabii. Ahmad and Fahd said in their hadith: from Rafi ibn Khadij, and al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah said in his hadith: from Abu Ishaq al-Sabii, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sows in a people's land without their permission has nothing of the crop, and his expenses are returned to him. In this hadith, whoever sows anything in a man's land without his order, what comes out of that crop belongs to the owner of that land, not the sower, and the sower has upon the owner of the land the expenses he spent on it. We do not know anyone among the people of knowledge who adhered to this hadith and held by it except Sharik ibn Abd Allah al-Nakhai. As for others among the people of knowledge, they are upon its opposite. With us it is a sound view, because of what has strengthened it from this hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and because what that man sowed in that land has transformed in it and become consumed in it, then what came about thereafter is different from it, and its cause was the land in which it was sown. So the owner of the land has the right to say to the one who sowed in it what he sowed: what is in my land of what it caused is other than what you sowed in it, so it is mine, not yours. Except that you spent expenses on it until what my land produced came about through it, so those expenses for what returned to me, of what my land caused, are expenses upon something that has become mine, not yours. So those expenses are upon me for you. This is a sound view that should not be opposed. There has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what strengthens it, which we shall mention in the chapter following this chapter, if Allah wills. And in Him is success."
"3056","Chapter 425: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who sows in another's land a crop upon a corrupt sharecropping deal, what is the ruling in it. 2670 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya - meaning al-Qattan - narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar al-Khatmi narrated to us, he said: I came to Saeed ibn al-Musayyib and said: Something about sharecropping reached us from you. He said: Ibn Umar used to see no harm in it until there was mentioned to him a hadith from Rafi ibn Khadij about it. He went to Rafi, and Rafi informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Banu Haritha and saw crops in the land of Zuhayr. He said: How fine is the land of Zuhayr! They said: It is not Zuhayr's. He said: Is it not the land of Zuhayr? They said: Yes, but he let so-and-so cultivate it. He said: Return his expenses to him, and take your crop. Rafi said: So we returned his expenses to him and took our crop. Saeed said: Lend your brother or hire him for dirhams."
"3057","2671 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Jafar al-Khatmi, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"3058","2672 - And Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Baghandi and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn Amir narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Nun, he said: Rafi ibn Khadij narrated to me that he sowed a land. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was watering it, and asked him: To whom does the crop belong and to whom does the land belong? He said: My crop with my seed and my labor; half is mine and half is for the sons of so-and-so. He said: You have taken usury; return the land to its people and take your expenses. And what we mentioned in this chapter is of the same kind as what we mentioned in the first chapter; because when the sharecropping became corrupt by what corrupted it, the landowner's permission to the cultivator for what he sowed in it became as no permission, and its ruling returned to the ruling of one who sowed it without its owner's order. So the ruling in that is like the ruling brought by the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. And similar to that whose ruling is referred to the ruling of what is in these two hadiths we mentioned in these two chapters: a man plants in another man's land without his order, or plants in it by his order upon a corrupt transaction, young palm shoots that become palms; they belong to the owner of the land, not the planter; because there occurred in it increase caused by the land that cannot be separated from the shoot planted in it. So all that belongs to the owner of the land, and upon the owner of the land for his planter is what he spent on it. And we ask Allah for success."
"3059","Chapter 426: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about share-irrigation of palms for a portion of their fruit, and about dealing for land for a portion of what comes out of it. 2673 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with the people of Khaybar for half of what comes out of the crops."
"3060","2674 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usama ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: When Khaybar was conquered, the Jews asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to keep them in it upon working for half of what comes out of it of fruits and crops. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I keep you in it upon that as long as we wish. So they remained in it thus in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, and part of Umar's rule. The fruits were divided upon the shares of half of Khaybar, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take the fifth."
"3061","2675 - And Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Awn al-Ziyadi narrated to us, they said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, granted Khaybar as booty; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept them as they were and made it between him and them. Then he sent Ibn Rawaha, who estimated it upon them."
"3062","2676 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Abu al-Qasim, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khaybar for half, then sent Ibn Rawaha who divided with them. Abu Jafar said: In these reports we narrated is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting share-irrigation in palms for a portion of their fruit and dealing in land for a portion of the crops the worker sows in it. Then an objector said: How may you attribute this to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about dealing in land as you mentioned, while you narrate from him the prohibition of sharecropping in land, and the prohibition of muhaqala, which is exactly this?"
"3063","2677 - And he mentioned what Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym and al-Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, (Ha). And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Saeed ibn Mansur narrated to us. Then they all gathered and said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Saeed ibn al-Musayyib, from Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabana and muhaqala, and said: Only three cultivate: a man who owns land and cultivates it, a man whose brother granted him land and cultivates what was granted to him, and a man who hires for gold or silver."
"3064","2678 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Yala ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever owns land, let him cultivate it or let his brother cultivate it, and let him not hire it for a third, nor a fourth, nor a named food. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his dealing in Khaybar in its palms and its land continued in his time, in Abu Bakr's time after him, and in what Allah willed of Umar's time after Abu Bakr. That proves the ruling of that dealing in land and that share-irrigation in trees remained, and that no prohibition nor abrogation affected them. Then we sought what was narrated from him, apart from Khaybar, to ascertain his prohibition narrated from him therein and what was its cause."
"3065","2679 - We found Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah informed me that Abd Allah ibn Umar used to hire out his land until it reached him that Rafi ibn Khadij al-Ansari forbade hiring out land. He met him and said: O Ibn Khadij, what do you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about hiring land? He said: I heard my two uncles, who had witnessed Badr, narrating to the household that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hiring land. Abd Allah said: I knew that land was hired in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Abd Allah feared the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had introduced something about that he had not known, so he left hiring land. In this, from Ibn Umar, he knew land was hired in the Prophet's time. This objector said: This does not say it was hired for part of what comes out of it; it may have been hired for dinars or dirhams. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: Ibn Umar by this statement meant only to inform Rafi that he knew land was hired in the Messenger of Allah's time upon the meaning which Rafi's narration declared prohibited. It was also narrated from him what proves the Prophet's prohibition was about hiring land for a third and a fourth, for a reason they introduced into it that corrupted sharecropping upon it."
"3066","2680 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, that Rafi ibn Khadij informed Abd Allah ibn Umar, while he was leaning on my hands, that his uncles came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then returned and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hiring farms. Ibn Umar said: We knew that a farm owner hired it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon having what is on the irrigated spring where water gushes, and a portion of straw, I know not what it is. In this is proof that the dealing was upon part of what comes out of the land, into which entered what corrupts it: the landowner reserving a portion of his land, having what comes out of what the worker sows in it as a dealing, plus a portion of the straw from the wheat coming out of the land. That corrupts sharecropping. So the Prophet's prohibition of sharecropping was for the corruption that entered it, not that it is in itself corrupt when that corruption is removed. The like of that was narrated from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas."
"3067","2681 - As Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us. 2682 - And as Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Salih al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ikrima ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Ibn Labiba, from Saeed ibn al-Musayyib, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: People hired farms for what is upon the water-channel, and what is watered around the well. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that, and said: Hire them for gold and silver. It was also narrated from Jabir ibn Abd Allah that the prohibition of sharecropping was for this reason too."
"3068","2683 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Nafi al-Madani narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that men hired their farms for half of what comes out of them, a third, and for the madhiyanat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about that: Whoever owns land, let him cultivate it; if he does not cultivate it, let him grant it to his brother; if he does not do so, let him keep it."
"3069","2684 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad informed me that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki narrated to him, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we took land for a third or a fourth with madhiyanat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that."
"3070","2685 - And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We did mukhabara in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and gained from such-and-such. He said: Whoever owns land, let him cultivate it or let his brother plow it; if not, let him leave it. The like of that was also narrated from Rafi ibn Khadij."
"3071","2686 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Saeed al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Hanzala ibn Qays al-Zuraqi informed us that he heard Rafi ibn Khadij saying: We were the people of Medina with most fields, and we used to say to the one we sharecropped with: This plot is yours and this plot is ours, cultivate it for us. Sometimes this plot produced and that produced nothing, and sometimes that produced and this produced nothing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us that."
"3072","2687 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi informed us, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Yahya, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us hiring our land, and there was then neither gold nor silver. A man hired his land for what is upon the rabi and the aqbal, and known things. And he drove the hadith."
"3073","2688 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Hujayn ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzala ibn Qays, from Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: An uncle of mine narrated to me: They hired land in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for what grows upon the arbi'a and something of the crop which the landowner excepted. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us that."
"3074","2689 - And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: al-Mughira ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Isa - that is Ibn Yunus - narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzala ibn Qays al-Ansari, he said: I asked Rafi ibn Khadij about hiring land for gold or silver. He said: There is no harm in that. People in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hired for what is upon the madhiyanat and the approaches of the streams; this would be safe and that would perish, or that would perish and this would be safe. People had no hire except this, so he prohibited it for that. As for something known and guaranteed, there is no harm. In what we narrated is proof that the Messenger of Allah's prohibition of sharecropping for a portion of what the land produces was for this corruption they introduced into it, not for anything else contradicting what he did in giving Khaybar's land to the Jews for half of what comes out of it. It was narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did therein was not prohibition nor forbidding, and that it was for something else."
"3075","2690 - And as Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abu Ubayda ibn Muhammad ibn Ammar, from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: May Allah forgive Rafi. By Allah, I knew the hadith better than him. Two men of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having fought each other. He said: If this is your condition, do not hire farms. He heard: Do not hire farms. It was also narrated from Ibn Abbas about that."
"3076","2691 - What Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, (Ha). And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, (Ha). And what al-Rabi also narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr. Then they gathered and said: from Tawus, he said: I said to him: If you left mukhabara, for they claim the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it. He said: The most knowledgeable of them informed me - meaning Ibn Abbas - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid it; he only said: That one of you grants his brother is better for him than taking a known rent upon it. Abu Jafar said: When we stood upon these meanings, it became clear to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid the like of what he did in Khaybar, dealing upon its land for half of what comes out of it, by abrogating that; rather it was for a reason that corrupted the dealing, so his prohibition was for that. What he did in Khaybar remained upon its ruling, nothing abrogated it. Then an objector said: As for share-irrigation in palms for a portion of its fruit, we do not oppose you in that. As for sharecropping in land, we oppose you in that, and hold it is the muhaqala which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade."
"3077","2692 - And there was mentioned in that what Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abi Salama narrated to me, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqala, muzabana, and mukhabara."
"3078","2693 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Sulaym ibn Hayyan, from Saeed ibn Mina, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. This objector said: Muhaqala is hiring land for part of what comes out of it. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: we do not agree that its interpretation is as he interpreted it; for other than what he interpreted was narrated in its interpretation."
"3079","2694 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysara informed me, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mukhabara, muzabana, and muhaqala. Mukhabara is upon a third, a fourth, and half of the white land. Muzabana is selling fresh dates on the palms for dried dates, and selling grapes on the trees for raisins. Muhaqala is selling standing crops upon their roots for food."
"3080","2695 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Saeed al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muhaqala in crops and muzabana in dates. He said: Muhaqala: a man comes to crops while in its heap and says: I buy this heap from you for such-and-such, meaning of wheat. Muzabana: he comes to dates on the palms and says: I take this from you for such-and-such of dates. By this hadith he clarified for us what muhaqala is, and that it is different from hiring land for part of what comes out of it of known portions. As for mukhabara whose prohibition is mentioned in this hadith, upon a third and a fourth of the white land, that is upon what Abu al-Zubayr clarified from him, what they add to it corrupting it. Another objector said: I permit dealing upon land between palms which cannot be benefited from except with work in the palms, and do not permit dealing upon it alone. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: Ibn Umar, one who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his dealing with the Jews in Khaybar's palms and land, was narrated to permit dealing upon land apart from palms as lawful."
"3081","As Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Kulayb ibn Wail, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: I come to a man who owns land and water but owns no seed nor cattle; I plow his land for half, sowing it with my seed and my cattle, sharing half with him? He said: Good. This Ibn Umar permitted dealing upon land alone for half of what comes out, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with the people of Khaybar upon Khaybar's palms and land for a portion of what comes out of them. Others of his companions after him acted by that."
"3082","As Musa ibn al-Hasan al-Saqalli narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, he said: al-Harith ibn Hasira informed us, from Sakhr ibn al-Walid, from Amr ibn Sulay, he said: A man came to Ali ibn Abi Talib and said: So-and-so took land and sowed it. Ali called the man and said: I took it for half from its owner; I hire it and work it, and whatever comes out, half is his and half is mine. He saw no harm in it. Abu Jafar said: This hadith has a good chain; al-Bukhari mentioned that Amr ibn Sulay is Basran from Muharib ibn Khasafa, that he has companionship, Sakhr ibn al-Walid narrated from him, and mentioned that al-Harith ibn Hasira is Azdi. We do not need that in it for its fame and people's acceptance of its narration, except that we cited it to mention his tribe."
"3083","And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father mentioning from Musa ibn Talha, he said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, granted land to a group of the Prophet's companions: Abd Allah ibn Masud, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Sad ibn Malik, and Usama. Two of my neighbors among them, Sad and Ibn Masud, gave their land for a third and a fourth."
"3084","And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, he said: I asked Musa ibn Talha about sharecropping. He said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, granted Abd Allah land, granted Sad land, granted Khabbab land, and granted Suhayb land. Both my neighbors sharecropped for a third and a fourth."
"3085","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Musa ibn Talha, similar, and he added: and Khabbab. In that is what is higher than this: what they were upon in Yemen when Muadh ibn Jabal came to them as governor in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3086","As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, that Muadh came to Yemen while they did mukhabara, and he approved them upon that. And as Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, that when Muadh came to Yemen he hired land, or farms, for a third or a fourth, or he said: he came while they did it, and he let it continue. Abu Jafar said: The successors differ in that like the difference of those after them. As for whoever permits sharecropping land for part of what comes out along with share-irrigation in palms for part of what it produces, he must permit each of them separately, as he permits them together; because dealing occurred in each of them, so each has its ruling. When its ruling together with its companion is permissibility, its ruling separately is likewise. As for the jurists of the cities who permit that: Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. As for Malik, his view was permitting the share-irrigation we mentioned, and nullifying the sharecropping we described. As for Abu Hanifa and Zufar, their view was nullifying both. As for al-Shafii, he permitted them when joined in one land with palms, permitted share-irrigation in palms without land, and did not permit dealing in land for a portion of what comes out of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the model; from him in Khaybar was dealing in land and share-irrigation in palms together. He did not clarify to us that the muhaqala he forbade is of that kind, since Jabir ibn Abd Allah, among those from whom that prohibition was narrated, told us it is selling standing crops for food. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3087","Chapter 427: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what he ordered Ammar, when he asked him about madhi, to wash his private parts and perform ablution from it. 2696 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayya ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Iyas ibn Khalifa, from Rafi ibn Khadij, that Ali ordered Ammar to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about madhi. He said: He washes his private parts and performs ablution. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is his order to wash his private parts. Then an objector said: What is meant by that? Washing the private parts is not ordered for one who urinates, yet the ruling of madhi's exit is referred to the ruling of urine's exit. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: he ordered him that so the madhi contracts and does not exit; because water cuts it off from that, as the Muslims ordered one who drives a sacrificial camel with milk to sprinkle its udder with water so that milk does not flow from it; because water contracts it. Likewise what he ordered in this hadith of washing the private parts is only so the madhi contracts and does not exit, not that it is obligatory like the obligation of prayer ablution upon its exit. The proof of that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, which came from him successively."
"3088","2697 - As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated to us, he said: Ubayda ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Saeed ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was a man who emitted much madhi, so I ordered a man who asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: In it is ablution."
"3089","2698 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Saeed ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: al-Amash informed us, from Mundhir Abu Yala al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya, he said: I heard him saying from his father, he said: I used to find madhi, so I ordered al-Miqdad to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, being shy to ask him because his daughter was with me. He asked him, and he said: Every stallion emits madhi; when it is semen, in it is washing, and when it is madhi, in it is ablution."
"3090","2699 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja al-Ghudani narrated to us, he said: Zaida ibn Qudama narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhi, and the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with me. I sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Perform ablution and wash it."
"3091","2700 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Saeed ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about madhi. He said: In it is ablution, and in semen is washing."
"3092","2701 - And as al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Hani ibn Hani, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhi; when I emitted madhi I washed. I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: In it is ablution."
"3093","2702 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Zaida narrated to us, he said: al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi al-Fazari narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Qabisa, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhi. I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: When you see madhi, perform ablution and wash your male organ; and when you see fluid, wash."
"3094","2703 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ayyash ibn Anas. And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutayba ibn Saeed informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Aish ibn Anas, he said: I heard Ali upon the pulpit saying: I was a man who emitted much madhi; I wanted to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but was shy of him because his daughter was under me. I ordered Ammar, and he asked him. He said: In it is ablution. Sahl ibn Hunayf also narrated from him in this meaning the same."
"3095","2704 - As Nasr ibn Marzuq and Sulayman ibn Shuayb both narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Saeed ibn Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from his father, from Sahl ibn Hunayf, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about madhi. He said: In it is ablution. Abu Jafar said: In these reports we narrated is the Messenger of Allah's information that the obligation upon madhi's exit is ablution. In that is negation that any other obligation is in it. When ablution is the obligation in it and nothing else, what he ordered in it besides ablution is not for obligation, but for something else with no aspect except what we mentioned in it. Allah knows best, and Him we ask for success."
"3096","Chapter 428: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: Any slave who marries without his masters' permission is a fornicator. 2705 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Salih, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Any slave who marries without his masters' or his family's permission is a fornicator."
"3097","2706 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil saying: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same."
"3098","2707 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"3099","2708 - And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Makki, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Any slave who marries, or he said: contracts marriage, without his masters' permission is a fornicator."
"3100","2709 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Wahid informed us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to me that Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same."
"3101","2710 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Mindal narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any slave who marries without his master's permission is an adulterer. Then an objector said: What is the meaning of applying adultery or fornication in these reports to a slave marrying without his master's permission, when there is no mention of consummation by him with whom he married thus, and there is no disagreement among you that when he marries thus and consummates he is not subjected to the hadd, and in that is negation that his contracting that marriage upon himself is as in this hadith of what was applied to him thereby. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: he applied to him what he applied in these reports for the marriage that is a cause of the consummation by which he becomes as was applied in these reports. Its cause was named by its name, as was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in naming the things by which adultery is reached, adultery which is the name of the reality of what occurs by them."
"3102","2711 - As Abu Umayya, Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud, and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdala narrated to us, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two eyes commit adultery, the two hands commit adultery, the two feet commit adultery, and the private part commits adultery."
"3103","2712 - And as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from Abu Rafi, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"3104","2713 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah wrote upon every limb its share of adultery. The eye commits adultery, and its adultery is looking. The tongue commits adultery, and its adultery is speech. The two hands commit adultery, and their adultery is grasping. The foot commits adultery, and its adultery is walking. The ear commits adultery, and its adultery is listening. And the private part confirms that or denies it."
"3105","2714 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar informed us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two eyes commit adultery, the tongue commits adultery, the two hands commit adultery, the two feet commit adultery, and the private part confirms that or denies it."
"3106","2715 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. In these reports we narrated is the Messenger of Allah's applying adultery to these limbs, since they are among its causes, and since it is reached only by them. There was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what enters this meaning."
"3107","2716 - What Ali ibn Mabad and Abu Umayya narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, he said: Thabit ibn Umara narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghunaym ibn Qays saying: I heard Abu Musa al-Ashari narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Any woman who perfumes herself and passes by people so they smell her fragrance is an adulteress, and every eye is adulterous. Likewise what we narrated from him about his applying to the slave marrying without his masters' permission what he applied in these reports we began mentioning in this chapter; because it is a cause of what deserves that name. He is not subjected to hadd in that by scholars' agreement, for he is not bounded therein; due to the doubt entering it from the preceding marriage, of the waiting period becoming obligatory by it, and of establishing lineage of a child, if any from him. Not every fornicator is subjected to hadd, just as not every thief is cut. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3108","Chapter 429: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what he ordered Hamna bint Jahsh in the prolonged bleeding she had. 2717 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talha, from his uncle Imran ibn Talha, from his mother Hamna bint Jahsh, she said: I had prolonged heavy severe bleeding; I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his verdict and informing him. I found him in the house of my sister Zaynab bint Jahsh. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have prolonged heavy bleeding; what do you see in it? It has prevented me from prayer and fasting? He said: I describe cotton for you; it removes blood. She said: It is more than that. He said: Then bridle yourself. She said: It is more than that. He said: Then take a cloth. She said: It is more than that; it only gushes gushing. He said: I shall order you two matters; whichever you do suffices you for the other, and if you are able upon both then you know best. It is only a kick of Satan's kicks. Consider yourself menstruating six or seven days in Allah's knowledge until, when you see you are pure and clean, pray twenty-three or twenty-four nights and their days, and fast; that suffices you. Do so every month as women menstruate and purify according to their menstruation and purity times. If you are able to delay noon and hasten afternoon, wash, then join noon and afternoon; delay sunset and hasten night prayer, then wash and join the two prayers, do so; and wash at dawn, then pray and fast if you can upon that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the more pleasing of the two matters to me."
"3109","2718 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talha, from his uncle, from his mother Hamna bint Jahsh, that she had prolonged bleeding in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I had severe extraordinary prolonged bleeding. He said: Stuff cotton. She said: It is worse than that; I gush it gushing. He said: Bridle yourself, and consider yourself menstruating each month in Allah's knowledge six or seven days, then wash fully, pray and fast twenty-three or twenty-four; or delay noon and advance afternoon and wash once for both, delay sunset and advance night prayer and wash once for both."
"3110","2719 - And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: I read upon Sharik ibn Abd Allah, then he mentioned this hadith. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's order to Hamna to consider herself menstruating in Allah's knowledge six or seven days, then pray and fast twenty-three or twenty-four nights and their days. Then an objector said: How may you accept upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of this, his ordering Hamna to leave prayer and fasting a day in which fasting and prayer may be obligatory upon her? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: what he supposed of what this woman was ordered in this hadith is not as he supposed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not order her what he imagined he ordered her, returning the choice to her to menstruate six or seven. Rather he ordered her to menstruate in Allah's knowledge what her stronger supposition is that she is menstruating therein, by her investigating that, as he ordered one whom doubt enters his prayer, not knowing whether he prayed three or four, to investigate the stronger in his heart and act upon it. Likewise his order to this woman in her menstruation. That from him is only after she informed him that knowledge of her days she menstruates, which days of each month they are, had left her, so he ordered her to investigate them, as he ordered the praying one in his prayer at his doubt how much he prayed, to act upon what his investigation leads to. What is in this hadith of six or seven is only doubt entering one of its narrators, who said that upon doubt. As for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he ordered her only six days or seven days, not with choice in that for either number; rather because her days were, Allah knows best, one of the two numbers, and their place in each month had left her, and she informed him of that, so he ordered her what he ordered therein. As for what is in this hadith of his saying to her: If you can delay noon and hasten afternoon, wash and join noon and afternoon, until he mentioned with that what he mentioned, its aspect with us, Allah knows best, is upon permission for her to join the two prayers as mentioned; because no prayer time comes upon her except it may be that she is menstruating with no prayer upon her, or pure from menstruation with washing obligatory upon her, or in prolonged bleeding with ablution obligatory upon her. What was upon her in that is to wash for every prayer time until she prays the prayer she washes for upon knowledge she is pure, a purity with which that prayer suffices her. When she was unable and weak for that, he made for her to join noon and afternoon with one washing, and sunset and night prayer with one washing, by delaying the first to the time of the latter, washing then, praying the first at the time of the latter, praying the latter in its time, and washing once for dawn and praying it while pure by that washing. This is the best what that woman can do in her prayers. This hadith is among the best reports in this kind. And we ask Allah for success. If an objector says: Why was she ordered to pray the two prayers at the time of the latter, not at the time of the first? It is said to him: For two reasons; one, had she prayed them at the first's time she would have prayed the latter before its time entered. The other, when the latter's time enters, washing becomes obligatory upon her, so she is pure by it to the end of that time; when she prays both in it she prays them while pure. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3111","Chapter 430: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) showing the measure of the least menstruation, how much it is. 2720 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Umm Salama, that a woman bled profusely in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umm Salama sought a verdict for her from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Let her consider the number of nights and days she used to menstruate of the month before what befell her befell her; then let her leave prayer, then wash, wrap with a cloth, then pray."
"3112","2721 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"3113","2722 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad known as al-Daif narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Umm Salama, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"3114","2723 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii, he said: Sufyan said, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Umm Salama, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, except he said: She leaves prayer the number of nights and days she used to menstruate, or the days of her periods - the doubt is from Ayyub - I know not whether he said this or said that. In this hadith, from the Messenger of Allah's saying: Let her consider the number of nights and days she used to menstruate of the month before what befell her, then leave prayer, then wash, is proof that menstruation is nights and days. In that is proof for the view of whoever said it is three days, no less. Among those holding that among the people of knowledge is Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions."
"3115","2724 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi narrating from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Umm Salama asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh, who bled profusely. He ordered her to leave prayer her periods and their measure of the month, then wash, wrap with a cloth, then pray. In this hadith there was no mention of days nor nights. Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, Ayyub, and Malik agreed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in this hadith the statement requiring menstruation to be nights and days. In that is negation that it is less than three days. Then an objector said: This hadith's chain is corrupt, from Nafi's path from Sulayman ibn Yasar, and from al-Zuhri's path from Sulayman ibn Yasar."
"3116","2725 - And he mentioned in that what Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn al-Furat narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, he said: Yahya ibn Saeed said: Nafi informed me that Sulayman ibn Yasar informed him from a man who informed him, from Umm Salama, then he mentioned the same as Malik's hadith from Nafi from Sulayman, or with the Messenger of Allah's words therein."
"3117","2726 - And what Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni Abu Qurra narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that a man of the Ansar informed him, from Umm Salama, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith. Our answer to him in that is that this hadith's chain was affected by what he mentioned, but we found from Abd Allah ibn Dinar's hadith, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in another hadith, what proves this meaning about the least menstruation."
"3118","2727 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih, al-Layth's scribe, narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar."
"3119","And Yazid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: O assembly of women, give charity and increase seeking forgiveness, for I saw you most of the Fire's people. A firm woman among them said: Why are we, O Messenger of Allah, most of the Fire's people? He said: You increase cursing and deny the husband. I never saw any deficient in intellect and religion more overpowering to a sensible man than you. She said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the deficiency of intellect and religion? He said: As for deficiency of intellect, the testimony of two women equals the testimony of one man; this is of deficiency of intellect. And she stays nights not praying, and breaks fast in Ramadan; this is deficiency of religion. We found in Abu Hurayra's hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) this meaning like this hadith of Ibn Umar."
"3120","2728 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed, admonished, then said: O assembly of women, give charity, for I saw you most of the Fire's people. A woman said to him: Why that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: For your much cursing and denying the husband. I never saw any deficient in intellect and religion more overpowering to the minds of men of opinion than you. A woman said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the deficiency of our intellects and religion? He said: The testimony of two women among you equals the testimony of a man, and the deficiency of your religion: menstruation, one of you stays three and four not praying. He said: We know nothing narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the measure of the least menstruation except what we mentioned. This proves its measure, that it is days and nights, necessitating holding by it and leaving its opposite. And we ask Allah for success."
"3121","Chapter 431: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about black blood and blood not like that, whether they prove the reality of menstruation or of prolonged bleeding or not. 2729 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa, from Aisha, that Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh had prolonged bleeding. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Menstrual blood is black and recognized; when it is that, refrain from prayer, and when it is the other, perform ablution and pray. Abu Jafar said: In this is the Messenger of Allah's order to Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh to consider her blood, to know by its blackness it is menstrual blood, and to know by seeing it otherwise it is prolonged-bleeding blood. Except we examined this hadith's chain and found no one narrating it from Aisha except Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna. Ahmad ibn Shuayb mentioned to us that it was objected upon him when he narrated it thus; it was said to him: Ahmad ibn Hanbal had narrated it from Muhammad ibn Abi Adi, stopping it upon Urwa, not going beyond to Aisha. He said: I only heard it from Ibn Abi Adi from memory. That proved he was not strong in it. It strengthened in hearts that its reality from Ibn Abi Adi is as Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated, not as he narrated. Then we sought it from other than this narration returning to al-Zuhri."
"3122","2730 - We found Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Suhayl - meaning Ibn Abi Salih - from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Asma bint Umays, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh had prolonged bleeding for such-and-such, and did not pray. He said: Glory be to Allah, this is from Satan. Let her sit upon a tub; if she sees yellowness above water, let her wash once for noon and afternoon, then wash once for sunset and night prayer, and perform ablution between that. In this hadith is mention of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Fatima, and in it is no order to consider blood color. Then we sought this hadith from other than al-Zuhri's narration."
"3123","2731 - We found Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have prolonged bleeding; blood does not cease from me. He ordered her to leave prayer the days of her periods, then wash, perform ablution for every prayer and pray, even if blood drips upon the mat drop by drop."
"3124","2732 - And we found Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us. And we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifa narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, that Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I menstruate a month and two months. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is not menstruation; it is only a vein of your blood. When menstruation approaches, leave prayer; when it turns away, wash for your purity, then perform ablution for every prayer."
"3125","2733 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, from Hammad - meaning Ibn Zayd - from Hisham, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her: Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh had prolonged bleeding; she asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have prolonged bleeding and am never pure; shall I leave prayer? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is only a vein, not menstruation. When menstruation approaches, leave prayer; when it turns away, wash from you the trace of blood and perform ablution, for it is only a vein, not menstruation. It was said to him: And washing? He said: Does anyone doubt that?"
"3126","2734 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, except he said: When its measure goes, wash from you the blood, perform ablution and pray."
"3127","2735 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr, Saeed ibn Abd al-Rahman, Malik, and al-Layth narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwa, that he informed them, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, meaning that Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she had prolonged bleeding. She said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I am never pure; shall I ever leave prayer? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is only a vein, not menstruation. When menstruation approaches, leave prayer; when its measure goes, wash from you the blood, then pray. In what we mentioned from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, in Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh's matter, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to leave prayer only in the days of menstruation itself. That proves she knew her days without his order to judge them by blood color. We found from her herself, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his order to her, what agrees with this meaning and contradicts what is in Ibn Abi Adi's hadith."
"3128","2736 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. 2737 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us. Shuayb said: al-Layth narrated to us, and Abu al-Aswad said: al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah, from al-Mundhir ibn al-Mughira, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, that Fatima bint Abi Hubaysh narrated to him that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of blood. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: It is only a vein. Look: when your period comes to you, do not pray; when the period passes, purify yourself, then pray from period to period. That too agreed with what Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his order to her, and contradicted what is in Ibn Abi Adi's hadith. Then we considered what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his order to other prolonged-bleeding women, whether in it is anything of considering blood color or not."
"3129","2738 - We found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that Umm Habiba bint Jahsh had prolonged bleeding; she asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: It is only a vein, not menstruation. She washed and prayed."
"3130","2739 - And al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: al-Numan, al-Awzai, and Abu Mabad Hafs ibn Ghaylan narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, he said: Urwa and Amra narrated to me, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Umm Habiba bint Jahsh had prolonged bleeding; she sought a verdict from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said to her: This is not menstruation, but it is a vein which Satan tore. When menstruation turns away, wash and pray; when menstruation turns away, wash and pray; when it turns away, leave prayer for it."
"3131","2740 - And we found Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: Urwa and Amra bint Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Umm Habiba had prolonged bleeding, then he mentioned the same, except he did not say in it: which Satan tore."
"3132","2741 - And we found al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa and Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that Umm Habiba bint Jahsh had prolonged bleeding for seven years. She asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered her to wash, and said: This is a vein, not menstruation."
"3133","2742 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, the same. These reports too were free of considering blood color in this story. We found reason proves no meaning to considering blood color; for we saw impurities of stool and urine, their colors are not considered; rulings are for them in themselves, not their colors. We found period blood, and found the people of knowledge upon two views in it: some say it is not an impurity - the view of Medina's people - and some hold it is an impurity - the view of Kufa's people. None of them considered its color; the ruling with them is for itself. Likewise in reason is menstrual blood: its ruling is its own ruling, not its color's ruling. And we ask Allah for success."
"3134","Chapter 432: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his distinguishing between freeing a person and releasing a neck. 2743 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, author of al-Tayalisa, narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: I asked Talha al-Iyami, and he narrated to me from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsaja, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Teach me a deed that admits me to Paradise. He said: Though you shortened the speech, you posed a broad question. Free the person, and release the neck. He said: Are they not one? He said: No; freeing the person is to single-handedly free it, and releasing the neck is to help in its price. The productive loaned milch-camel, and bestowing upon the unjust kin. If you cannot do that, feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, command good and forbid evil. If you cannot do that, restrain your tongue except from good."
"3135","2744 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Talha al-Iyami narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsaja, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, except he said: and bestowing upon the unjust kin. Abu Jafar said: We reflected upon what is in this hadith of freeing the neck; we found it what people know of what Allah obligated them of freeing necks in expiation of mistaken killing, in zihar, in oath expiations, and in the like of vows they vow and obligations they obligate, and the like of what they volunteer of that kind. We reflected upon his saying: and releasing the neck; we found that upon releasing it from what it is captive by, of debt in which it is held, and of other claims by which it is sought, until released from that by freeing it from it and removing it from it. From that is said releasing a pledge, freeing it from its pledgee's hand by paying what it is pledged for. From that is the Prophet's saying which we narrated earlier in this book of ours at his sleep: release my pledge, freeing me from what I am claimed by. From that too is the captive about whom was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was narrated; he is the prisoner."
"3136","2745 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abd Allah ibn Judan maintained kinship, honored the guest, released the captive, and I praised him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He never said one day: Forgive me my sin on the Day of Judgment."
"3137","2746 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Umara ibn Abi Hafs narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about my cousin Ibn Judan. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What was he? I said: He slaughtered fat camels, milked upon water, honored the neighbor, honored the guest, maintained kinship, spoke truthfully, fulfilled protection, released the captive, fed food, and rendered trusts. He said: Did he say one day: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from Hellfire? I said: No; he did not know what Hell is. He said: Then no."
"3138","2747 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Feed the hungry, visit the sick, and release the captive. He said: Sufyan: The captive is the prisoner. He said: What we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports about the captive proved that the release he meant in the first hadith we narrated in this chapter, of what he informed is different from freeing the person, is freeing from captivity, and from the debt by which he is claimed, of contracted slaves and others, until they return free of that, freed from it, not claimed by it. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3139","Chapter 433: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: The maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir. 2748 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Zakariyya ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Maysara al-Makki Abu Yahya and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysara al-Uqayli, from Ali ibn Abi Talha, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Abu Amir al-Hawzani, from al-Miqdam al-Kindi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am closer to every believer than himself. Whoever leaves dependents or a lost property, to me. Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs. I am the patron of one who has no patron; I inherit his wealth and release his captive. The maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir; he inherits his wealth and releases his captive. He said: This hadith was used by whoever held to giving inheritance to kin, following those companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who held it: Umar ibn al-Khattab, Ali ibn Abi Talib, and Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them. Those holding that, arguing by this hadith and following those companions we mentioned, were opposed by saying: The uncle the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in this hadith is only the one who joins with maternal-unclehood to the deceased agnateship from his fathers' side. And he mentioned in that."
"3140","2749 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us. And what Ibn Abi Mughira narrated to us, he said: Badal ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, they said: Shuba narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysara, from Ali ibn Abi Talha, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Abu Amir, from al-Miqdam al-Kindi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leaves dependents, to us, or to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs. I am heir of one who has no heir; I inherit his wealth and pay blood-money for him. The maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir; he inherits his wealth and pays blood-money for him. This opponent said: That uncle whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended is the uncle who pays blood-money, who is of maternal uncles an agnate, apart from other maternal uncles who do not pay blood-money because they are not agnates. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: what was mentioned of that is not as mentioned. This hadith's reality is upon what Hammad ibn Zayd narrated it, not upon what Shuba narrated it. Shuba was affected in that because he narrated from memory, not returning to his book, narrating the meanings of what he heard, not the words he heard from his narrator, since he was incapable of that. He was not a jurist to refer that to jurisprudence until its meanings distinguish in his heart, like Malik and al-Thawri. Proof of the corruption of what he narrated this hadith upon, and that the better is what Hammad ibn Zayd narrated it upon: in both their hadiths the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir. That proves he meant only the uncle who does not inherit with another heir of kin. We found the people of knowledge all agreeing about one who is an agnate, whether uncle or not, inheriting with named-share kin of kin: he inherits with the mother what remains after her share, a third or a sixth; inherits with one daughter and with more than one daughters what remains after their shares, a half for one and two-thirds for more, meaning the shares of daughters inheriting him; inherits with one sister, whether full or paternal, and with more sisters of their kind, what remains after their inheritances from him. That proves the uncle the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant is the uncle who is not an agnate, with his clarifying that to us by his saying: The maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir. He clarified thereby he meant of maternal uncles whoever does not inherit with named-share kin we mentioned, who is not an agnate of uncles. Then we found other than Hammad ibn Zayd and Shuba narrating this hadith like Hammad ibn Zayd, not like Shuba."
"3141","2750 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Rashid ibn Sad narrated to me that he heard al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Allah and His Messenger are patron of one who has no patron; he inherits his wealth and releases from him. The maternal uncle is heir of one who has no heir; he inherits his wealth and releases from him."
"3142","2751 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman and Abu Zura Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, the wording is Fahd's, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the same. This hadith Muawiya ibn Salih narrated from Rashid ibn Sad; he is the one from whom Budayl ibn Maysara, from whom Shuba and Hammad ibn Zayd took this hadith, narrated. When they differed upon him therein, they counterbalanced in that, rising, and more deserving of the hadith than them is whoever narrated it besides them without dispute. If an objector says: Muawiya ibn Salih did not mention in this hadith between Rashid ibn Sad and al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib Abu Amir al-Hawzani. It is said to him: It is not denied of Rashid ibn Sad to have heard al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib; for he heard from those in his days among the Prophet's companions; he heard from Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan. Hadith scholars differ in hadith chains, some adding upon others a man and more in number. Muawiya ibn Salih's case therein must be carried upon what they carried it upon. What we understand thereafter is that it is impossible with us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended an uncle who is an agnate, mentioning him for inheritance by unclehood, leaving mentioning him for inheritance by agnateship; for agnateship is stronger in inheritance than a non-agnate uncle; and because a non-agnate uncle inherits only where there is no agnate and no named-share kin. It is impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his mention his weaker state, leaving his mention by his stronger state. What besides what is needed in giving kin inheritance by kinship is not this place to detail, bringing more than we brought; for we brought therein only the explanation of the difficulty narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, not else. As for what is needed therein besides what we mentioned in this chapter, we brought it in our book on Quran rulings and in Sharh al-Athar. That sufficed from repeating it here. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3143","Chapter 434: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever is assigned to a solvent one, let him follow. 2752 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Delaying by the rich is injustice, and whoever is assigned to a solvent one, let him follow."
"3144","2753 - And Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is assigned to a solvent one, let him follow."
"3145","2754 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Yunus, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Delaying by the rich is injustice, and if you are transferred to a solvent one, follow."
"3146","2755 - And Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd informed us, he said: Nafi informed us, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you are transferred to a solvent one, follow him. We reflected upon what was narrated in this chapter from Abu Hurayra's hadith with which we began: Whoever is assigned to a solvent one, let him follow. The intended following puzzled us, what it is. What is in Ibn Umar's hadith with which we seconded clarified it: When you are transferred to a solvent one, follow him. We understood thereby he meant by that following transfer of what he has of debt upon whoever is assigned of the rich. Except we found Yahya ibn Main spoke about this hadith of Ibn Umar, mentioning Yunus ibn Ubayd did not hear from Nafi. As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main said to me about Yunus ibn Ubayd's hadith from Nafi from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Delaying by the rich is injustice. Yahya said: I heard it from Hushaym, but Yunus did not hear it from Nafi. Ibn Abi Dawud said to us: I said to Yahya: Did Yunus hear nothing from Nafi? He said: Yes, but this hadith in particular Yunus did not hear from Nafi. He said: We reflected upon what Yahya said in that; we found it an answer to what Ibn Abi Dawud asked him about, of: Delaying by the rich is injustice. Yahya answered him about it with what he answered. Then we found in Mualla's, the ultimate in firmness, from Hushaym in this hadith, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd informed us, he said: Nafi narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as we mentioned from Abu Umayya in this chapter. We understood thereby that what Yahya meant of denying Yunus's hearing it from Nafi is: Delaying by the rich is injustice, not what is in it besides that of his saying: When you are transferred to a solvent one, follow him. Allah knows best the reality in that. Then we sought what is in this hadith of jurisprudence; we found the people of knowledge all holding about transfer that it is shifting what the transferee had upon the transferor to the transferred-upon; they do not differ in that, except Zufar and al-Qasim ibn Man, who said: Transfer is like surety, guarantee, and sponsorship, and the transferee may claim each of his transferor and the transferred-upon for what he has. The Prophet's saying: Whoever is transferred to a solvent one, let him follow, refuted that to him; and because it exists in language from people's saying: I have such upon so-and-so, and so-and-so is surety for me in it, or guarantor, or sponsor, so therein is mention that the thing he has upon the one originally liable remains as it was before guarantee, sponsorship, and surety. We did not find them saying: I have such upon so-and-so, and so-and-so is transferee for me in it, nor: I have such upon so-and-so, and he transferred me in it upon so-and-so. Rather they say: I had such upon so-and-so, and he transferred me in it upon so-and-so. That proves transfer with it shifts wealth from whoever was liable to whoever was assigned. Surety, sponsorship, and guarantee are otherwise. Then we found the people of knowledge differing in this transfer by what it occurs. One group says: it is by transfer upon whoever is assigned, whether such wealth was upon him or not. Among those saying that: Abu Hanifa and his companions and al-Shafii. One group says: transfer occurs only with a like debt of the transferor upon the transferred-upon. Among those saying that: Malik ibn Anas. We found no distinction in the Prophet's hadith between transfer with wealth of the transferor upon the transferred-upon like it, and transfer with nothing of the transferor upon the transferred-upon. It was not permitted to distinguish between what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined except by his distinction. Then we found them differing in transfer upon one whose poverty the transferee does not know, assigned upon him as solvent. One group says: he may return with his wealth upon the transferor, nullifying the transfer; among them Malik. The other group says: he may not break the transfer; transfer remains as it is. Among those saying that Abu Hanifa, except Abu Yusuf and Muhammad said: When the judge decrees his bankruptcy, the transferee returns with wealth upon the transferor. What Malik, may Allah have mercy on him, said therein is better than what Abu Hanifa and al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on them, said. What Abu Yusuf and Muhammad said therein is close to what Malik said. Then we found them differing in the loss of wealth upon the transferred-upon by his death destitute. One group says: the transferee returns with what he has upon the transferor; among those saying that Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions. One group says: the transferee does not return upon the transferor; the loss is from his wealth ever. Among saying that Malik and al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on them. We reflected upon that to know the view therein; we found transfer therein compensates the transferee from the transferor's liability the transferred-upon's liability. That became like selling a liability for a liability. Like that is compensating the one liable of his wealth liable upon him, a slave he sells him for it; his wealth shifted from the liability of whoever was liable to the sold slave, becoming in it. Then we found the slave dying thereafter; his death is from his seller's wealth, and the wealth he had upon whoever was liable returns. Likewise is the loss of the transferred-upon's liability; by that the wealth in it returns to the liability given in exchange. If an objector says: Malik's view, may Allah have mercy on him, about a sold slave dying in his seller's hand is that he dies from his buyer's wealth, though he did not take possession. It is said to him: Of his view about food sold by measure, when lost in his seller's hand, is that it is lost from his wealth, not his buyer's wealth. There is no difference in analogy between this and before it. In what we mentioned is proof of what we described. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3147","Chapter 435: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his order to expel Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula. Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurra ibn Abi Khalifa narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salama al-Azdi informed us, he said: 2756 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: If I live, I shall expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula, leaving only a Muslim in it."
"3148","2757 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"3149","2758 - And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"3150","2759 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Shihab ibn Abbad al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Saeed ibn Samura narrated to me, from Samura, from Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The last the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke is that he said: Expel the Jews of Hijaz and the people of Najran from the Arabian Peninsula."
"3151","2760 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maymun narrated to me, he said: Sad ibn Samura ibn Jundub narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The last the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke, then he mentioned the same."
"3152","2761 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Maymun, freedman of Samura, narrated to us, from Sad ibn Samura, from his father, from Abu Ubayda, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Expel the Jews of Hijaz. Fahd said to us: al-Ramadi - meaning Ibrahim ibn Bashshar - said: Ibn Uyayna narrated from this shaykh only this hadith."
"3153","2762 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Sad ibn Samura, from Samura ibn Jundub, from Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The last the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke: Expel the Jews of Hijaz from the Arabs' city, and know that among the worst Arabs are those who take graves as mosques."
"3154","2763 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, without mentioning Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: If I live, I shall expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula until only a Muslim remains in it. And Umar said: If I live, I shall expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula until only a Muslim remains in it. Then we returned to Abu Ubayda's hadith; we found in its chain something its narrators differed in: Ibn Samura. Muhammad ibn Bishr said: Saeed ibn Samura, while Yahya al-Qattan, Ibn Uyayna, and Abu Ahmad said: Sad ibn Samura. Three are more deserving of memory than one. We reflected upon this hadith; we needed knowledge of what the Arabian Peninsula is."
"3155","We found Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, in what Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Razi related to us, from Musa ibn Nasr, from Hisham ibn Ubayd Allah, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said: As for the Arabs' land, meaning in which Jews and Christians are not left residing except as long as they fulfill needs of selling their trade they brought, like Mecca, Medina, Taif, al-Rabadha, and Wadi al-Qura; all this is of the Arabs' land."
"3156","Hisham said: I read upon Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two religions shall not gather in the Arabian Peninsula. Ibn Shihab said: Umar ibn al-Khattab investigated that until certainty came to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two religions shall not gather in the Arabian Peninsula. So he expelled the Jews of Najran and Fadak. We found Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us from Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam that he said: In the Prophet's hadith that he ordered expelling Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula, he said: Abu Ubayda said: The Arabian Peninsula is between Hafar Abi Musa to the farthest Yemen in length; as for width, between the sands of Yabrin to the end of al-Samawa. He said: al-Asmai said: The Arabian Peninsula is from the farthest Aden Abyan to Iraq's countryside in length; as for width, from Jeddah and its coast to Syria's edges. Abu Ubayd said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered expelling them from all this. They hold that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, deemed lawful expelling Najran's people from Yemen, Christians, to Iraq's Sawad, for this hadith; likewise his expelling Khaybar's people to Syria, Jews. We reflected upon expelling Jews from this peninsula we mentioned; we found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expelling some of them, Banu al-Nadir, in his life."
"3157","2764 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shuba, from Abu Bishr, from Saeed ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying: There is no compulsion in religion. He said: An Ansari woman hardly had a child live; she vowed if a child lived she would make him Jewish. When Banu al-Nadir were expelled, among them were people of Ansar's sons. The Ansar said: O Messenger of Allah, our sons. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: There is no compulsion in religion. Saeed said: Whoever wished joined them, and whoever wished entered Islam. This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expelling of Jews whom he expelled in his life. As for what was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, about those expelled among them in his caliphate."
"3158","2765 - We found Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aisha narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought Khaybar's people until he confined them to their fortress. He overcame land, crops, and palms. They made peace to be expelled from it, having what their mounts carry, and to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the yellow and white and the halqa, that is weapons, leaving it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions had no servants to maintain it, and they were not free to maintain it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them Khaybar upon having half of every crop and palm as long as it seemed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When Umar ibn al-Khattab's time came, may Allah be pleased with him, they cheated Muslims, deceived them, and threw Ibn Umar from atop a house, breaking his hands. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever has a share of Khaybar, let him estimate until he divides it among them. Their chief said: Do not expel us; leave us in it as the Messenger of Allah let us stay. Umar said to their chief: Do you think the Messenger of Allah's saying to you dropped from me: How about you when your mount trots you toward Syria one day, then a day, then a day. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, divided it among those who witnessed Khaybar on Hudaybiya day. This is what was narrated reaching us about the cause by which Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, expelled whom he expelled of Khaybar's Jews."
"3159","2766 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Abi Muslim al-Ahwal, maternal uncle of Ibn Abi Najih, he heard Saeed ibn Jubayr saying: Ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counseled three. He said: Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, honor the delegations as I honored them, and he was silent about the third; I know not whether he said it and I forgot, or was silent deliberately. Abu Jafar said: This hadith contains what contradicts what we narrated before in this chapter of those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered expelled from the Arabian Peninsula; for those ordered expelled therein in what we narrated earlier in this chapter are Jews and Christians, while in this they are polytheists, different from Jews and Christians. Except we fear that came from Ibn Uyayna; for he narrated from memory, so he may have put polytheists in place of Jews and Christians, lacking the jurisprudence to distinguish. Allah knows best the reality in that. Except the group is more deserving of what they memorized than the single one in what he opposed them. What we said therein is proved by what follows."
"3160","2767 - What al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Qabus ibn Abi Zibyan, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two qiblas are not proper in a land, and there is no jizya upon a Muslim. The meaning of his saying: and there is no jizya upon a Muslim, after his saying: Two qiblas are not proper in a land, is that he meant thereby that the Muslim upon whom there is no jizya is the one upon whom jizya was before his Islam, namely Jews and Christians, not Arab polytheists. His mentioning qibla proves he meant whoever follows a religion, not whoever has no religion. Jews and Christians follow what they follow; they are people of qibla. Polytheists follow nothing; they are not people of qibla. In that is another subtle meaning to be noted: what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counseled in Yunus's hadith we narrated from Ibn Abbas was in his death illness, after Allah annihilated polytheism and its people by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by their entering Islam and by killing whoever refused entering Islam, as He, Mighty and Majestic, said: To Him submitted whoever is in heavens and earth, willingly or unwillingly. Those who submitted willingly or unwillingly are those who submitted; others were annihilated by killing. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counseled what he counseled of what we mentioned, there was no one. How may he counsel expelling non-existent ones? His counsel was expelling existing ones: Jews and Christians. And we ask Allah for success."
"3161","Chapter 436: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the distinguished ones among the Messenger of Allah's companions who were given to him. 2768 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, from Fitr ibn Khalifa, from Kathir Abu Ismail, from Abd Allah ibn Mulayl, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There was no prophet except given seven distinguished ones, ministers, and companions. I was given fourteen: Hamza, Jafar, Abu Bakr, Umar, Ali, al-Hasan, al-Husayn, Abd Allah ibn Masud, Salman, Ammar, Hudhayfa, Abu Dharr, al-Miqdad, and Bilal."
"3162","2769 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Kathir, seller of date-stones, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Mulayl saying: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same."
"3163","And Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn al-Walid al-Ataki narrated to us, he said: al-Ashjai narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi Hafsa, from Abd Allah ibn Mulayl, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Every prophet has seven distinguished ones of his nation; our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has fourteen distinguished: among them Abu Bakr and Umar."
"3164","And Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Salim ibn Abi Hafsa, he said: It reached me from Abd Allah ibn Mulayl this hadith; I came asking him about it, and found them at his funeral. A man narrated to me from him, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Every prophet was given seven distinguished ones, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given fourteen distinguished; among them Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith, from Salim ibn Abi Hafsa, he took it from an unnamed man, from Abd Allah ibn Mulayl. It may be that man is Kathir al-Nawwa. If so, Salim's hadith returns to the same chain as Fitr's hadith."
"3165","2770 - And Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sad Abu Ghaylan al-Shaybani narrated to us, he said: Kathir, seller of date-stones, kunya Abu Ismail, narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Umm Tawil al-Thumali narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Mulayl al-Bajali, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said while upon the pulpit: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every prophet has seven distinguished companions; I have fourteen. Ali said: I, my two sons, Hamza, Jafar, Abu Bakr, Umar, Abu Dharr, al-Miqdad, Salman, Hudhayfa, Ibn Masud, Ammar ibn Yasir, and Bilal. He said: In this hadith Yahya ibn Umm Tawil is inserted between Kathir al-Nawwa and Abd Allah ibn Mulayl. This Yahya ibn Umm Tawil is unknown. Some people mentioned this hadith's chain was corrupted thereby. With us it was not as mentioned; for Fitr ibn Khalifa with hadith scholars is a proof, and Sad Abu Ghaylan is unknown, unfit to oppose Fitr in his narration. So what Sad narrated this hadith with falls, and what Fitr narrated it with stands."
"3166","It was narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in his mentioning the distinguished among the Messenger of Allah's companions, what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shuba, from Abu Ishaq, from Haritha ibn al-Mudarrib, he said: I read Umar's letter, may Allah be pleased with him, to Kufa's people: To proceed, I sent to you Ammar as commander and Abd Allah ibn Masud as minister; they are among the distinguished of Muhammad's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Listen to them and follow them. I preferred you with Abd Allah over myself preferring. Abu Jafar said: An asker asked about the distinguished, who they are. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: they are the elevated by what Allah elevated them with of righteous deeds and praised matters. He said: Are there no distinguished among the Messenger of Allah's companions besides those mentioned in this hadith? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: it may be the distinguished among the Messenger of Allah's companions are more than those mentioned in this hadith; but those mentioned in this hadith of this number were mentioned without denying others of that kind among them. As a man says: I have thousands of dinars and thousands of dirhams of wealth; that does not deny he has more wealth than thousands of dinars and thousands of dirhams. Likewise the Messenger of Allah's mentioning with distinction whom he mentioned of his companions named in this hadith does not deny distinction from others among them. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3167","Chapter 437: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the mosques in which retreat is not permitted except therein. 2771 - Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Jami ibn Abi Rashid, from Abu Wail, he said: Hudhayfa said to Abd Allah: Retreat between your house and Abu Musa's house, you do not change! You knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No retreat except in the three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the Prophet's mosque (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis. Abd Allah said: Perhaps you forgot and they remembered; you erred and they were right. Abu Jafar said: We reflected upon this hadith; we found in it Hudhayfa informing Ibn Masud that he knew what he mentioned to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Masud left denying that upon him, answering him with what he answered therein, from his saying: Perhaps they remembered, abrogating what you mentioned of that, and were right in what they did. The Quran's apparent meaning proves that: His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Do not touch them while you are retreating in the mosques. He generalized all mosques thereby. Muslims were upon it, of retreating in their towns' mosques: either congregational mosques in which Fridays are held, or they and other mosques with imams and muezzins, upon what the people of knowledge said therein. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3168","Chapter 438: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man's happiness with a spacious dwelling, a righteous neighbor, and a comfortable mount. 2772 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr and Fahd ibn Sulayman both narrated to us, they said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Khumayl, from Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of a man's happiness are a spacious dwelling, a righteous neighbor, and a comfortable mount."
"3169","2773 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: Khumayl narrated to me, with me Mujahid, from Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same. Abu Jafar said: We reflected upon this hadith seeking the intended meaning; we found the neighbor ordered to honor his neighbor, as was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that."
"3170","2774 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna informed us, from Amr, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the Last Day, let him honor his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him do good to his neighbor. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him say good or be silent. Sufyan said: Ibn Ajlan added in it, from Saeed al-Maqburi, from Abu Shurayh, he said: His reward is a day and night; hospitality is three. What exceeds that is charity upon the guest. It is not lawful for him to stay with him until he burdens him."
"3171","2775 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, who had companionship, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the same, except he did not mention what Sufyan mentioned therein of Ibn Ajlan's addition."
"3172","2776 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Saeed ibn Abi Saeed, from Abu Shurayh al-Adawi, that he said: My ears heard and my eyes saw when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke, then he mentioned the same, except he did not mention what Ibn Uyayna mentioned of Ibn Ajlan's addition."
"3173","2777 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"3174","2778 - And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: It was read upon Shuayb ibn al-Layth, from al-Layth, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"3175","2779 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Saeed ibn Abi Saeed al-Maqburi, from Abu Shurayh al-Kabi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same, and added about the guest: His reward is a day and night; hospitality is three days. What is after that is charity. It is not lawful for him to stay with him until he burdens him. Malik said: His reward is to present him the best he finds in the day and night. He said: Yathwi means stays with him."
"3176","2780 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar."
"3177","2781 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Farwa ibn Abi al-Maghra narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbor. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him say good or be silent. He said: In what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the neighbor honoring his neighbor is what we mentioned therein, and what was narrated from him about not harming him confirmed that. When that is so of neighbor upon neighbor, fulfilling it to him is happiness for the fulfiller. This is the meaning of what was narrated about the neighbor in this hadith. As for what was narrated of spaciousness of dwelling, let the dwelling's owner thereby praise Allah, Mighty and Majestic, knowing His favors upon him and preferring him over others, being of thanks to Him, Mighty and Majestic, upon what its like is upon in that. As for what is therein of a comfortable mount, that be by lifting occupation from his heart, being in his riding upon one of two aspects: either occupied with remembering his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, or with heart unoccupied by what harms him of his mount. All that is happiness. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"3178","Chapter 439: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about reward for patience upon a bad neighbor. 2782 - Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata informed us, he said: al-Jurayri informed us, from Abu al-Ala, from Ibn al-Ahmas, that he said: It reached me that Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, says: Three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves, and three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, hates. He said: I met him and said: O Abu Dharr, what hadith reached me from you narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I loved to hear it from you. He said: What is it? I said: Three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves, and three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, hates. Abu Dharr said: I said it and heard it. He said: I said: Who are those Allah loves? He said: A man meeting a group or a squad whose companions scattered, meeting them with himself and his chest until killed or Allah, Mighty and Majestic, grants victory. A man with people who prolonged night travel until they liked to touch the earth; they alighted, he withdrew and prayed until he woke his companions for departure. A man with a bad neighbor, patient upon his harm until death or departure separates them. He said: I said: These are those Allah loves; who are those He hates? He said: The swearing merchant, or swearing seller - al-Jurayri doubted - the miserly reminded of favors, and the poor arrogant."
"3179","2783 - al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"3180","2784 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, (Ha). And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, (Ha). And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us. Then they all gathered and said: from Yazid Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, he said: A hadith reached me from Abu Dharr; I loved to meet him and ask him about it. I met him and said to him: O Abu Dharr, a hadith reached me from you; I loved to meet you and ask you about it. He said: You met, so ask. He said: I said: It reached me you say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves, and three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, hates. He said: Yes; I do not think I lie upon my beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying it thrice. I said: Who are the three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves? He said: A man raiding in Allah's path, striving, seeking reward, fighting until killed. You find it in Allah's Book, Mighty and Majestic: Allah loves those fighting in His path in ranks. A man with a neighbor harming him, patient upon his harm, seeking reward, until Allah suffices him of him by death or life. A man with people traveling until drowsiness and sleep burden them; they alight at night's end, and he stands to his ablution and prayer. I said: Who are the three Allah hates? He said: The boastful arrogant. You find it in Allah's Book, Mighty and Majestic: Allah loves not every arrogant boaster. The miserly reminded of favors, and the swearing seller. Abu Jafar said: We reflected upon what is in this hadith of patience upon a bad neighbor; we found of the neighbor's right upon the neighbor honoring him. When he withholds that, mixing it with harming him, and the harmed is patient upon that, seeking reward, he is in the ruling of one overcome upon a right of his, seeking reward. Whoever is thus, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves him; for he is of the obedient, holding to what Allah ordered him by His saying: Those who, when calamity befalls them, say: We belong to Allah and to Him we return. Upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy; they are the guided. And we ask Allah for success."
"3181","Chapter 440: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: Gabriel kept enjoining me about the neighbor until I thought he would make him inherit. 2785 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gabriel, upon him be peace, kept enjoining me about the neighbor until I thought he would make him inherit."
"3182","2786 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Abu Jafar said: Malik and Ibrahim ibn Tahman agreed in this hadith that between Yahya ibn Saeed and Amra in its chain are only they two. Al-Layth ibn Sad and Ali ibn Mushir opposed them therein, inserting in its chain between Yahya ibn Saeed and Amra Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm."
"3183","2787 - As al-Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Saeed narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same."
"3184","2788 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Saeed, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. We found this hadith also narrated from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad: Ibn al-Had."
"3185","2789 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying that. He said: We found Abd Allah ibn Saeed ibn Abi Hind also narrating it from Abu Bakr."
"3186","2790 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Saeed ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amra, she said: Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same. We found Zayd ibn Thabit narrating it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3187","2791 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib, from al-Muttalib, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. We found it narrated from Mujahid too, from a man of the Prophet's companions; there was disagreement from him who he is."
"3188","2792 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Umar al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Bashir ibn Salman narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: We used to come to Abd Allah ibn Amr, who had sheep; he gave us hot milk to drink. One day he gave us cold milk to drink. We said: What is with cold milk? He said: I moved away from the sheep because among them is the dog. His slave was skinning a sheep. He said: O boy, when you finish, begin with our Jewish neighbor. He did that thrice. A man of the people whom Mujahid knew said to him: How much you mention the Jew, may Allah keep you right? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoining about the neighbor until we feared, or thought, he would make him inherit."
"3189","2793 - And as Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Mujahid narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same."
"3190","2794 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Mujahid, he said: Abu Hurayra narrated to me, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Gabriel, peace be upon him, kept enjoining me about the neighbor until I thought he would make him inherit. And it was narrated from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, by another path."
"3191","2795 - As Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababab narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Farahij (ha). And as Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard Dawud ibn Farahij say: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it (i.e. the hadith about Jibril's constant advising regarding the neighbor until he thought he would make him an heir). And this hadith has also been narrated from a man of the Ansar whose name was not mentioned."
"3192","2796 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Hisham, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Abu al-Aliyah, from a man of the Ansar, who said: I left my house intending (to visit) the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there he was standing with a man with him, each of them turned toward his companion. So I thought they had some need (to discuss), and by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept standing so long that I began to feel pity for him because of the length of the standing. When he finished, I said: O Prophet of Allah, the man kept you standing so long that I began to feel pity for you because of the length of the standing. He said: And did you see him? I said: Yes. He said: And do you know who this is? He said: No. He said: That was Jibril (peace be upon him); he kept advising me regarding the neighbor until I thought he would make him an heir. Then he said: Indeed, had you greeted him he would have returned (the greeting) to you. So we examined this hadith to grasp the meaning by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thought that Jibril (peace be upon him) would make the neighbor an heir through him. We found that people in the beginning of Islam used to inherit by adoption: whoever adopted a man, that man inherited him to the exclusion of others, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adopted Zayd ibn Harithah, and as al-Aswad al-Zuhri adopted al-Miqdad ibn Amr, and as Abu Hudhayfah adopted Salim. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, annulled that by His saying: {Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, but he is the Messenger of Allah and the Seal of the Prophets}, and by His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {Call them by (the names of) their fathers; that is more just with Allah, but if you do not know their fathers, then (they are) your brothers in religion and your allies}. And they also used to inherit by alliance (hilf) until Allah annulled that by His saying: {And for all We have appointed heirs for what the parents and near relatives leave, and those with whom your oaths have contracted - so give them their share}. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, thereby turned their affairs away from inheritances toward (other ties of) support, aid, and bequest, and we have mentioned that from Ibn Abbas in what has preceded from us in this book of ours. So it is possible that this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the time when inheritance was by adoption and by what else we mentioned. Since the affair of the neighbor alongside the neighbor had been stressed beyond that, or beyond alliance, or the like of the two, it is not denied that just as inheritance occurred with one of the two, it could occur with what is like them or above them. So what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that was in its proper place. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated that with what He abrogated it. We thus understood that had what came from Jibril (peace be upon him) of that been in the second state (after abrogation), this supposition would not have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it. And Allah we ask for success."
"3193","Chapter 441 - Explanation of the problematic (reports) over which the people of knowledge differed regarding the neighbor: who is he, and what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarifying that. 2797 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni Abd al-Malik ibn Habib, from Talhah ibn Abdillah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have two neighbors, so to which of them should I give a gift? He said: To the one of them whose door is nearer to you."
"3194","2798 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam, meaning Ibn Harb, narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu al-Ala al-Awdi, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When two inviters gather (i.e. invite you at once), then answer the one whose door is nearer, for the nearer of them in door is the nearer of them in neighborhood. And if one of them precedes (the other in inviting), then answer the one who was first."
"3195","2799 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abi Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Imran al-Jawni narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Babnus, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it (the hadith about giving to the nearer neighbor). Abu Ja'far said: So in what we have narrated there is what indicates that neighbors differ in nearness to the one they neighbor and in farness from him, since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described some of them as near to the one to whom they are neighbors, and (implied) that he has among the neighbors those who are farther from him than they are."
"3196","And in that is what negates what some people narrated from Abu Hanifah, which we took from al-Hajjaj ibn Imran by handing over and authorization, from Safwan ibn al-Mughallis, from Abu Sulayman al-Juzajani, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from one of the companions of Abu Hanifah, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him), who said: The neighbors of a man who deserve his bequest to his neighbors are those around his house who, if he sold his house and they owned what they dwell in of that, would deserve it by preemption (shuf'ah). For the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what we narrated from him made some of them nearer to him (the testator) than others of them, while all of them bear the name of neighborhood to him. And what is in this report (of Abu Hanifah) necessitates their equality in neighborhood, whereas what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negates that and necessitates their difference in nearness and farness in neighborhood. And in that is also what negates a position which al-Rabi' permitted for us from al-Shafi'i in his book on bequests: that the farthest neighbor of a man who bequeaths to his neighbors is one between whom and the house he lives in there are forty houses, likewise from every side of it. For that returns to fixing a limit for what has no mention in the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and fixing a limit (tawqit) is binding only by (revealed) fixing (tawqif). And since these two sayings are negated."
"3197","We did not find with the people of knowledge regarding neighborhood, after that, anything except what has been narrated in it from Abu Yusuf and from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah have mercy on them both). For Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf say: Every city whose people neighbor by tribes - then all the people of a tribe are neighbors; and every city whose people neighbor by alleys - then all the people of an alley are neighbors; and every city whose people neighbor by mosques - then all the people of a mosque are neighbors. Since among what we took from al-Hajjaj ibn Imran, from Safwan, from Abu Sulayman, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, and from Muhammad as his own opinion, is the like of this saying also, this saying was the worthiest of the sayings in it. And Allah we ask for success."
"3198","Chapter 442 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the best of neighbors: who is he? 2800 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Shurahbil ibn Sharik al-Ma'afiri narrated to us, that he heard Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli narrate from Abdallah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of companions with Allah is the best of them to his companion, and the best of neighbors with Allah is the best of them to his neighbor."
"3199","2801 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So we examined this hadith to grasp what is meant by it, and we found that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding neighborhood what he commanded, and obligated of the rights of some of its people upon others what he obligated, of what we have mentioned in what preceded from us in these chapters of ours which we narrated regarding neighbors. Since that is so, whoever of them holds fast to what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded him regarding his neighbor is praised with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for what he is upon of that. And when that is so, he is the best of the kind he belongs to - meaning of the neighbors - with Allah, Mighty and Majestic. And Allah we ask for success."
"3200","Chapter 443 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding Surah Sad: is there a prostration in it or not. 2802 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Iyad ibn Abdillah ibn Sa'd, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in (Surah) Sad. So we examined this hadith and found it abridged from a (longer) hadith containing a meaning which does not necessitate what this hadith was abridged to."
"3201","2803 - And it is what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abi Hilal, from Iyad ibn Abdillah ibn Sa'd, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited (Surah) Sad while on the minbar, and when he reached the prostration he descended and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. Then on another day he recited it, and when he reached the prostration they prepared themselves - or a word like it - for prostration, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is only the repentance of a prophet (i.e. Dawud's prostration of repentance), but I saw you preparing yourselves - or you girded yourselves, or a word like it - for prostration. So he descended, and they prostrated. So in this hadith is Abu Sa'id's report that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in it when he recited it at first, then recited it thereafter and the people prepared for prostration in it along with his prostration in it, so he informed them that it is a prostration of thanks from a prophet upon Allah's acceptance of his repentance - meaning it is not among the firm (obligatory) prostrations, and that it is only for a meaning that belonged to that prophet apart from them. And we understood thereby that when there comes to one of them from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, what is of that kind, prostration upon it is permitted for him. And in that is what indicates the permissibility of the prostration of thanks, as Muhammad ibn al-Hasan and al-Shafi'i used to say regarding that. And in that is what indicates that among prostrations are those that are firm (azimah), which one must perform, and that among them are those that are not so. So we sought that: do we find it in anything narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or from one of his companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them."
"3202","So we found that Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zir, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Indeed the firm (binding) prostrations are: {Alif Lam Mim, the revelation (al-Sajdah)}, and {Ha Mim (Fussilat)}, and {By the Star}, and {Read in the name of your Lord (al-Alaq)}."
"3203","And we found that Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And this from Ali - he did not say it by deduction, but he said it from what he knew by what is above deduction. So that indicates: since among prostrations there are firm ones, with them is obligation, and whatever of them has no firmness with it, its reciter and its hearer have the choice between prostrating in it and leaving that. And Abu Hanifah and his companions (may Allah have mercy on them) used to hold that the Qur'anic prostrations, which are the prostration according to them - fourteen prostrations, among them (Surah) Sad - are obligatory. And Malik ibn Anas, in what Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim related from him, used to say regarding the Qur'anic prostrations: they are firm (binding), and they are eleven, among them the prostration of (Surah) Sad. And Abu Hanifah and Malik, both, and their companions, used to count in Surah al-Hajj only one prostration, which is the one at its beginning. And al-Shafi'i, in what al-Muzani related to us from him, used to hold that they are fourteen prostrations besides (Surah) Sad, and he places in al-Hajj two prostrations, one at its beginning and one at its end. And what we have narrated of what is indicated by what Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we mentioned, and of what we narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), which supports that, is worthier than what they all said. So the firm prostrations which Ali mentioned are those which one must perform, and what is besides them of the Qur'anic prostrations is otherwise: whoever hears them or recites them may prostrate in them, and may leave that. And there has also been narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) what enters into this chapter."
"3204","Of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Mujahid, who said: Ibn Abbas was asked about the prostration in (Surah) Sad, and he said: {Those are the ones whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance}."
"3205","And of what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab informed us, from Mujahid, and he mentioned the like of it and added: So he was among those whom your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to follow."
"3206","And of what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn and al-Awwam narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3207","And of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Awwam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he prostrated in (Surah) Sad and said: {Those are the ones whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance}. So the aspect of that according to us - and Allah knows best - is that he be followed in prostrating in the like of what Dawud (peace be upon him) prostrated for, of thanks. And in that is what indicates Ibn Abbas's agreement with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) in what we narrated from him of that. And Allah we ask for success. And it has been narrated from Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) that he prostrated in it also."
"3208","As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, that he saw Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) prostrating in (Surah) Sad."
"3209","And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Marwan al-Uthmani narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: And that according to us is possibly intended as thanks to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for what came from Him to His prophet Dawud (peace be upon him) of accepting his repentance; so its ruling according to him would be that there is no prostration in it except for one who intends prostration in it for this meaning, and its ruling would be different from the ruling of the rest of the Qur'anic prostrations. And it is possible that he prostrated it as one prostrates at his recitation the Qur'anic prostrations besides it, not for this meaning which we began by mentioning of these two possibilities."
"3210","And we found from Abdallah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding it of what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Khusayf narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said to me: Do you prostrate in (Surah) Sad? I said: No. He said: Then prostrate in it, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: {Those are the ones whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance}. And this is among what is possibly intended as following Dawud (peace be upon him) and prostrating in it when Dawud (peace be upon him) prostrated it for the like of it, not because it is prostrated for a particular recitation as the other Qur'anic prostrations besides it are prostrated. And with Allah is success."
"3211","And it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that it is among the Qur'anic prostrations, as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Abu al-Uryan al-Mujashi'i, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he mentioned the Qur'anic prostrations, and mentioned among them (Surah) Sad. Abu Ja'far said: So in this is what indicates that Ibn Abbas made it like the other Qur'anic prostrations, and that it is prostrated for recitation, not for anything besides it as others are prostrated. Then we found from Ibn Abbas also what indicates that it is not among the firm (azaim) of the Qur'an."
"3212","2804 - As Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: (Surah) Sad is not among the firm prostrations of the Qur'an, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray with it and prostrate in it. So that indicates that prostration in it according to him differs from prostration in the other Qur'anic prostrations besides it."
"3213","Chapter 444 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his command to take mosques in the quarters (dur). 2805 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abi Yazid al-Qatrubulli narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command the building of mosques in the quarters (dur) and command their cleaning."
"3214","2806 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from al-Furafisah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So Khalid ibn Abi Yazid and Ya'qub ibn Ishaq differed over Abdallah ibn al-Mubarak regarding who is between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Urwah in the chain of this hadith; each of them said in it what we mentioned from him."
"3215","2807 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr ibn al-Hakam al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Su'ayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that mosques be built in the quarters, and that they be cleaned and perfumed, or as he said. And some of those who hold that when a man builds a mosque in his house and leaves it open between people and it so that they pray in it, it thereby becomes like the rest of the mosques, and his ownership departs from it thereby, argued against one who opposes that and says in it: it is not a mosque, and it does not leave his ownership thereby since it is in a house whose door is locked upon it and which at times comes between people and it - and that is of his rights by right of his ownership of the rest of the house in which he made it; and among those who used to say that were Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) and his companions. So we examined this hadith ourselves: does it indicate anything of what this one arguing with it mentioned in what we mentioned, or not. We found that his command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to take mosques in the quarters possibly intends the places in which the houses are, not the houses upon which doors are locked - so that taking of those mosques would be amidst the quarters in which they are built, and in their courtyards, not inside anything of them upon whose doors their doors are locked. For what the quarters gather of places is called in its entirety quarters (dur), since houses cannot be inhabited without it. Just as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, named the town which He mentioned in His Book that it is the abode of the defiantly disobedient as the abode of the defiantly disobedient, saying, Mighty and Majestic: {I shall show you the abode of the defiantly disobedient}, and in it are roads and what besides them without which towns cannot exist. And the like of that is His saying, Mighty and Majestic, in the threat to the people of His prophet Salih (peace be upon him): {Enjoy yourselves in your abode for three days}, then He said after that: {So the earthquake seized them and they became in their abode fallen prostrate}. And of that is His saying, Mighty and Majestic, in the place in which He mentioned the Blast regarding those He mentioned it regarding: {And the Blast seized those who wronged, so they became in their dwellings fallen prostrate}. So He, Mighty and Majestic, mentioned their places as dwellings (diyar) and mentioned that it is an abode (dar). So that indicates that a town may be called an abode (dar) and may be called quarters (dur). And of that is what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning."
"3216","2808 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi, and al-Layth ibn Abdah narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us. And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, they said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini narrated to me, from Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd, from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best quarters of the Ansar are the quarter of Banu al-Najjar, then the quarter of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, then the quarter of Banu al-Harith, then the quarter of Banu Sa'idah; and in all the quarters of the Ansar there is good."
"3217","2809 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So these quarters were the comprehensive quarters of their people mentioned in these two hadiths; each quarter of them is a quarter for its people, and they are a large number of whom it is known with certainty that a single house like these houses of ours would not contain them, and that what is meant thereby is the district gathering the houses they dwell in. So he mentioned that as the quarter (dar) which gathers courtyards, roads, and what is understood of what is between the houses, each man separately dwelling in a house of them, the whole of which is called a quarter or quarters. Like that is what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded of taking mosques in the quarters: it is possible that what is meant thereby is likewise, and that the mosques he commanded to be taken in them are amidst them, not in their inner rooms. And it is possible that it is inside them, and that those mosques are the ones people take in their homes to pray in, not to admit anyone of the people into them; their ownership is not removed from them according to all the people of knowledge, and the occurrence of the name of mosques upon them neither removes their ownership from them, nor permits others to enter them, nor prevents them from being inherited from them when they die. And in what we mentioned of this is evidence for what we described, that there is in this hadith no proof for some of the disputants in this meaning which we mentioned in this chapter against others. And Allah we ask for success."
"3218","Chapter 445 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding hospitality: his making it obligatory and what is besides that. 2810 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: al-Miqdad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: I came, I and a companion of mine whose hearing and sight had almost gone from hunger, and we began to present ourselves to people but no one hosted us. So we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we suffer severe hunger, and we presented ourselves to people but no one hosted us, so we came to you. So he took us to his house, and with him were four goats, and he said: O Miqdad, milk them, and divide the milk into a portion for every two."
"3219","2811 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Miqdad ibn Amr, who said: I arrived at Madinah, I and a companion of mine, then he mentioned the like of it. So this hadith indicates that hospitality is not obligatory, for had it been obligatory the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have denounced those who held back from it. Then someone said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you narrate from him."
"3220","2812 - Then he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. And he mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Miqdam Abu Karimah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A night of hospitality for the guest is a right, obligatory upon every Muslim; if he (the guest) wakes in his courtyard (deprived), it is a debt: if he wishes he exacts it, and if he wishes he leaves it."
"3221","2813 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So in this hadith is affirmation of the obligatoriness of hospitality, making it a debt upon the one with whom he alights. He said: And you also narrate from him, in stressing its obligatoriness, what exceeds what is in this hadith."
"3222","2814 - Then he mentioned what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir, who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, you send us out and we pass by people. He said: If you alight with people and they order for you what befits the guest, then accept; and if they do not do so, then take from them the right of the guest which befits."
"3223","2815 - And of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir al-Ghassani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from Marwan ibn Ru'bah, that he narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Awf al-Jurashi, from al-Miqdam ibn Ma'dikarib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever man hosts people and they do not feed him (as guest), he has (the right) to penalize them with the like of his hospitality."
"3224","2816 - And of what Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdallah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever guest alights with people and the guest wakes deprived, he may take to the measure of his hospitality, and there is no sin upon him."
"3225","2817 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us that Abu Talhah narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. He said: And this also indicates making it obligatory, and that it becomes for its people a debt upon those with whom they alight, like the rest of debts besides it. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: each guest of these two guests is possibly other than the other of them. What is in the hadith of al-Miqdad may be upon a guest who can compensate for hospitality with other than it, by buying what suffices him for it with what he has of what he can spend on its price, or by asking if he has nothing with him, until he reaches that by his asking - though the better for the one with whom he alights is to spare him that and to comply in his affair with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him of honoring him, upon what we mentioned before this chapter of this book of ours in that meaning. And what is in the two hadiths of Abu Hurayrah and al-Miqdam may be upon travelers passing by people in a desert who find no substitute for their hosting them, nor find what to buy of what suffices them for that. So each of the two hadiths we mentioned has an aspect other than the aspect of the other hadith. And among what indicates that is what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3226","2818 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Let none of you milk the livestock of his brother without his permission. Would one of you like his storeroom to be approached, his chest broken, and his food carried away? The udders of their livestock only store their foods for them. So let none of you milk the livestock of a person except with his permission."
"3227","2819 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3228","2820 - And as Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3229","2821 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Mas'ud al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3230","2822 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Suhayl, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd, from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a person to take the staff of his brother without his willing consent. He said: And that is because of the severity of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made sacred to the Muslim of the property of the Muslim."
"3231","2823 - And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id, from Umarah ibn Harithah, from Amr ibn Yathribi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: Nothing of the property of his brother is lawful to a person except with his willing consent. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, if I meet the sheep of my cousin, may I take something from them? He said: If you meet them carrying a blade and firesticks in the rugged land of al-Jamish, do not touch them. Abu Ja'far said: So in what we narrated is affirmation of the prohibition of the property of the Muslim to the Muslim. Then someone said: You have narrated from Abu Sa'id from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts this."
"3232","2824 - Then he mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I think, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When one of you comes upon a (walled) garden, let him call its owner three times; if he answers him, (well); otherwise let him eat without spoiling. And when he comes upon sheep, let him call their shepherd three times; if he answers him, (well); otherwise let him drink without spoiling. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: this is possibly upon necessity to that; indeed, we found it so."
"3233","2825 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Mukhawwal ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abdallah ibn Usmah, who said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: When people run out of provisions and come in the morning to camels, let them call the herdsman three times; if they do not find the herdsman and find the camels, let them draw the milk of the milch camel, if among the camels there is a milch camel, and they have no right to (the camel) itself. If the herdsman comes, two men should hold him and they should not fight him, and let them drink; if they have dirhams with them, it is unlawful for them (to take freely) except with the permission of its people. He said: So this is halted (mawquf) upon Abu Sa'id. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: the hadith he argued against us with is doubted: is it raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or halted upon Abu Sa'id? And we found this hadith of Ibn Usmah raised in Sharik's narration of it."
"3234","2826 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdillah narrated to us, from Abdallah ibn Usm, who said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri raising it (to the Prophet), who said: It is not lawful for anyone to untie the hobble of a she-camel except with the permission of its people, for it is their seal upon it. Abu Ja'far said: So that indicates that what is in the hadith of Abdallah ibn Usmah - whose father's name in this hadith is named Usm in place of Usmah - is raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that it is upon destitution (irmal), not upon (mere) finding. And we found from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) what indicates the meaning we adopted in this chapter."
"3235","2827 - As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir informed me, he said: Abd al-Rahman, the freedman of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, narrated to us, he said: I was with Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas on a journey, and night drove us to the village of a dihqan (landowner), and there were camels upon them their loads. Sa'd said to me: If you wish to be a true Muslim, do not eat anything of them; so we spent the night hungry. So this saying from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) indicates that his compliance with the realities of the affairs of Islam which its people must hold to, and leaving what opposes them, is what he does and what he commanded his freedman of what we mentioned. And that from him was in a village, not in a desert; so that saying from him was upon the rulings of villages, not upon the rulings of what is besides them of deserts. And Allah we ask for success."
"3236","Chapter 446 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The lahd (niche grave) is for us, and the shaqq (trench grave) is for others, or for the People of the Book. 2828 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman, from Zadham, from Jarir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lahd is for us, and the shaqq is for others."
"3237","2829 - And Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah al-Thumali, from Zadham, from Jarir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lahd is for us, and the shaqq is for the People of the Book."
"3238","2830 - And Ibn Abi Dawud al-Lahiqi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, he said: Uthman al-Bajali narrated to us, from Zadham, from Jarir ibn Abdillah, who said: A Bedouin embraced Islam, and while he was traveling his camel's hoof entered a lizard's hole, so it threw him and he died. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about him, saying: What did the Bedouin do; so he was told his story. He said: May Allah have mercy on him; he did little and will live long. Go with him and dig for him. They said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we make a shaqq for him or a lahd? He said: Make a lahd for him; the lahd is for us and the shaqq is for others."
"3239","2831 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn Ghannam narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Umayr, from Zadham, from Jarir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make a lahd and do not make a shaqq, for the lahd is for us and the shaqq is for others. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this saying of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found it possibly means: the lahd is for us, i.e. it is what we know, for the Arabs knew no other; and the shaqq is for the People of the Book, i.e. because it is what they used to use and knew no other. They had prophets (Allah's blessings upon them), and they were in their days upon that. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded His prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to follow those of the prophets before him by His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {Those are the ones whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance}. So upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was following them until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated their law for him with what He abrogated it. So the lahd and the shaqq both became among the practices of the Muslims, since they were not forbidden either of them - except that the lahd is worthier of the two, because it is what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose for His prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for a lahd was made for him and no shaqq was made for him. And among what indicates the permissibility of the shaqq and that no prohibition attached to it is what has been narrated of what the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after his death."
"3240","2832 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Baynuni narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, there was a man who made a lahd and a man who made a shaqq (darih). They said: We seek our Lord's choice, Mighty and Majestic, and we send to them both; whichever of them precedes, we leave him (to do it). So both were sent to, and the maker of the lahd preceded, so they made a lahd for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3241","2833 - And as Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Mubarak narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in that is what indicates that the lahd and the shaqq were both used. And by what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the lahd over the shaqq became clear the superiority of the lahd over the shaqq. And if someone says: In what you narrated in the report of the Bedouin, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when they said to him: Shall we make a lahd for him or a shaqq, said: Make a lahd for him; and in the hadith of Qays which you also narrated: and do not make a shaqq - so that would be upon prohibition of the shaqq. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success, is that that was not upon prohibition of the shaqq as disliked, but upon prohibition of leaving the more excellent and taking what is below it, of what has been narrated of what was done with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of their choosing for him the lahd over other than it."
"3242","2834 - Of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Ja'far al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd, from Amir ibn Sa'd, that Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Make a lahd for me, and set bricks over me, as was done with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3243","2835 - And of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Ja'far al-Mukharrimi narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3244","2836 - And of what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abu Usayb, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was placed in his lahd, al-Mughirah said: There remains upon me something near his feet they did not fix. He said: I shall enter and fix it. So he put his hand in and touched the feet of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then said: Heap the dust over me. So they heaped it upon him until it reached half his shanks, then he came out and said: I am the last of you in contact with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3245","2837 - And of what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Imran al-Jawni narrated to us, from Abu Usaym, who said: He witnessed that, then he mentioned this hadith."
"3246","2838 - And of what Isma'il ibn Hamdawayh al-Bikandi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu Burdah, whose dwelling is among Banu Hujr, narrated to us, he said: Alqamah ibn Marthad narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken from the direction of the qiblah, a lahd was made for him, and bricks were set over him upright."
"3247","2839 - And of what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman informed us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, who said: I was among those who dug the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When his lahd was leveled over him, I found something in the grave, so I descended and placed my hand upon the lahd; so I am the last of people in contact with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3248","2840 - And of what Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrate from his father Ishaq ibn Yasar, from Abdallah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, who said: I went out with my uncle Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) performing Umrah in the time of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him). When he arrived at Makkah, he alighted with Umm Hani bint Abi Talib. When he finished his circumambulation and shaved his head, a group of the people of Iraq entered upon him and said: Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah narrates that he is the last of people in contact with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He lied; the last of people in contact with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Qutham ibn Abbas."
"3249","2841 - And of what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A lahd was made for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and for Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"3250","2842 - And of what Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Asim ibn Umar, from Abdallah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A lahd was made for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and for Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both). Abu Ja'far said: So what we mentioned indicates that the shaqq is not forbidden, even though the lahd is better than it, because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose it for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then there was the like of that for the people of Badr, that Allah honored them with what He chose for them of the lahd over the shaqq."
"3251","2843 - As Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: Isma'il al-Suddi narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Four entered the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): al-Abbas, Ali, and al-Fadl (may Allah be pleased with them), and his lahd was leveled by a man of the Ansar who is the one who leveled the lahds of the graves of the martyrs on the day of Badr. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas a hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreeing with the hadith of Jarir regarding the lahd and the shaqq."
"3252","2844 - And it is what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Hakkam ibn Salm al-Razi narrated to us, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abd al-A'la mention from his father, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lahd is for us and the shaqq is for others. And some of the people of knowledge of chains claimed that Abd al-A'la, the author of this hadith which his son narrated from him, is Abd al-A'la ibn Abi Jamilah; if that is so, his standing in knowledge is eminent."
"3253","And there has been narrated from Abu al-Darda regarding the shaqq what Fahd ibn Sulayman and Harun ibn Kamil both narrated to us, they said: Abdallah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu al-Darda, that he was asked about the shaqq in the grave and saw no harm in it. So in what we narrated from Abu al-Darda in this is what agrees with what we adopted in this chapter regarding the shaqq in this hadith, of its permissibility, even though the lahd is better than it. And Allah we ask for success."
"3254","Chapter 447 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding wala (clientage) by alliance. 2845 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever allies himself with people without the permission of his (former) patrons, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; Allah accepts from him neither just recompense (adl) nor ransom (sarf)."
"3255","2846 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydallah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3256","2847 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hakim ibn Sayf al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydallah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Sulayman, meaning al-A'mash, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So in what we narrated of this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what indicates that it is permitted for a man to ally himself with a man, so that he thereby becomes a client after his acceptance of that from him. For since he forbade him to ally himself with him without the permission of his patrons - they being those who were his patrons before that - in that is what indicates that he may ally himself with him with their permission to him for that and their releasing him to him. And in that is what indicates that he was a client to them by other than manumission; for had he been a client to them by their manumitting him, he would have had no (right) to ally himself with others, nor to be a client to anyone besides them, whether they permitted him that or not."
"3257","2848 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Marwan said to them: Go and reconcile between these two: Sa'id ibn Zayd and Arwa bint Uways. So we went and said: What is your (dispute) with this woman? He said: Do you think I took anything of this woman's right? And I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever takes anything of land will be collared with it from seven earths; and whoever cuts off of the property of a Muslim man by his oath, no blessing will be in it for him; and whoever allies himself with the client of people without the permission of his (former) masters, upon him is the curse of Allah, Mighty and Majestic; Allah, Mighty and Majestic, accepts from him neither ransom nor just recompense. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith, from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'whoever allies himself with the client of people without the permission of his masters, upon him is the curse of Allah,' in that is what indicates that it is permitted for him to ally himself with him with the permission of his masters to him in that. And this hadith has been narrated with other than this wording."
"3258","2849 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us. 2850 - And as Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani and al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Azdi narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that they said: And whoever allies himself with a client without his permission, upon him is the curse of Allah. He said: So in that also is what indicates that it is permitted for him to ally himself with him with his permission."
"3259","2851 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote upon every clan its blood-money obligations, and said: No client shall ally himself with people except with their permission. He said: And I found in his document: And cursed... So in this hadith also: that no client shall ally himself with people except with their permission; and in that is what indicates that he may ally himself with them with their permission. And in all these reports was affirmation of (prior) wala upon the one allying himself, with other people, before this (new) alliance. So in that is what indicates that it is permitted for a man to ally himself with a man by his allying with him and by the acceptance of the one he allies with of that from him. And in that is release (confirmation) of the obligatoriness of wala by other than manumission, as the Iraqis say in that. And an opponent from the Hijazis opposed them in that with what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Wala belongs only to one who manumits; and we shall mention that in its chains in other than this place of this book of ours, in what is worthier of it than this place, if Allah, Exalted, wills. So of the proof against him in that, for those who oppose him in it, is that what he mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is as he mentioned, but it is intended for wala by manumission, not for wala by what is besides it. And we found a thing intended by the like of this saying, while it does not prevent there being in something besides it something of that kind. Of that is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {Alms are only for the poor and the needy}, the verse. So that was a negation from Him that those alms - they being the zakat - belong to other than those Allah named in this verse, while it did not prevent Him thereby that there be alms besides zakat for other people besides the categories mentioned in this verse; they being the voluntary alms of some people upon others, of those who are neither poor nor needy nor of a category of the categories mentioned in this verse. And what is in this verse was specific to zakat, while what is besides it of alms is otherwise, and for people other than its people. So like that is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding wala: wala belongs only to one who manumits - it is upon wala by manumission, and does not prevent there being besides it a wala, which is the wala which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in the hadiths of Ali, Sa'id ibn Zayd, and Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, by alliance. And the soundness of the hadiths of Ali, Sa'id, and Jabir, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that wala may be by alliance, and that the client may transfer his wala from the one he was a client to by it to others of people, with the permission of the one he transfers from and with the permission of the one he transfers to. And that he is not a client to the one he transfers to except by these three things, not without them. And Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad used to hold the obligatoriness of wala by alliance upon what is in these hadiths, and hold that the client may transfer his wala to whomever he wishes to transfer it to, whether his first patron is pleased with that or dislikes it, so long as he has not borne (paid) for him a جنayah (offense compensation) he committed; for if that was so, he has in their saying no (right) to transfer his wala from him in any state. And what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what we clarified its meanings and uncovered in this chapter, is worthier than what they said in it of what opposes that. For no one may stay behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in word or deed except in what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished him by from the rest of his nation, making his ruling in it different from their rulings in it. And there is in these hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) no mention of bearing جنayah; so that indicates there is no meaning to considering جنayah-bearings in that. And Allah we ask for success."
"3260","Chapter 448 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding a man's embracing Islam at the hands of a man, that he thereby becomes the worthiest of people of his life and his death: does he thereby become his client, or does he not become his client thereby until there is between him and him a fresh alliance. 2852 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Abu Ayyub Ubaydallah ibn Ubayd ibn Imran al-Tabarani narrated to us, they said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Abdallah ibn Mawhab, who said: I heard Tamim al-Dari say: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man who embraces Islam at the hands of a man, and he said: He is the worthiest of people of his life and his death."
"3261","2853 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir Abd al-A'la ibn Mushir al-Ghassani narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hamzah al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, from Abdallah ibn Mawhab, from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, from Tamim al-Dari (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. Fahd said to us: So I said to Abu Nu'aym when he narrated this hadith to us without mentioning in it anyone between Abdallah ibn Mawhab and Tamim al-Dari: Abu Mushir narrated it to us from Yahya ibn Hamzah, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, inserting between them Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb; but he said nothing."
"3262","2854 - Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shirazi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdallah ibn Mawhab narrate from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, from Tamim al-Dari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3263","2855 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abdallah ibn Mawhab, from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, that Tamim al-Dari said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"3264","2856 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from his father, from Abdallah ibn Wahb - and he did not say Ibn Mawhab - from Tamim al-Dari, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, (what of) a man of the polytheists who embraces Islam at the hands of a man of the Muslims? He said: He is the worthiest of people of his life and his death. He said: So in what we narrated of this hadith of Tamim was the Messenger of Allah's affirmation (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that a man's embracing Islam at the hands of a man necessitates for him that he is the worthiest of people of his life and his death. So people attached themselves to this hadith and affirmed thereby wala for the one at whose hands the Islam occurred, from the one who embraced Islam at his hands, making him thereby his client, and giving him inheritance from him. Among them is Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"3265","As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, who said: I witnessed Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz judge by that, meaning what is in this hadith of his, regarding a man who embraced Islam at the hands of a Muslim man and died leaving property and a daughter: so he gave the daughter half, and the one at whose hands he embraced Islam the remainder."
"3266","And as Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And among them is Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman."
"3267","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, that he said: When a disbeliever comes and embraces Islam at the hands of a Muslim, in enemy land or in Muslim land, his inheritance belongs to the one at whose hands he embraced Islam. And among them is Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib."
"3268","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Shaddad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Whoever embraces Islam at the hands of people, they guarantee his offenses, and his inheritance is lawful for them. And others went - they being most scholars besides them - to the view that a man's embracing Islam at the hands of a man does not necessitate his wala until he allies himself with him after that, so that he thereby becomes his client, as he would be his client had he allied with him without having embraced Islam at his hands before this. And this is the school of the Kufans. And this saying has been narrated from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri."
"3269","As Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Dhuhli al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Jamil al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that he was asked about a man who embraced Islam and allied himself with a man: is there harm in that? He said: There is no harm in it; Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) permitted that. He said: So in this hadith is affirmation of wala by alliance, not by Islam before it at the hands of a man without alliance of the Muslim with him. And the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'He is the worthiest of people of his life and his death,' is possibly in (the sense of) that he not ally himself with others, and that he intend his alliance toward him, since Allah, Mighty and Majestic, guided him at his hands and directed him by his directing him to the religion he entered. And we ask Allah for success."
"3270","Chapter 449 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his casting lots between two claimants before him for the oath: which of them begins with it. 2857 - Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Sa'id - he being Ibn Abi Arubah - narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Khilas, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that two men claimed a beast, and neither of them had proof, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to draw lots for the oath."
"3271","2858 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Ihab narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: People disputed before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he commanded them to swear; both parties hastened to the oath, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that lots be drawn between them as to which of them swears. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith, and what became clear to us of the aspect intended by it, by Allah's success, is: those two disputants had between them something in which each of them was a claimant against his companion, in which the oath was due upon him; so they were equal in that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not advance one of them over the other in taking it, but drew lots between them for it."
"3272","Chapter 450 - Explanation of the problematic over which the people of knowledge differed, of the longest duration of pregnancy, with what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. 2859 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Hasirah, who said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, who said: Abu Dharr said: That I swear ten times that Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal is dearer to me than that I swear once that he is not he. And that is because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to his mother and said: Ask her how long she carried him. So I asked her, and she said: I carried him twelve months. Then he sent me to her a second time and said: Ask her about his cry when..."
"3273","2860 - So we found that Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad ibn Nuh al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Abd al-Wahid, meaning Ibn Ziyad, from al-Harith ibn Hasirah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: I heard Abu Dharr say: That I swear ten times that Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal is dearer to me than that I swear a single oath that he is not he; and that is for something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to the mother of Ibn Sayyad and said: Ask her how long she carried him. So I asked her, and she said: I carried him twelve months. So I came to him and informed him, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. So in this was Abu Dharr's informing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that twelve-month pregnancy without his denying it, and in that is what indicates the permissibility of pregnancy being that long."
"3274","Of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Farwah ibn Abi al-Maghra al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: When a woman gives birth at nine months, twenty-one months of suckling suffice him; and when she gives birth at seven months, twenty-three months of suckling suffice him; and when she gives birth at six months, then two full years, for Allah, Exalted, says: And his gestation and his weaning are thirty months."
"3275","And of what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: When pregnancy is nine months, twenty-one months of suckling suffice; and when she carries six months, twenty-four months of suckling suffice; then Ibn Abbas recited: {And his gestation and his weaning are thirty months}. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that Ibn Abbas did not take gestation and weaning out of the thirty months; and in that is what indicates that gestation according to him does not exceed the thirty months. And when that is so, and pregnancy is two years, the remainder of the thirty months is six months. That is of what one questioner asked about, saying: Is it permitted that weaning be six months, while the bodies of infants cannot stand upon it, for they need of suckling a period longer than it? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: it is possible that those born after those six months return to delicate nourishment, so that is a living for them and suffices them instead of suckling. Except that we examined what is in the Book of Allah of mention of gestation and weaning, and we found of it (verses indicating two full years for suckling)."
"3276","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydallah ibn Abdillah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: There is no suckling after two full years."
"3277","As Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Amr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: There is no suckling after two years. Abu Ja'far said: So this Ibn Abbas restricted suckling to two years; so that indicates they are its duration according to him, and most jurists of the lands are upon that. So in that is what indicates the interpretation we interpreted of the three verses we recited in this chapter. And someone said: You mentioned regarding the duration of pregnancy in this chapter what you mentioned, of Abu Dharr's transmission to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the mother of Ibn Sayyad that she carried him twelve months, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that, and you made that a proof against one who denies that pregnancy is more than nine months. But Ibn Sayyad is possibly singled out in his mother's carrying him in this duration, to be a sign to the worlds, because of what is mentioned in him that he is the Dajjal. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: this possibility would only be hoped if he is the Dajjal whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the prophets before him, upon them be peace, warned their nations of, mentioning to them his states and his claiming divinity."
"3278","Chapter 451 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the envoys of the disbelievers, that they are not killed, even if there comes from them what would have necessitated his killing had they not been envoys. 2861 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us; and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us; they said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, he said: Abu Wa'il narrated to me, he said: Ibn Mu'ayz al-Sa'di narrated to me, he said: I went out exercising a horse of mine at dawn, and passed by a mosque of the mosques of Banu Hanifah, and heard them testify that Musaylimah is the Messenger of Allah. So I returned to Abdallah ibn Mas'ud and mentioned their affair to him; so he sent the police, and they seized them and brought them to him. They repented and retracted what they said, saying: We shall not return. So he released them, but brought forward a man of them called Abdallah ibn al-Nawwahah and struck his neck. People said: You seized people for one affair, then released some and killed some. He said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Ibn al-Nawwahah came to him with a man with him called Ibn Waththal Hajr, as two delegates from Musaylimah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? They said: We testify that Musaylimah is the Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah said: Were it not that envoys are not killed, I would have struck your necks."
"3279","2862 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, that he came to Abdallah and said: There is no rancor between me and anyone of the Arabs; I passed by the mosque of Banu Hanifah and there they believe in Musaylimah. So Abdallah sent to them and they were brought; he asked them to repent except Ibn al-Nawwahah, to whom he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Were it not that you are an envoy, I would have struck your neck; and today you are not an envoy. So he commanded Qarazah ibn Ka'b, who struck his neck in the market. Then he said: Whoever wishes to look at Ibn al-Nawwahah slain in the market, let him look."
"3280","2863 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Yunus, meaning Ibn Bukayr, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Sa'd ibn Tariq narrated to me, from Salamah ibn Nu'aym, from his father, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the envoys of Musaylimah came to him with his letter, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says to them: And do you two say the like of what he says? They said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, were it not that envoys are not killed, I would have struck your necks. So we examined these reports seeking to grasp what is meant by the Messenger of Allah's exempting delegations from being killed, even if there comes from them the like of what came from Ibn al-Nawwahah and his companion of what would necessitate their killing had they not been two envoys. We found that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said in His Book to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {And if any of the polytheists seeks your protection, grant him protection until he hears the speech of Allah} - i.e. so he follows it, i.e. he must reside where the Muslims besides him reside; or he does not follow it, so convey him to his safety. And his remaining upon his disbelief, which would necessitate shedding his blood had he not come to him seeking to hear Allah's speech, was made unlawful by that shedding of his blood until he leaves that seeking."
"3281","Chapter 452 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever changes his religion, kill him. 2864 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Ayyub (ha). And al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought people who were heretics, or who apostatized from Islam, and books were found with them. So he ordered a fire and it was kindled, and he cast them and their books (into it). That reached Ibn Abbas, who said: Had I been I, I would have killed them, by the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and not burned them, by the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever changes his religion, kill him, and do not punish with Allah's punishment."
"3282","2865 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah and Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever changes his religion, kill him."
"3283","2866 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us; and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Bundar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us - all of them from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever changes his religion, kill him."
"3284","2867 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, who said: People whom Ali burned were mentioned before Ibn Abbas, and he said: Had I been (in his place), I would have killed them, by the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever changes his religion, kill him; and I would not have burned them with fire, by the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): None punishes with Allah's punishment. That reached Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and it is as if he did not dislike it."
"3285","2868 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Isma'il, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So some went to the view that whoever apostatizes from Islam, his killing is obligatory, whether he returns to Islam or not; they made his apostasy necessitate killing upon him as a hadd for what came from him. They said: Just as the adulterer - his repentance does not lift from him the hadd of adultery; and just as the thief - his repentance does not lift from him the hadd of theft; like that is the apostate - his repentance does not lift from him the hadd of his apostasy, which is killing. So of our proof against them in that, for our opposing them in it, is that we found Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanding us to establish the hadd of adultery upon the adulterer, and to establish the hadd of theft upon the thief, saying in His Book: {The adulteress and the adulterer - flog each of them a hundred lashes}, and saying: {And the thief, male and female - cut their hands}. So the name of adultery does not leave the adulterer even if he abandons adultery, and likewise the name of thief adheres to the thief even if he ceases theft and leaves it. And we found the apostate - by his apostasy he became a disbeliever."
"3286","2869 - And it is what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A man of the Ansar apostatized and joined Makkah, then regretted, and sent to his people: Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whether I have repentance. He said: So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {How shall Allah guide a people who disbelieved after their belief and bore witness that the Messenger is true} - to His saying - {except those who repent after that and amend}. So they wrote with it to him, and he returned and embraced Islam. Abu Ja'far said: So the people of the first saying said: We found in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, what indicates what we mentioned, which is His saying, Majestic and Mighty: {Indeed whoever associates with Allah, Allah has forbidden Paradise to him}. So He, Mighty and Majestic, informed that whoever associates with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, He deprives him of Paradise, without mentioning that his return from his polytheism takes him out of that until he returns to being of the people of Paradise. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: it is permitted that He intends thereby the polytheism of one who remains upon it until he dies upon that, as He, Mighty and Majestic, said in the other verse: {And whoever of you turns back from his religion...}."
"3287","Chapter 453 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: A woman secures three inheritances: her freedman, her foundling, and her son over whom she invokes curses (li'an). 2870 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Sulayman ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Umar ibn Ru'bah, from Abd al-Wahid al-Nasri, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman secures three inheritances: her freed slave, her foundling, and her son over whom she invokes curses. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that a woman secures the wala of one she picks up. We examined that and found it possibly means the wala of one she picks up is due to her by her picking him up; and it is possible that since there is no wala upon him to anyone, just as he has no lineage from anyone, his ruling is like the ruling of the rest of people besides him who have no wala upon them: he may ally himself with whomever he wishes of people, and the worthiest of them in that is the one who picked him up and sponsored him until that was from him a cause of his life. So he ought not ally himself with others of people, since none of them has upon him the like of what he has."
"3288","Of what Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Sunayn Abu Jamilah, a man of Banu Sulaym, that he found an abandoned child in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and brought him to Umar ibn al-Khattab. He said: What carried you to take this soul? He said: I found it lost, so I took it. His headman (arif) said to him: O Commander of the Believers, he is a righteous man. He said: Is that so? He said: Yes. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Then go; he is free, and to you is his wala, and upon us is his maintenance. Malik said: And the matter with us regarding the abandoned child is that he is free, and his wala belongs to the Muslims: they inherit him and bear his blood-money."
"3289","And of what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-Zuhri, who said: I heard Sunayn Abu Jamilah narrate to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I found an abandoned child in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him); my headman mentioned me to Umar, saying: Summon him. So I came to him, and he said: What is your (business) with this? I said: I found a neglected soul and wished that Allah reward me in it. He said: He is free, and to you is his wala, and upon us is his maintenance. Abu Ja'far said: And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah have mercy on him) used to hold that Umar's saying (may Allah be pleased with him) to Abu Jamilah regarding this foundling of his, 'He is free, and to you is his wala,' means: by my assigning him to you. For the Imam, whose hand is upon the child who has no wala, may assign his wala to whomever he wishes of the Muslims, so he thereby becomes his client, as he would be his client had he allied with him while adult, sound of mind. And this bears what he said. And likewise Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) and all his companions used to say regarding the foundling: he is free, and he allies himself with whomever he wishes when he grows up; if he does not ally with anyone until he dies, his wala belongs to all the Muslims, his inheritance is placed in their treasury, and if he commits an offense before allying with anyone, its compensation is upon the Muslims in their treasury. And the meaning of what is in the hadith (of the woman securing three inheritances is upon alliance, not mere picking up)."
"3290","Of what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The abandoned child is free - meaning the foundling; if he wishes to ally himself with the one who picked him up, he allies with him, and if he wishes to ally himself with others, he allies with them. Abu Ja'far said: So the meaning of Ali's saying (may Allah be pleased with him), 'He is free,' is like the meaning of Umar's saying (may Allah be pleased with him), 'He is free,' in his hadith which we narrated before this hadith. And in Ali's saying, 'If he wishes to ally with the one who picked him up, he allies with him, and if he wishes to ally with others, he allies with them,' is what indicates that Umar's saying (may Allah be pleased with him) to Abu Jamilah, 'To you is his wala,' is in the sense: by our assigning him to you - not that to you belongs his wala by your picking him up without his allying himself with you. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3291","Chapter 454 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Between my grave and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise. 2871 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Qurashi al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: My father narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My minbar has been placed upon a rivulet of the rivulets of Paradise, and what is between my minbar and my house is a garden of the gardens of Paradise. Abu Ja'far said: And this hadith has been narrated by more than one of its people, among them: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Quta'i, Isma'il ibn Ishaq the judge, and Abu Shu'ayb Salih ibn Hakim, from this Muhammad ibn Sulayman."
"3292","2872 - And Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu Salamah, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my grave and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise, and the legs of my minbar are upon supports in Paradise."
"3293","2873 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, from Ubaydallah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my house and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon my Pool."
"3294","2874 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my grave and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise. Abu Ja'far said: And this is of the hadith of Malik; the people of knowledge of hadith say: none narrated it from Malik except this Ahmad ibn Yahya, and Abdallah ibn Nafi' al-Sa'igh."
"3295","2875 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Abu Hurayrah, or from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri - so Yunus narrated it to us with doubt - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my house and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon my Pool."
"3296","2876 - al-Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif ibn Abdillah al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Abu Sa'id, or from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3297","2877 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Hafs ibn Asim informed him, from Abu Hurayrah and from Abu Sa'id - so Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated it to us without doubt mentioned in it - then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yunus exactly, mentioning it from Abu Sa'id and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3298","2878 - And al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Kufi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My minbar is upon my Pool, and what is between my house and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise, and a prayer in this mosque of mine is like a thousand prayers in other than it of mosques, except the Sacred Mosque."
"3299","He said: And al-Miswar ibn Rifa'ah narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it."
"3300","2879 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sharfa, freedman of the family of Umar, narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that Abdallah ibn Umar said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my grave and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise."
"3301","2880 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdallah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdallah ibn Zayd al-Mazini, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my house and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise."
"3302","2881 - al-Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif ibn Abdillah narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abdallah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdallah ibn Zayd al-Mazini, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my house and my minbar is a garden of the gardens of Paradise."
"3303","2882 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman both narrated to us, they said: Abdallah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdallah ibn Zayd, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: What is between my minbar and my house is a garden of the gardens of Paradise."
"3304","2883 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Hushaym, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my minbar and my house is a garden of the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon a rivulet of the rivulets of Paradise. Then someone said: These reports indicate that the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his minbar are outside the garden. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: it is permitted that they are outside the garden as he mentioned, while his minbar is upon what has been clarified in these reports which we narrated in this chapter: that its legs are supports in Paradise, so it is of Paradise though other than the garden. And what has been narrated from Sahl ibn Sa'd from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning indicates this interpretation."
"3305","2884 - As Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Abdillah, meaning al-Wasiti, narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman, meaning al-Qari, narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This minbar of mine is upon a rivulet of the rivulets of Paradise. He said: So Sahl ibn Sa'd said: Do you know what the rivulet (tur'ah) is? It is a gate of the gates of Paradise. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that his minbar (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is of Paradise though other than the garden; it is the rivulet upon what is in this hadith. And his grave (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is of Paradise, either in a garden besides that garden of what is grander, more blissful, and higher in rank - for since his minbar, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, conveyed him by his sitting and standing upon it what He conveyed him, his grave which contained his body, becoming thereby his resting place, is worthier of that and likelier of increase over it. And Paradise - in it are gardens, not one garden, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said in His Book: And those who believe and do righteous deeds are in the gardens of Paradise; they have what they wish with their Lord; that is the great bounty. So it is permitted, if the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is of Paradise, that it be in a garden besides that garden."
"3306","2885 - And of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdallah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from al-Tufayl ibn Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he said: Between my house - or my grave - and my minbar. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success. Chapter 455 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in seeking refuge for al-Hasan and al-Husayn and his saying: There is no hamah. 2885 (continued) - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrate from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge for al-Hasan and al-Husayn, saying: I seek refuge for you both in Allah's perfect words from every devil and venomous pest, and from every envious eye. Then he says: Thus used Ibrahim to seek refuge for Ishaq and Isma'il, upon them be peace."
"3307","2886 - Then he mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from al-Hadrami, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no hamah."
"3308","2887 - And of what Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us. 2888 - And of what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us; then both agreed, saying: from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it (there is no hamah)."
"3309","2889 - And of what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to me, he said: al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, Zayd ibn Aslam, and Ubaydallah ibn Miqsam narrated to me, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3310","2890 - And of what Bahr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to him that Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no ham, there is no ham."
"3311","2891 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion, no Safar, and no hamah. He said: So in these hadiths is his negation of the hamah, and in that is negation of its existence; so how is it permitted that he seeks refuge for them both from something nonexistent? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: the hammah from which he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought refuge for them is the venomous pests of the earth whose harms are feared; and the hamah which he negated is other than it: it is what the Arabs used to say regarding their dead - they used to say the bones of the dead become a hamah (owl/spirit) and fly - until that was mentioned in their poetry. Of that is what Labid eulogized his brother Arbad with, and of that is the poetry of Abu Du'ad al-Iyadi. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negated that, upon what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we narrated. As for the hammah from which he sought refuge for Hasan and Husayn, it exists: it is the feared venomous pests of the earth, with doubled mim; and the hamah which he negated has un-doubled mim, so it is nothing of it. And of what the Arabs mentioned in their poetry regarding owls (ham) also is the saying of the one who said (verses eulogizing the disbelievers of Badr). Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) married a woman of Banu Kilab called Umm Bakr; when Abu Bakr emigrated he divorced her, and her cousin married her - this poet who spoke this poem eulogizing the disbelievers of Badr at the well. So it became clear, by Allah's praise and blessing, that there is no contradiction in anything of what this ignorant one thought contradictory of the sayings of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Each of the hammah and the ham turned to what we directed each of them to in this chapter. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3312","Chapter 456 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the evil eye: that it is real, and regarding washing for one afflicted by it. 2892 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Tawus narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The evil eye is real; and had anything preceded decree, the eye would have preceded it. And when you are asked to wash, then wash."
"3313","2893 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amr al-Ash'athi narrated to us, he said: Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: They used to command the afflicter (mu'in) to perform ablution, then the afflicted (mu'an) washes with it. Abu Ja'far said: So Ali narrated it to us, saying al-mu'in and al-mu'an; and what we retain from the people of language is that the doer from (the verb of) the eye is a'in, and the one done to is ma'yun. And some of them return the object of it to fa'il, like makil and mabi' and the like, so they say ma'in."
"3314","2894 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, who said: Amir ibn Rabi'ah passed by Sahl ibn Hunayf while he was bathing, and Sahl said: I never saw like today, nor the skin of a maiden in seclusion. It was not long before he was struck down by it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was approached and told: Reach Sahl, fallen. He said: Whom do you suspect of him? They said: Amir. He said: For what does one of you kill his brother? When he sees what pleases him, let him invoke blessing. And he commanded Amir to perform ablution for him and wash his face, his hands, his knees, and the inner part of his waist-wrapper, and pour upon him, inverting the vessel from behind him. Sufyan said to us: And they said from al-Zuhri - I did not retain: so he went off with the procession."
"3315","2895 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned the like of it, adding: So Sahl went off with the people with nothing wrong with him. Yunus said to us: Ibn Wahb said to us: Malik said: The inner part of the waist-wrapper is what is under the wrapper next to the body."
"3316","2895 bis - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Muhammad ibn Abi Umamah ibn Sahl, that he heard his father say: My father Sahl ibn Hunayf bathed at al-Kharrar, removing a cloak that was upon him, with Amir ibn Rabi'ah looking. Sahl was white, of beautiful skin, so Amir said to him: I never saw like today, nor the skin of a virgin. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"3317","2896 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ya'qub al-Juzajani informed me, he said: Shababab narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from his father, that Amir passed by him while he was bathing, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3318","2897 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Ja'far - he being Ibn Burqan - from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Amir ibn Rabi'ah, that he saw Sahl ibn Hunayf, while with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Ji'ranah, bathing, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3319","2898 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Abu Umamah narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yunus from Ibn Wahb from Malik from Ibn Shihab, adding: Muhammad ibn Muslim said: And the washing which we found our scholars describing: the man who afflicts his companion with the eye is brought a cup in it water, held for him raised from the ground; the one who afflicts his companion inserts his right hand into the water and pours upon his face of it once into the cup; then he inserts his left hand into the water and washes his right hand to the elbow with his left hand of it once into the cup; then he inserts his right hand and washes his left hand to the elbow one pouring into the cup; then he inserts both hands into the water and washes his chest one pouring into the cup; then he inserts his hand and rinses, then spits it into the cup; then he inserts his left hand and scoops of the water and pours it upon the back of his right hand one pouring into the cup; then he inserts his left hand and pours upon the elbow of his hand of it once into the cup, bending his hand to his neck; then he does the like of that upon the elbow of his left hand; then he does that upon the outer part of his right foot from the roots of the toes, and the left likewise; then he inserts his left hand and pours upon the back of his right knee; then he does with the left the like of that; then he dips the inner part of his right waist-wrapper into the water; then the one in whose hand is the cup stands with the cup until he pours it upon the head of the afflicted from behind him; then he inverts the cup upon the face of the earth behind him."
"3320","2899 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah, then he mentioned the like of it upon what is in this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and upon what is in it of the description of washing. And we do not know narrated regarding washing from the eye other than what we mentioned in this chapter in it. As for what has been narrated regarding the eye that it is real, in which there is no mention of washing, I narrated that in reports."
"3321","2900 - Of them is what Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Talib ibn Habib ibn Amr ibn Sahl al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir al-Ansari narrated to us, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Most of those of my nation who die, after Allah's Book and His decree and destiny, die by souls (i.e. the evil eye)."
"3322","2901 - And of them is what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sulayman, meaning al-Ruhawi, narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Ruzayq, from Abdallah ibn Isa, from Umayyah ibn Hind, from Abdallah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from his father, who said: I went out, I and Sahl ibn Hunayf, seeking a thicket; we found a pool with thickets, and each of us was shy to strip while anyone saw him, concealing himself until when he saw that he had done so, he removed a woolen cloak upon him. I looked at him, and his build pleased me, so I struck him with the eye. Seizure took him, so I called him but he did not answer me. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He said: Stand. He raised (his garment) from his shanks until he waded to him through the water, and it is as if I look at the whiteness of the shanks of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He struck his chest and said: In the name of Allah; O Allah, remove its heat, its cold, and its ailment. Stand, by Allah's permission. So he stood. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sees in himself, his property, or his brother what pleases him, let him invoke blessing, for the eye is real. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sufficed for Sahl with supplication; and in the hadith of Abu Umamah is his commanding Amir to wash for him. It is possible he combined both for him; it is possible that was twice, Sahl being afflicted in each of them by Amir with what afflicted him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did for him in each of them what he did therein, of supplication and of commanding washing; and it is possible the washing was, then abrogated by other than it."
"3323","2902 - Of what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad, meaning Ibn al-Awwam, narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from the eye of the jinn and the eye of men; when the two Mu'awwidhatan (al-Falaq and al-Nas) were revealed, he took them and left what was besides that. And there has been narrated of them also:"
"3324","2903 - What Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ma'bad ibn Khalid, who said: I heard Abdallah ibn Shaddad narrate from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to seek ruqyah from the eye."
"3325","2904 - And of them is what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Fahd ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained, so Jibril (peace be upon him) performed ruqyah for him, saying: In the name of Allah I perform ruqyah for you, from everything that harms you, and from every envier and eye; and Allah heals you. He said: So in these reports is sufficing with the two Mu'awwidhatan and with ruqyah; and in that is what indicates the abrogation of washing, especially what is in the hadith of Abbad from al-Jurayri from Abu Nadrah from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from the eye of the jinn and the eye of men, then when the two Mu'awwidhatan were revealed he took them and left what was besides that. So in it is the abrogation of washing and what besides it he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do before their revelation to him. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3326","Chapter 457 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding sitting ihtiba (leaning on shanks with arms around legs) on Friday while the imam preaches. 2905 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Marhum Abd al-Rahim ibn Maymun, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh ibn Anas al-Juhani, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ihtiba on Friday while the imam preaches. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ihtiba on Friday while the imam preaches. And we found from a group of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they used to sit in ihtiba on Friday while the imam preaches. Of that is what..."
"3327","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to sit in ihtiba on Friday while the imam preaches, and sometimes he dozed until his forehead struck his ihtiba (his knees/arms). And of that is what..."
"3328","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Hayyan al-Raqqi narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zibriqan, from Ya'la ibn Shaddad ibn Aws, who said: I was at Bayt al-Maqdis, with Mu'awiyah preaching to people, all of them companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I saw them sitting in ihtiba. Abu Ja'far said: And the like of this prohibition from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is far from being hidden from their group; so in their practicing what we narrated from them in these reports is what indicates that the meaning of the prohibition which came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that is not the ihtiba they used to do while the imam preaches. For they are trusted upon what they did, as they are trusted upon what they narrated. And since that is so, the worthier for us is to carry it upon ihtiba newly begun during the sermon, for it is disliked in the sermon to occupy oneself with other than it and turn to what is besides it. The ihtiba they used to do would be an ihtiba they adopted before the sermon, the imam preaching while they are in it, until he finishes from it while they remain upon it. And what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them would be besides that, of what they newly begin while their imam preaches, thereby becoming distracted from turning to what they were commanded to turn to. And Allah we ask for success."
"3329","Chapter 458 - Explanation of the problematic of what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding reinforcements arriving to the imam in the abode of war after he gained spoils in it, before leaving it, dividing them, or selling them: do they share with those with him in those spoils or not? 2906 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdallah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, that Anbasah ibn Sa'id informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrate to Sa'id ibn al-As, Abu Hurayrah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Aban ibn Sa'id upon an expedition from Madinah toward Najd. Aban and his companions arrived to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar after he conquered it, with the bundles of their horses of fiber. Aban said: Apportion to us, O Messenger of Allah. Abu Hurayrah said: So I said: Do not apportion them anything, O Prophet of Allah. Aban said: You with it, O wabr (hyrax, i.e. contemptible creature), descending upon us from the top of a stray (mountain). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sit, O Aban. And he did not apportion them anything."
"3330","2907 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz, who said: I heard al-Zuhri inform from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard him narrate from Sa'id ibn Abi al-As - so Ibn Abi Dawud narrated it to us, but it is Sa'id ibn al-As who narrates - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-As in an expedition toward Najd; Aban and his companions arrived to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after he conquered Khaybar, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to apportion us anything - so Ibn Abi Dawud narrated it to us also, but it is to apportion them anything. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith the asker of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to apportion to him and his companions is Aban; and it has been narrated that the asker of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that was Abu Hurayrah."
"3331","2908 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, that he asked al-Zuhri, I being present - Sufyan said: I did not retain it - he said: Anbasah ibn Sa'id informed me, he said: Abu Hurayrah and his companions arrived at Khaybar after it was conquered, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there; so he asked him to share him in the spoils. One of Banu Sa'id ibn al-As spoke, saying: O Messenger of Allah, this is the killer of Ibn Qawqal. He said: How strange - he blames me for the killing of a Muslim man whom Allah honored at my hands and did not disgrace me at his hands. Sufyan mentioned it from Isma'il ibn Umayyah and others."
"3332","2909 - And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anbasah ibn Sa'id ibn al-As, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I arrived to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions at Khaybar after they conquered it; I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to allot me of the spoils. One of Banu Sa'id ibn al-As said: Do not allot them, O Messenger of Allah. I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the killer of Ibn Qawqal. Sa'id said: How strange for a wabr descending upon us from the peak of a sheep-mountain, blaming me for the killing of a Muslim man whom Allah honored at my hands and did not disgrace me at his hands. Sufyan said: I do not know - or I do not retain - whether he allotted him or not. Sufyan said: I heard Isma'il ibn Umayyah ask al-Zuhri about it, I being present. Abu Ja'far said: So this disagreement occurred as to who the asker of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for what he asked him in this hadith is - and Allah knows best which of that it was."
"3333","2910 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Khuthaym ibn Irak narrated to us, from his father, from some of his people, that Abu Hurayrah arrived at Madinah, he and some of his people, saying: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gone out to Khaybar, with a man of Banu Ghifar called Siba' ibn Urfutah left in charge of Madinah. We came to him while he prayed with people the dawn prayer, reciting in the first rak'ah {Kaf Ha Ya Ayn Sad} and in the second {Woe to the defrauders}. Abu Hurayrah said: So I say while in prayer: Woe to so-and-so's father, he has two measures: when he measures for himself he measures full, and when he measures (for others) he measures short. When we finished our prayer, we came to Siba', who provisioned us something until we arrived to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Khaybar conquered; so he spoke to the Muslims, who shared us in their shares. He said: So this hadith indicates that the asker of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this story was Abu Hurayrah, not Aban ibn Sa'id. And in this hadith is a point of jurisprudence over which scholars differed: a group of them obligate, for one whose state in this meaning is like the state of Aban or the mentioned Abu Hurayrah in these reports, entering into the spoils gained before his arrival, since the imam is resident in the abode of war to that time, not safe from the enemy befalling him and taking what is in his hand of spoils. His need for reinforcements to that time - they obligate for them sharing in those spoils. Of those saying that are Abu Hanifah and his companions (may Allah have mercy on them). And a group of them say: they do not share them in those spoils; they are Malik, al-Awza'i, and al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on them). And Umar ibn al-Khattab and Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with them both) differed in that. As..."
"3334","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, who said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab: the people of Basra raided Nahawand, and the people of Kufa reinforced them; they prevailed, but the people of Basra wished not to apportion to the people of Kufa. Ammar was over the people of Kufa, and a man of Banu Utarid said: O maimed one, do you wish to share in our spoils? He said: With the better of my two ears you insulted. He said: So he wrote of that to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote: Spoils belong to one who witnessed the battle. He said: And both groups agreed that had the imam conquered that abode until it became like the abode of the Muslims, and he became safe from the enemy and their return to it and their fighting him over what he gained of them in it, then those reinforcements joined them after that, they do not share them in the spoils they gained before their joining them and arriving to him. Then we looked at the cause by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused Aban or Abu Hurayrah entry into those spoils: what is it? It is possible that it is because Khaybar had become, before their joining him and arriving to them, an abode of Islam, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had no need by their arrival to him, and so did not apportion to them thereby. And it is possible that he did not apportion to them because Khaybar was what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, promised the people of Hudaybiyah by His saying: {Allah promised you many spoils which you will take} - meaning the people of Hudaybiyah - so He hastened for you this, meaning Khaybar. And that has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3335","2911 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I never witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoils except he apportioned to me, except Khaybar, for it belonged to the people of Hudaybiyah exclusively. He said: And the Messenger of Allah's leaving apportionment in that to Aban or to Abu Hurayrah was because they were not of the people of Hudaybiyah. And in Aban's or Abu Hurayrah's asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to apportion to him - he being a man of his companions, a jurist - and the Messenger of Allah's leaving denunciation of that asking upon him, is what indicates that he did not ask an impossibility. For had he asked an impossibility, he would have said to him: And how shall I allot to you when you did not witness the fighting from which those spoils came. Then someone said: And how can those spoils belong to the people of Hudaybiyah when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shared Abu Hurayrah in them, upon what is in the hadith of Irak which we narrated? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, is: it is permitted that those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke to in that until they allowed it are the people of Hudaybiyah."
"3336","2912 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, from Hafs ibn Ghiyath, from Burayd ibn Abdillah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three days after the conquest of Khaybar, and he allotted shares to us, and he did not allot to anyone who had not witnessed the conquest besides us. He said: So this too is possible to have been that he allotted to them by speaking to the people of al-Hudaybiyah concerning them until they willingly allowed that to them, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that, and Him we ask for guidance."
"3337","459 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting speech intended for making peace between people, and speech a man speaks to his wife, and speech a woman speaks to her husband, and speech in war. 2913 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Uthman - meaning Ibn Khuthaym - from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Asma the daughter of Yazid who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Lying is not proper except in one of three: making peace between people, a man's lie to his wife to please her, and the lie of war."
"3338","2914 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Waqid, from Abdillah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Verily lying is not proper except in one of three: a man who lies to his wife to improve her disposition, a man who lies to make peace between two Muslim women, and a man who lies in the stratagem of war; indeed war is deception."
"3339","2915 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Shahr ibn Hawshab who said: Asma the daughter of Yazid al-Ashariyah informed me, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every lie is written against the children of Adam except: one who lies to his wife, or a man who lies between two Muslim women making peace between them, and a man who lies in war. He said: So we examined these reports and found in them the statement of the one from whom they were reported among the cases deemed proper for lying attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we found Allah, Mighty and Majestic, saying in His Book: O you who believe, fear Allah and be with the truthful, and we found Him, Mighty and Majestic, saying in His Book: And shun the false word. So in what we recited was His command, Mighty and Majestic, to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who believed in him, to be with the truthful, and they are the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the prophets before him, the blessings of Allah upon them, and He did not restrict that to one condition apart from another, nor one time apart from another, but rather made it general over all conditions and all times, and likewise what He commanded to be avoided therein is likewise over all times and all conditions. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the furthest of people from contradicting what his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, commanded him. Then we looked whether anything besides what we narrated in this chapter was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning these meanings."
"3340","2916 - So we found that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah that she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The liar is not the one who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good."
"3341","2917 - And we found that Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us that his mother Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah, and she was among the emigrant women who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The liar is not the one who conveys good or says good to make peace between people. And in these two hadiths was the denial by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of lying from one who makes peace between people, conveying good or saying good, and that could only be upon speech with equivocations of words by which its speaker is not a liar."
"3342","2918 - And we found that Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah informed him that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The liar is not the one who makes peace between people, saying good or conveying good, and he did not permit anything of what people call a lie except in three: in war, and making peace between people, and a man's speech to his wife, and a woman's speech to her husband."
"3343","2919 - And we found that Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Harb, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman that Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah narrated to him that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like of it. He said: And in this hadith too was the denial by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of lying from one from whom these conditions came, and in it was: and he did not permit anything of what people call a lie, meaning: according to its outward appearance to them, while its speaker is not a liar since he did not intend lying by it but intended another meaning, so in that was the denial of lying from what came from him."
"3344","2920 - And we found that Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Al-Husn ibn Muhammad, meaning al-Zafarani, informed us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ayyub and Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not a liar who makes peace between people, or says good or conveys good. He said: So the discussion on this was like the discussion on what we narrated before it in the second of the two sections whose narration preceded in this chapter. So a speaker said: The hadith of Umm Kulthum was reported like what the hadith of Asma was reported with, so he mentioned what."
"3345","2913 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us with it, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was narrated from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted lying in three: in war, and in a man's speech to his wife, and in making peace between people."
"3346","2921 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Bukayr narrated to us. 2922 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, each one of them said: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn al-Had, from Abd al-Wahhab, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum the daughter of Uqbah, she said: I did not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting anything of lying except in three; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: I do not count as a liar the man who makes peace between people, saying speech by which he intends nothing but peace, and the man who says speech in war, and the man who speaks to his wife, and the woman who speaks to her husband. So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, was: that the hadith of Ibrahim ibn Abi Asim is corrupt in chain, because Ibn Jurayj only narrated it from an unknown man from Ibn Shihab; as for the hadith of Abd al-Wahhab, then what is in it is a report from one of its narrators that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted these things, and there is not in it that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: there is no harm in lying in those things, but only in it that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted in that in those things, and what is in it of mention of lying may be what the speaker of that among the narrators of this hadith counted as lying though it is not a lie in reality, but only due to his supposition of that. And in that is what we have established of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein agreeing with that chapter. So if a speaker says: Is equivocation permitted in such like this, such that the addressee falls in his heart upon the opposite of the reality of the speech of the one addressing him? So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: that is among what is not objectionable. He said: And it is in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in the story of Musa, peace be upon him, with his companion when he said to him: Do not blame me for what I forgot, not because he forgot, but upon equivocations of speech, and the like of that is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: War is deception."
"3347","2923 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Dhi Huddan, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called war deception."
"3348","2924 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq together narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: War is but deception."
"3349","2925 - And as Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Fadalrah ibn al-Mufaddal ibn Fudalah ibn Ubayd al-Qitbani narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in the mention by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of war that it is such was what we understood by it that the speech intended for war is speech whose outward is a meaning frightening the people of war, though its inward is among what the speakers by it intend the opposite of that, and since that is so in war we understood by it that what is permitted therein in war in the preceding reports in this chapter is this meaning itself, nothing else, and since that is so in war, then what a man makes peace between people with, and what he improves his wife's heart with, and what the wife improves her husband's heart with, is this meaning too, not lying, and that was also established in the hadith of Umm Kulthum: and he did not permit anything of what people say is a lie except in three, meaning of what people say is a lie, while it is not a lie, and these meanings are more worthy for the people of knowledge to carry the commands of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon, and in what we narrated of these hadiths of Umm Kulthum from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the liar is not the one who walks making peace between people, conveying good or saying it, and in that is the denial by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of lying from one whose condition was that, and when lying is thereby negated from him, lying is negated from one from whom lying also came, and it is established that what was in that is equivocations, nothing else. And there was reported concerning equivocations from Umar ibn al-Khattab and Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, what."
"3350","Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Abu Muhammad al-Razi narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Is there not in equivocations what suffices the Muslim from lying? And what."
"3351","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abdillah who said: I accompanied Imran ibn Husayn from Kufah to Basrah, and no day would pass upon us but he would recite poetry to us upon it, and he said: Indeed in equivocations there is ample room away from lying. He said: And these are the meanings upon which we explained the meanings of these reports; as for the hadith of Asma the daughter of Yazid in which is the explicitness he was explicit with therein, then it only revolved upon Abdillah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, and he is a man impugned in his narration, attributed to bad memory, to little precision, and bad taking. As for the hadith of Umm Kulthum, then whoever among the people of knowledge whose like is taken from them narrated it, then only the denial of lying is mentioned therein, among them Malik ibn Anas, and among them Salih ibn Kaysan, and he added over Malik therein that what he permitted therein, so he mentioned those things, then he said: of what people say is a lie, so he attributed lying to the speech of people concerning those things, not to the realities of those things, and Allah we ask for guidance."
"3352","460 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning snakes, of unrestricted killing of them, and of leaving concession in that, and what was reported from him therein contradicting that. 2926 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Talut ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd al-Abdi, from Abu al-Ayan al-Abdi, from Abu al-Ahwas al-Jushami who said: While Ibn Masud was preaching one day, behold a snake walking upon the wall, so he cut his sermon and struck it with his staff until he killed it, then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever kills a snake, it is as if he killed a polytheist man whose blood had become lawful."
"3353","2927 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Kill the snakes, and kill the striped and the short-tailed, for they seek out sight and cause miscarriage of pregnancy; whoever finds the striped and the short-tailed and does not kill them, then he is not of us."
"3354","2928 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, he said: And Muhammad ibn Muslim informed me that Salim ibn Abdillah informed him that he heard Abdillah ibn Umar saying: Kill the striped and the short-tailed, for they seek out sight and cause miscarriage of pregnancy."
"3355","2929 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of snakes: We have not made peace with them since we warred with them; whoever leaves them out of fear, then he is not of us. He said: So in what we narrated is the command to kill all snakes, and leaving concession in that, and there was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his prohibition of killing the house-dwellers among them."
"3356","2930 - As Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqilah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kill the snakes, and the striped and the short-tailed, for they seek out sight and cause miscarriage of pregnancy. He said: And Ibn Umar used to kill every snake he saw, then Abu Lubabah or Zayd ibn al-Khattab saw him chasing a snake and said: He prohibited house-dwellers."
"3357","2931 - And as Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Muslim, from his uncle, from Salim who said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Kill the snakes, and kill the striped and the short-tailed, for they seek out sight and cause miscarriage of pregnancy. Ibn Umar said: So I would not leave a snake on earth I was able over but I killed it, then while I was seeking a house snake, Zayd ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abu Lubabah saw me and said: Stop, stop, O Abdillah. So I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to kill them. So he said: No, for he prohibited house-dwellers, meaning the inhabitants of houses."
"3358","2932 - And as Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi, from Ibn Umar that he used to kill all snakes, leaving nothing of them, and Abu Lubabah al-Badri narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited killing the small snakes that are in houses, so he refrained."
"3359","2933 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3360","2934 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Abu Lubabah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited killing the small snakes that are in houses."
"3361","2935 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Nafi that Abu Lubabah passed by Abdillah ibn Umar while he was at the fortress by the house of Umar watching for a snake, so Abu Lubabah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), O Abu Abd al-Rahman, prohibited killing the inhabitants of houses, so Abdillah ibn Umar refrained after that, then he found thereafter in his house a snake, so he commanded concerning it, and it was taken and taken out at Bathhan. Nafi said: I saw it thereafter in his house."
"3362","2936 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydillah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited killing the small snakes in houses."
"3363","2937 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Yahya ibn Said who said: Nafi informed me that Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir al-Ansari, his dwelling was at Quba, then he moved to Madinah, then while Ibn Umar was sitting with him, he opened for him a small door, when behold a snake of the house inhabitants, so he wanted to kill it, so Abu Lubabah said: They were prohibited, meaning the inhabitants of houses, and killing the short-tailed and the striped was commanded, and he said: They are the two that snatch sight and cast the children of women. He said: So in these hadiths the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited killing house-dwellers after he had commanded killing all snakes, so that was more entitled than the first hadiths, because in them is abrogation of part of what is in the first hadiths. Then we looked into the cause by which that abrogation was, what it is."
"3364","2938 - So we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Sayfi, freedman of Ibn Aflah, who said: Al-Saib, freedman of Hisham ibn Zuhrah, informed me that he said: I entered upon Abu Said al-Khudri in his house and found him praying, so I sat waiting for him when his prayer would end, then I heard movement in palm stalks in a corner of the house, so I turned, and behold a snake, so I sprang to kill it, so he signaled to me to sit, so I sat, then when he finished he pointed to a room of the house: Do you see this room? He said: There was in it a young man newly wed, so we went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Trench, and that youth would seek permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at midday to return to his family, so he sought his permission one day, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take your weapon upon you, for I fear Qurayzah for you, so he took his weapon, then returned to his family, when his wife was standing between the two doors, so he inclined to her with the spear to stab her with it, and jealousy seized him, so she said: Restrain your spear upon you, and enter the door until you see what brought me out, so he entered and behold a great snake coiled upon the bed, so he inclined to it with the spear and pierced it with it, then he went out and fixed it in the house, then it writhed upon him, so I do not know which of them was quicker in death; the snake or the youth, so we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and we said: Supplicate Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to give him life for us, so he said: Seek forgiveness for your companion, then he said: In Madinah there are jinn who have become Muslim, so if you see anything of them, warn it three times, then if it appears to you after that, kill it, for it is but a devil."
"3365","2939 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Ajlan, from Sayfi Abu Said, freedman of the Ansar, from Abu al-Saib, then he mentioned this hadith with fewer words than these, without difference in meanings."
"3366","2940 - And Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi that a youth of the Ansar who was newly wed went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, then when he returned he entered his house, when his wife was standing in the house, so he inclined to her with the spear, so she said: Stay as you are, do not hasten, enter the house, so he entered and behold a snake coiled upon his bed, so he pinned it with his spear and brought it out to the house, and placed it, then the snake writhed and the man writhed, so the snake died and the man died, then that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A tribe of Muslim jinn has descended at Madinah, so if you see anything of them, seek refuge in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from them, then if they return, kill them. So we examined these reports, and we found in the two hadiths of Abu Said and Sahl what is therein of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed of the jinn who appeared at Madinah, of those who became Muslim and became inhabitants of its houses, so he prohibited killing them for that until they are adjured, then if they appear after that they are outside the meaning for which killing them was prohibited, and they return to the ruling all snakes were upon before that, of lawfulness of killing them. And there was reported from Abu Thalabah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what enters into this chapter."
"3367","2941 - Of what Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Thalabah al-Khushani that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The jinn are upon three thirds: a third having wings flying in the air, a third snakes and dogs, and a third settling and departing. So that was among what established that some snakes are jinn, and that therein is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in the two hadiths of Abu Said and Sahl, and Allah, Glorified and Exalted, we ask for guidance."
"3368","461 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Ibn Sayyad the Jew, of what a people thereby unrestrictedly called him the Dajjal, and of what a people thereby prevented him from being the Dajjal. 2942 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: A Jewish woman at Madinah gave birth to a boy whose eye was wiped, protruding, bulging, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared he was the Dajjal, so he found him under a blanket murmuring, so his mother alerted him, saying: O Abdillah, this is Abu al-Qasim who has come, so go out to him, so he came out of the blanket, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with her - may Allah kill her - had she left him he would have made clear, then he said: O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see? He said: I see truth, and I see falsehood, and I see a throne upon water, so he said to him: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So he said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I believe in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His messengers, then he went out and left him, then he came to him another time and found him in their palm trees murmuring, so his mother alerted him, saying: O Abdillah, this is Abu al-Qasim who has come, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with her - may Allah kill her - had she left him he would have made clear. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hoped to hear something of his speech, so he would know whether it is he or not, so he said: O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see? He said: I see truth, and I see falsehood, and I see a throne upon water, so he said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So he said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I believe in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His messengers, so it was made confused to him, then he went out and left him, then he came a third and fourth time, with Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, among a group of Emigrants and Helpers, and I was with him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened ahead of us hoping to hear something of his speech, so his mother preceded him to him, saying: O Abdillah, this is Abu al-Qasim who has come, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with her - may Allah kill her - had she left him he would have made clear, so he said: O Ibn Sayyad, what do you see? He said: I see truth, and I see falsehood, and I see a throne upon water, so he said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So he said: Do you testify that you are the Messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I believe in Allah and His messengers, so it was made confused to him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ibn Sayyad, we have hidden for you something hidden, so what is it? He said: Smoke, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be gone, be gone. So Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Permit me to kill him, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If it is he, you are not his companion; his companion is only Isa son of Maryam, and if it is not he, you have no right to kill a man of the people of covenant. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained fearing he was the Dajjal. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw of Ibn Sayyad what he saw of his eye, and heard of his murmuring what he heard, and stood upon the evidences mentioned of him in this hadith, he did not feel safe from his being the Dajjal whose emergence in his nation Allah had informed him of, so he said concerning him what he said, without verifying from him that it is he, since no revelation came to him with that; nor that it is not he, since no revelation came to him with that, and he withheld from unrestrictedly stating either of those two matters concerning him. So a speaker said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, swore by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he is the Dajjal, and he did not deny that upon him."
"3369","2943 - And he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muthanna ibn Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to us, from Shubah, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdillah swearing by Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal, without exception, so I said to him: You swear by Allah without exception! So he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, swearing upon that by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny it."
"3370","2944 - And of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Shubah, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3371","2945 - And of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Ufayr ibn Madan narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So in this is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard Umar swearing he is the Dajjal and did not deny that upon him, nor forbid him from it. He said: So in that is what indicated his affirmation of him upon what he swore upon of that, and were it not that he would have refuted him upon it. So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: that it may be that the leaving by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of denying that was because he swore upon a possibility of what he swore upon, of what no revelation had descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to it, so he left denying upon him for that. This speaker said: And there was reported from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that there was from him the like of that after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of what."
"3372","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abdillah ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: By Allah, that I swear nine times that Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal is dearer to me than that I swear once that he is not he. He said: So our answer to him concerning that too, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, about this was like our answer to him with what we answered him in the hadith before this, and there was reported from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, what indicated that this that was from him concerning Ibn Sayyad was only from him due to the like of what Umar had stood upon of him, so there was from Umar therein what was of his swearing he is the Dajjal."
"3373","2946 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking, then we passed by boys among whom was Ibn Sayyad, so the boys fled and Ibn Sayyad sat, so it was as if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked that, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May your hands be dusted, do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So he said: No, but do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So Umar said: Leave me to kill him, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If it be the one you see, you will not be able to kill him. So we established by this hadith that what was from Abdillah ibn Masud in his matter until he said on account of it what he said, is what was with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in his matter until there was from his swearing that he is the Dajjal what was, and likewise Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, in the hadith of al-Harith ibn Hasirah which we narrated in what preceded of us in this book of ours, of his saying: That I swear Ibn Sayyad is the Dajjal ten times is dearer to me than that I swear once that he is not he, is like what Umar and Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them both, were upon in his matter, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood afterwards upon what Tamim al-Dari narrated to him."
"3374","2947 - As Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus, known as al-Susi, narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from al-Shaybani, from Amir, from Fatimah the daughter of Qays who said: While the people at Madinah were secure with no fear upon them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and prayed noon, then came walking until he ascended the pulpit, so the people were frightened. She said: So when he saw that in their faces he said: O people, I did not frighten you, but a matter came to me at which I rejoiced, so I loved to inform you of the joy of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Tamim al-Dari informed me that a people of his cousins rode a ship in the sea, then their ship ended with them at an island they did not know, so they went out looking, when behold a human of whom they did not know whether male or female due to abundance of hair, so they said: Who are you? He said: I am al-Jassasah. They said: So inform us. She said: Go to the monastery, for in it is a man longing for you to speak to him. He said: So they entered the monastery, when behold a man bound with iron groaning severe groaning, so he said to them: Who are you? So they said: Of the people of Palestine, of the Arabian Peninsula. He said: So did their Prophet come out after? So they said: Yes. He said: So what did he do? They said: A people followed him and a people parted from him, so he fought with those who followed him those who parted from him until they gave him the jizyah. He said: And of which land are you? So they said: Of the people of Palestine. He said: So what did the Lake of Tiberias do? So they said: It is full overflowing. He said: So what did the spring of Zughar do? They said: Its edge overflows. He said: So what did palms between Amman and Baysan do? They said: They have borne fruit. He said: Were I released from my bond I would have trodden all lands except Taybah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rejoice, O people."
"3375","Sulalyman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Uqbah ibn Abd al-Ghafir narrated to me, he said: Abu Said al-Khudri narrated to me, he said: We went out returning from Makkah when Ibn Sayyad caught up with me, saying: O Abu Said, the people have burned me, claiming that I am the Dajjal, while the Dajjal has no child born to him, and a child was born to me, and the Dajjal does not enter the two sanctuaries, and I entered them both; by Allah, I know his place. He said: So I did not doubt of him that it is he except then. So this speech of Ibn Sayyad was with us - and Allah knows best - possibly he said it due to standing upon what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had addressed of what Tamim al-Dari narrated to him of what we mentioned in what preceded of us in this chapter, of his informing them from Tamim, from his cousins, of his place which they saw him in, so he said on account of that what he said, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that, and Him we ask for guidance."
"3376","462 - Chapter explaining the difficulty scholars differed in concerning the Islam of young boys who have not reached puberty, with what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein of his asking Ibn Sayyad before his puberty: Do you testify that he is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 2948 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abdillah ibn Umar informed me that Abdillah ibn Umar informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group toward Ibn Sayyad, until he found him playing with boys, and Ibn Sayyad had nearly reached puberty that day, and he did not perceive until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck with his hand, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibn Sayyad, do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So Ibn Sayyad looked at him and said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejected him and said: I believe in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His messengers, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What do you see? Ibn Sayyad said: I am between truthful and liar. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The matter is confused upon you. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I have hidden for you something hidden. Ibn Sayyad said: It is smoke. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Be gone, you will not exceed your measure. So Umar said to him: Permit me concerning him, O Messenger of Allah, to strike his neck. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If it be he, you will not be given power over him, and if it be not he, there is no good for you in killing him."
"3377","2949 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahbullah ibn Rashid Abu Zurah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3378","2950 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim al-Zuhri informed us, he said: My uncle informed us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Salih, and he is Ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3379","2951 - And Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid ibn Abdillah ibn Jumay narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Ibn Sayyad while he was playing with boys, saying: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? And Ibn Sayyad says: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have hidden for you something hidden. He said: What is this? He said: Smoke. He said: Be gone, you will not exceed your measure. He said: So in these two hadiths the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tested Ibn Sayyad, who had not reached puberty, about his testimony to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of messengership from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and in that is what indicated that had he testified with it he would have deserved faith by his testimony with it, and were it not that it is so there would have been no meaning to his testing him about that, and in what we mentioned is what indicated that the Islam of the like of him among boys is Islam, and Allah we ask for guidance."
"3380","463 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the thirty liars who emerge after him, whether they are Dajjals or not. 2952 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab who said: Talhah ibn Abdillah ibn Awf narrated to me, from Iyad ibn Musafi, from Abu Bakrah brother of Ziyad by his mother who said: Abu Bakrah said: The people spoke much concerning Musaylimah the liar before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said anything concerning him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the people secondly praising Allah with what He is worthy of, then he said: To proceed, concerning this man about whom you spoke much, he is a liar among thirty liars emerging before the Dajjal, and there is no land but the terror of the Messiah the Dajjal enters it except Madinah; upon every pass of its passes that day are two angels repelling from it the terror of the Messiah. Abu Jafar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning Musaylimah that he is a liar among thirty liars emerging before the Dajjal, so it is possible these thirty liars among whom is Musaylimah are Dajjals, and possible they are liars and not Dajjals, so we looked into that."
"3381","2953 - So we found that Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Ararah narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: I read in the book of my father in his handwriting, and I did not hear it from him, from Qatadah, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Hammam, from Hudhayfah that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In my nation are lying Dajjals, twenty-seven, among them four women, and I am the seal of prophets, there is no prophet after me."
"3382","2954 - And we found that Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh al-Maafiri narrated to me, he said: I heard Sharahil ibn Yazid al-Maafiri saying: Muslim ibn Yasar narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be in the end of time lying Dajjals coming with hadiths you never heard, you nor your fathers, so beware of them, lest they tempt you and misguide you."
"3383","2955 - And we found that Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Thalabah ibn Abbad al-Abdi who said: Samurah ibn Jundub addressed us, relating in his sermon from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The Hour will not stand until thirty lying Dajjals emerge, all of them lying upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the last of them the one-eyed Dajjal, wiped of the right eye, as if it is the eye of Abu Tihya."
"3384","2956 - And we found that Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in these hadiths was what therein of what we mentioned, so it is possible these thirty mentioned therein are the thirty mentioned in the hadith of Abu Bakrah, so both matters together have combined in them, and possible those in this hadith are upon lying Dajjals, and those in the hadith of Abu Bakrah are upon liars who are not Dajjals, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that. So a speaker said: They are one kind, and the liars were named Dajjals because in their lying by which they are known they are like the Dajjal in his lying by which he is known. So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: what he said of that is impossible with us - and Allah knows best - because the liars mentioned in the hadith in which they were mentioned, had they been as mentioned, no number encompassing them would have been mentioned for them, because those of the liars among people in the future, and those before them after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this statement, are more in number than thirty, and when that is negated it was in reality other than the one-eyed Dajjal, and this name - meaning the Dajjal - was not derived from anything, because had it been derived from what some people mentioned, that it was derived from dajl, which is speed in movement, it would have been necessary that every hastener in his movement be a Dajjal, and when it is false that it is so, and it was of other than names derived from anything, it was a kind having the number the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned, so it was possible what we mentioned of its possibility in what preceded of us in this book, and Allah we ask for guidance."
"3385","464 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning carrying the heads of slain killed as exemplary punishment from land to land, and from region to region, of permissibility, and what was reported from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, contradicting that. 2957 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah al-Basri narrated to me, he said: Al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Ashqar narrated to us, from Ibn Qabus, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the head of Marhab."
"3386","2958 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Mubarak al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ashath, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara who said: I met my uncle with him the banner, so I said to him: Where are you going? So he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to a man who married his father's wife after him, that I bring him his head."
"3387","2959 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Said al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3388","2960 - And Ubayd ibn Rijal and Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani narrated to us, they said: Muammal ibn Ihab narrated to us, he said: Damrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani narrated to me, from Abdillah ibn al-Daylami, from his father who said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the head of al-Aswad al-Ansi the liar, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, you knew who we are, so to whom are we? He said: To Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we examined these reports, and we found therein the coming of Ali to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the head of Mirhab, and he was one of his enemies, so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, preceded with it to him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that upon him. And we found therein the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the uncle of al-Bara to bring him the head of the one who married his father's wife after his father, from the place wherein he is. And we found therein the coming of al-Daylami and his companions to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the head of al-Ansi the liar, and their coming with it to him was only from Yemen that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand upon the victory of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, over him, and upon the sufficiency of Muslims of his affair, and the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had indicated something of this by His saying: The adulteress and the adulterer, flog each one of them a hundred lashes - to His saying - and let a group of believers witness their punishment, and by His saying in the verse of warriors: That they be killed or crucified. And that was with us - and Allah knows best - that the infliction of the exemplary punishment of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, upon them be publicized among people, so like that was the display of the heads of those killed upon what was done upon them, the carried heads in the reports we narrated concerning that, that people stand upon the exemplary punishment that descended upon them."
"3389","So if a speaker says: There was reported from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, what contradicts this. And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, that Ulayy ibn Rabah narrated to him, that Uqbah ibn Amir said: I came to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, with the first conquest from Syria, and with heads, so he said: What would I do with these for anything."
"3390","Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ulayy ibn Rabah, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that Amr ibn al-As and Shurahbil ibn Hasanah sent him to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, with the head of Yannaq by the road of Syria, then when he came to him Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, denied that, saying to him Uqbah: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they do that to us. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Is it following Persia and Rome? Do not carry to me a head; the letter and the news suffice."
"3391","Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, from Said ibn Yazid, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So this Abu Bakr denied carrying heads to him, so our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: that Abu Bakr, though he denied that, yet its carriers were Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, and Amr ibn al-As, and Uqbah ibn Amir, in presence of those with them of his commanders over troops, among them Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, and others besides him of those who went out to raid Syria of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they did not deny that upon them, nor oppose them upon it, so that indicated their following them upon it, and when that was so, and they were trusted upon what they did, jurists in the religion of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, then what they did of that was permissible for what they saw therein of honoring the religion of Allah and the overcoming thereby of its people over disbelievers, and what was from Abu Bakr therein of his dislike of it may be for a meaning he stood upon therein preventing from that act, and his opinion, may Allah be pleased with him, was with it guidance, and the like of this thereafter returns to the opinion of imams in whose time the like of this occurs, so they do therein what they see as correct, and what they see of Muslims' need to it, and of their sufficiency from it, and there was from Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr concerning the head of al-Mukhtar when it was carried to him, leaving of denial in that, and with him were remnants of Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who were therein upon the like of what they were upon."
"3392","As Yunus and Bahr together narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shimr ibn Atiyah, from Hilal ibn Yasaf who said: The courier who came with the head of al-Mukhtar to Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, he said: So when I placed it before him he said: Kab did not narrate to me a hadith but I found it as he narrated to me except this, for he narrated to me that a man of Thaqif kills me, and here he is, I killed him. Al-Amash said: And he does not know that Abu Muhammad, meaning al-Hajjaj, lies in wait for him on the road, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for guidance."
"3393","465 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what decides between differing people of knowledge concerning what is obligatory upon the slanderer of a group, whether it is one hadd or a hadd for each one of them. 2961 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Makhlad ibn Husayn, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that Hilal ibn Umayyah slandered Sharik ibn Sahma with his wife, so he raised that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Bring four witnesses, otherwise a hadd upon your back. So he said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah knows I am truthful. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying to him: Four, otherwise a hadd upon your back. He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, Allah knows I am truthful, and Allah will send down upon you what clears my back of the hadd. He said: So the verse of lian descended."
"3394","2962 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bundar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, he said: Hisham informed us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas that Hilal ibn Umayyah accused his wife with Sharik ibn Sahma before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Proof, or a hadd upon your back. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, when one of us finds a man with his wife, he seeks proof! He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Proof, otherwise a hadd upon your back. So Hilal said: By the One who sent you with truth, I am truthful, and there will descend concerning my affair what clears my back of flogging, so the verse of lian descended. So in these two hadiths from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying to Hilal ibn Umayyah when he accused his wife with Sharik ibn Sahma, an accusation by which he became a slanderer of her and of Sharik ibn Sahma: Proof, otherwise a hadd upon your back, or bring four witnesses, otherwise a hadd upon your back, when the ruling at that time upon the husband when he accused his wife with a man, becoming thereby a slanderer of them both, was to bring what he was commanded to bring in each of these two hadiths, otherwise a hadd upon his back. So in that is what indicated that what became obligatory upon him in his slandering them both together was one hadd, as Abu Hanifah and Malik and their companions say concerning that, not as those besides them say concerning that among those who say: upon him for each one of them a hadd, and this agrees with what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also in the slander of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and the slander of those who cast her with it, that he hadd each one of them for that one hadd, not two hadds."
"3395","2963 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Abd al-Ala al-Sami narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, from Amrah the daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: When the Quran descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he went out and sat upon the pulpit, and recited to people what Allah sent down: Those who brought the slander are a group of you, do not think it evil for you, rather it is good for you - to His saying - a great punishment. She said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and commanded concerning two men and a woman, so they were struck their hadd eighty eighty, and they are those who undertook the bulk of that and spoke with indecency: Hassan and Mistah and Hamnah."
"3396","Abu Jafar said: And among those who went to this statement besides what we mentioned of people of knowledge was Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father that he said concerning a man who slandered a group: There is nothing upon him but one hadd, and we do not know from anyone of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor of their followers in this meaning any disagreement with this statement, and Allah, Glorified and Exalted, we ask for guidance."
"3397","466 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning fasting the day of Arafah, of urging upon it and of prohibition from it. 2964 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, and Fahd ibn Sulayman and Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Hawwar narrated to us, they said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, (h) and Bakr ibn Idris and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, they said: Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir - and Bakr and Salih said in their hadith - he said: I heard my father narrating from Uqbah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The days of sacrifice, and the days of tashriq, and the day of Arafah are our Eid, we people of Islam, days of eating and drinking. So in this hadith was the entrance by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the day of Arafah into the days of Muslim Eids, and his informing them that it is a day of food and drink as he informed them concerning the rest that they are days of food and drink. So we examined that and found the rest of the days mentioned in this hadith besides the day of Arafah specified with a meaning approximated to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, therewith, of prayer, and of sacrifice, and of takbir following the obligatory prayers prayed therein, so they were thereby Eids for Muslims, and fasting them was not permitted for that. And we found the day of Arafah therein also a cause of what is approximated with to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, not in other days, and it is standing at Arafah for Hajj, and that was of what is not in other lands besides Arafah, and what the days mentioned in the hadith of Uqbah besides it were specified with, its ruling is equal in all lands, so we understood thereby that they are Eids in all lands, so fasting them was not proper, and the standing at Arafah is only at Arafah, so it was possible the prohibition of its fast is only at Arafah, not elsewhere, and we sought what strengthens that."
"3398","2965 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. 2966 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Hawshab ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Mahdi al-Hajari, from Ikrimah who said: We were with Abu Hurayrah in his house, and he narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited fasting the day of Arafah at Arafah. So this strengthened what we mentioned, and since the day of Arafah is not an Eid in what is besides Arafah, its fast in what is besides Arafah was unrestricted, and whoever fasted it in what is besides Arafah was among those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised reward upon its fast mentioned in the hadith of Abu Qatadah."
"3399","2967 - Which Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrating from Abdillah ibn Mabad, from Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fasting the day of Arafah, saying: It expiates the past year and the remaining."
"3400","2968 - And which Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrating from Abdillah ibn Mabad al-Zimmani, from Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I expect from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in fasting the day of Arafah that it expiate the year before it and the year after it. So if a speaker says: We saw one who fasted the day of Arafah at Arafah for an obligation upon him, his fast sufficed him from it, while one who fasted a day of those other days for an obligation upon him, his fast does not suffice him from it, so how did their rulings differ while they are combined with one meaning in one hadith? So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: that things may be combined in one thing, while their rulings in themselves differ, of that is the saying of Allah: No sexual intercourse, nor wickedness, nor quarreling in Hajj, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, combined these things in one verse, prohibiting them with one prohibition, while they differed in rulings of what He prohibited therein, because intercourse is copulation, and it invalidates Hajj, while other than intercourse of wickedness and quarreling does not invalidate Hajj, so like that is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined of prohibition of fasting of the days mentioned in the hadith of Uqbah all of them with one prohibition, differing between their rulings in what I mentioned, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for guidance."
"3401","467 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning fasting the first ten of Dhu al-Hijjah, of what indicates his leaving it and of urging from him upon it. 2969 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash (h), and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishkib al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, then they joined saying: from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasting in the ten ever. So a speaker said: How do you accept this, while you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the virtue of deed in these days what you narrate from him therein."
"3402","2970 - So he mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn Zayd al-Warraq narrated to us, he said: Al-Qasim ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Said ibn Jubayr that he used to narrate from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no deed purer with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, nor greater in station than good done in the ten of sacrifice. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, nor one who strove in the path of Allah with himself and his wealth? He said: Nor one who strove in the path of Allah with himself and his wealth, except one who did not return with himself and his wealth."
"3403","2971 - And of what Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Masud ibn Sad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There are no days better with Allah, Exalted, nor dearer to Him for deed therein than these days, the days of the ten, so increase therein of tahmid and tahlil and takbir."
"3404","2972 - And of what Muhammad ibn Sulayman also narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhajir narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Babah, from Abdillah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and deeds were mentioned, saying: There are no days better for deed therein than this ten. They said: O Messenger of Allah, nor jihad? So he deemed it great and said: Nor jihad, except that a man go out with himself and his wealth in the path of Allah, then his soul's lifeblood be therein."
"3405","2973 - And of what Muhammad also narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Marzuq, meaning Ibn Mirdanah, narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no days better with Allah than the days of the ten. They said: Nor the like of them in the path of Allah? He said: Except one who powders his face in dust. He said: So how can it be that for deed in these days is of virtue what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned therein, then he stays back from fasting therein, while it is of the best deeds? So our answer to him concerning that was: by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that it is permissible he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not fast therein upon what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, because when he fasted he weakened from doing therein what is greater in station than fasting, and better than it, of prayer, and of remembrance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and recitation of Quran, as was reported from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning that of what he chose for himself."
"3406","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid that Abdillah would hardly fast, then when he fasted, he fasted three days of every month, saying: When I fast I weaken from prayer, and prayer is dearer to me than fasting. So what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, mentioned of him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of leaving fasting in those days, was to occupy himself therein with what is better than it, though fasting therein has of virtue what it has of what was mentioned in these reports we mentioned therein, and that does not prevent anyone from inclining to fasting therein, especially one able to combine fasting with other of deeds approximated with to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, besides it, and Allah we ask for guidance."
"3407","468 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Every deed of the son of Adam is for him except fasting, for it is Mine, and I reward for it, meaning for Allah, Blessed and Exalted. 2974 - Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn Shihab who said: Said ibn al-Musayyab informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every deed of the son of Adam is for him except fasting, it is Mine, and I reward for it - as if he narrates it from Allah, Mighty and Majestic - and by the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, the breath of the fasting one's mouth is better with Allah than the scent of musk."
"3408","2975 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah says: Fasting is Mine, and I reward for it; he leaves food and drink for My sake, and his desire for Me, and fasting is Mine, and I reward for it, and the breath of the fasting one's mouth is better with Allah than the scent of musk. So a speaker said: Do you count fasting among deeds? So our answer to him concerning that was: that a people of the people of language say: Fasting is not a deed, because it is only leaving things for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, rewards its leaver upon his leaving them for Him what He rewards him upon that, as He rewards possessors of praiseworthy deeds what He rewards them thereupon, and what he said of that is possible, and one went to that this fast, since it was not a deed, was not of the deed mentioned in the days of the ten upon what is in the reports we mentioned therein in the chapter before this chapter. And he went to that the deed mentioned therein is deed of prayer, and of remembrance, and of what resembles that, and that fasting is not entering into what was intended thereby therein, since it is not a deed. And what he said of that is possible for what he said. So a speaker said: In the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which you mentioned in this chapter is what indicates fasting is a deed among deeds, because therein: Every deed of the son of Adam, for it is for him except fasting, so fasting was excepted from deeds, so that indicated it is of them. So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: that what is in this hadith of his saying: Except fasting, for it is Mine, is not upon exception, but it is with meaning: But fasting, for it is Mine."
"3409","469 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning cutting lote-trees, of prohibition and of permissibility. 2976 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Malih ibn Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sharik narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn Aws, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those who cut - as if meaning lote-trees - are poured into Fire upon their heads pouring."
"3410","2977 - Al-Qasim ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Basri Abu Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Ala al-Sanani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq ibn Hammam narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Yazid - meaning al-Khuzi - from Amr ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn Aws, who said: I met an old man of Thaqif whose lote-trees had spoiled his crops, so I said: Will you not cut it, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except of crops. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever cuts lote-trees except of crops, Allah pours punishment upon him pouring, so I dislike cutting it of crops and of other than it. So in the first of these two hadiths is what prevents cutting all lote-trees, and in the second of them is exception of what was thereof in crops."
"3411","So we examined these two hadiths, and what they are upon of soundness in their chains, and of other than that, and we found that Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah Hammad ibn Usamah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and he did not pass him with it, he said: Whoever cuts a lote-tree, Allah pours punishment upon him pouring. So in this hadith is its stopping upon Urwah without passing him with it to Aishah nor to other than her of those mentioned in the first two hadiths, and therein also something Rawh mentioned to us, he said: I heard Hamid saying: I mentioned this hadith to Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, saying: I went to Amr ibn Dinar and asked him about it, saying to me: Go to Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, for he narrates with it. So I went to Uthman and he narrated to me therein two hadiths whose chains confused upon me. Sufyan said: So I asked Hisham ibn Urwah about cutting lote-trees, saying: These doors are of a lote-tree belonging to my father, which he cut and made of it these doors. So in what we mentioned from Sufyan in this hadith, of his asking Amr ibn Dinar about the hadith we mentioned in this chapter from him, meaning Amr ibn Dinar, and his answer therein with what he answered him, then what we mentioned therein from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, indicated that if the first two hadiths were sound, abrogation had joined them returning what was therein of prohibition to permissibility for what is in that prohibition, because Urwah, with his justice and knowledge and eminence of his station in knowledge, does not leave a thing established with him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to its opposite except for what necessitates that for him, so by what we mentioned is established the abrogation of these two hadiths, with what entered."
"3412","Besides that this Ibrahim was confused in this hadith, relating it once thus from Amr ibn Dinar, and relating it another time from Amr ibn Aws, and of what was reported from Urwah also in permissibility of cutting lote-trees: what Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Harb al-Tai narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Dawud al-Hamdani - Muhammad said: meaning al-Khuraybi - narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father that he used to cut lote-trees making them doors, and among those who opposed Ibrahim ibn Yazid in his hadith which we narrated from him in this chapter: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi, relating it from Amr ibn Dinar, as."
"3413","Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man of Thaqif who heard Ibn al-Zubayr saying: Whoever cuts lote-trees, Allah pours punishment upon him pouring. So this Muhammad ibn Muslim opposed Ibrahim in this hadith, returning it to Ibn al-Zubayr, and he is above this Ibrahim, and below Ibn Jurayj; as for the hadith of Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman which Sufyan mentioned."
"3414","2978 - So it is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from Muhammad ibn Said, from Abdillah ibn Hubshi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cuts a lote-tree, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, pours punishment upon his head pouring."
"3415","2979 - And of what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from Said ibn Muhammad, from Abdillah ibn Hubshi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cuts a lote-tree, Allah directs his head into Fire. So Ibrahim and Abu Umayyah differed in the man they differed in of narrators of this hadith: Ibrahim said: he is Muhammad ibn Said, and Abu Umayyah said: he is Said ibn Muhammad, and in that was what necessitates confusion of its narrators, except that the correct therein is what Abu Umayyah narrated, due to agreement of other than Abu Asim therein upon what he narrated from Abu Asim."
"3416","2980 - As Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from Said ibn Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Abdillah ibn Hubshi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cuts a lote-tree, Allah directs his head into Fire, except that this man differed in his name is not of those famous for narrating hadith, and we did not find for him mention in other than this hadith, and the like of this does not stand with one whose way is this, then this hadith of his mentioned it from Abdillah ibn Hubshi, and it is far from hearts that he met him, because we did not find anything of the hadith of Abdillah ibn Hubshi except from one whose age is above the age of this man, and he is Ubayd ibn Umayr, and his hadith from him is in the best prayer that it is length of standing, and Sufyan al-Thawri also used to deny this hadith and command acting with its opposite. As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Said, asked about cutting lote-trees, saying: We heard therein a hadith, we do not know what is upon the one who brought it."
"3417","2981 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sulayman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Stand, O Ali, and announce to people: Allah cursed the cutter of lote-trees. And al-Hasan ibn Muhammad did not hear from Ali, nor was he born in his time, so in the weakening by Sufyan of it is what the like of it falls with, besides that all people of knowledge of jurists of cities upon whom fatwa revolves are upon permissibility of cutting it, and in that is what indicated that the more entitled therein is permissibility of cutting it, not prevention from it, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for guidance."
"3418","470 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Most of the people of Paradise are the simple-minded, and what enters into that. 2982 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Most of the people of Paradise are the simple-minded. So I mentioned this hadith to Ahmad ibn Abi Imran, saying: Its meaning is a correct meaning. And the simple-minded intended therein are those simple-minded of the prohibitions of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, not others of those with deficiency of intellect with simplicity, and of it is the hadith reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3419","2983 - So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn Mutarrif narrated to us, from Hassan ibn Atiyah, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Modesty and reticence are two branches of faith, and obscenity and eloquence are two branches of hypocrisy."
"3420","2984 - And of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mutarrif, meaning Abu Ghassan, narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: And of that is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: They have hearts they do not understand with, and they have ears they do not hear with, meaning they do not understand good with their hearts, nor hear it with their ears due to what overcame their hearts and their hearing, preventing them from that. And of it is what was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Your love of a thing blinds and deafens, and we shall bring it hereafter if Allah wills. And of it is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also."
"3421","2985 - As Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Umarah, and he is Ibn al-Qaqa, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Ask me. So they were awed to ask him, then a man came and sat at his knee, saying: O Messenger of Allah, what is Islam? He said: That you associate nothing with Allah, establish prayer, give zakah, and fast Ramadan. He said: You spoke truth. He said: What is faith? He said: That you believe in Allah and His angels and His Book and meeting Him and His messengers, and believe in resurrection, and believe in decree all of it. He said: You spoke truth. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what is excellence? He said: That you fear Allah, Mighty and Majestic, as if you see Him, then if you do not see Him, He sees you. He said: O Messenger of Allah, when is the Hour? He said: The asked is not more knowledgeable than the asker, and I shall narrate to you of its signs: When you see the woman giving birth to her mistress, that is of its signs, and when you see the barefoot, naked, dumb, deaf as kings of earth, that is of its signs, and when you see shepherds of sheep competing in buildings, that is of its signs, in five of the unseen none knows them but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, then he recited this verse: Allah, with Him is knowledge of the Hour, to the end of the surah, then the man stood, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Return him to me. So they sought him and did not find him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is Jibril, peace be upon him. Abu Zurah said: Since they did not ask him. So in this hadith of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the signs of the Hour: And when you see the barefoot, naked, dumb, deaf as kings of earth, that is of its signs, he does not mean thereby the commonly known dumb, nor the commonly known deaf, but he means by dumb of praiseworthy speech, and means by deaf, deaf of praiseworthy speech, and the like of this is in the Quran in other than one place, of it what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what this is its meaning with people of knowledge."
"3422","2986 - And it is what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not stand until the year is like the month, the month like the week, the week like the day, the day like the hour, and the hour like the burning of palm fronds. So its meaning with people of knowledge is: that their understandings with which these things are understood, and their measures stood upon, are occupied with what overcame them of what they do not know the measures of those things, so they see thereby that they decreased from what they were upon before occurrence of these things with their understandings, while the matter therein is not so, but they are in their state in their measures upon what they knew them with before, and what changed them with them and decreased their measures in their suppositions was occupation of their understandings with other than them until they supposed what they supposed of what the matter in reality is in its state, and upon what it was upon before that."
"3423","471 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning bid, what it is. 2987 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Amrah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Muslims loved that Rome overcome Persia, because they are people of a Book, and the polytheists loved that Persia overcome Rome, because they are people of idols, so the Muslims mentioned that to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, so Abu Bakr mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: They shall be defeated. So Abu Bakr mentioned that to them, so they said: Set between us and you a term; if they overcome, you shall have such and such, and if we overcome, you shall have such and such. So he set between them a term of five years, but they did not overcome. So Abu Bakr mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Why did you not set it below bid? He said: Below ten. He said: And Said ibn Jubayr said: Bid is what is below ten. He said: So Rome overcame after that. He said: So that is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Alif Lam Mim. Rome was defeated in the nearest land, and they after their defeat shall overcome within bid years. He said: So Rome was defeated, then overcame after. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: To Allah belongs the command before and after, and that day the believers rejoice in the victory of Allah. Abu Ishaq said: Sufyan said: I heard they overcame them on the day of Badr. Abu Jafar said: And in the chain of this hadith is the omission of Sufyan between Abu Ishaq al-Fazari and Habib ibn Abi Amrah, so it may be from Abu Umayyah, and may be from me, except that."
"3424","2988 - And Ubayd ibn Rijal and Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shirazi narrated to us, they said: Al-Musayyab ibn Wadih narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Habib ibn Abi Amrah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, then they mentioned the like of it. So we verified thereby the entrance of Sufyan in the chain of this hadith between Abu Ishaq and Habib ibn Abi Amrah."
"3425","2989 - And we found that Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from al-Zuhri who said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah informed me from a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When Alif Lam Mim. Rome was defeated was sent down, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, met men of the polytheists, saying to them: The people of the Book shall overcome Persia. They said: In how many? He said: In bid years. He said: Then they wagered between them a wager, and that was before gambling was prohibited to them. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came and informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What is below ten is of bid. So the overcoming of Persia over Rome was for seven years, then Allah made Rome overcome Persia at the time of al-Hudaybiyah, so the Muslims rejoiced at the overcoming of the people of the Book, and the overcoming of the Muslims over the polytheists was after al-Hudaybiyah. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith of the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, that what is below ten is of bid, we understood thereby that the end of bid is below ten, and we needed to stand upon the measure of the least of bid, what it is."
"3426","2990 - So we found that Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Man ibn Isa narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When Alif Lam Mim. Rome was defeated was sent down, Abu Bakr wagered Quraysh, then mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not take precaution, for bid is what is between three and nine."
"3427","2991 - And we found that Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Hilal al-Basri informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid, meaning Ibn Uthmah, said: Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to us, he said: Al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr in his wagering concerning Alif Lam Mim. Rome was defeated: Why did you not take precaution, O Abu Bakr, for bid is what is between three and nine."
"3428","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman Luwayn narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Urwah, from Niyar ibn Mukram, and he had companionship, who said: When Alif Lam Mim. Rome was defeated was sent down, Abu Bakr went out with it to the polytheists, saying to them: This is the speech of your companion. He said: Allah sent this down. He said: And Persia had overcome Rome, taking them as near slaves, and the polytheists disliked that Rome overcome Persia, because they are people of denial and disbelief in resurrection, and the Muslims loved that Rome overcome Persia, because they are people of a Book and belief in resurrection. So they said to Abu Bakr: We pledge you that Rome shall not overcome Persia. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Bid is what is between three and nine. So they said: The middle of that is six, no less, no more. So they placed the wager - and that was before wagering was prohibited - so Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, turned to his companions and informed them the news. So they said: Evil is what you did; why did you not affirm it upon what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said? Had Allah willed to say six He would have said. So when the year of six was, Rome did not overcome Persia, so they took the wager. Then when the year of seven was, Rome overcame Persia. So that is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And that day the believers rejoice in the victory of Allah. Abu Jafar said: So in the first of these two hadiths of the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that bid is what is between three and nine, and in the second of them of the speech of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: Bid is what is between three and nine, we understood thereby that bid is from three, no less than it, to nine, no more than it, and we did not."
"3429","472 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what one who does not own of cattle slaughters without its owner's permission: is that a valid slaughter making its eating lawful or not? 2992 - Ali ibn Shaybah and Yazid ibn Sinan together narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a slave-girl of the family of Kab ibn Malik used to pasture sheep of theirs, so she feared for a sheep of them it would die, so she took a stone and slaughtered it with it. So that was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he commanded them to eat it."
"3430","2993 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a slave-girl of Kab ibn Malik used to pasture sheep of theirs, then a sheep of them was about to die, so she slaughtered it with a flint. So Kab asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, so he commanded him to eat it. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is the unrestricted permission by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Kab ibn Malik to eat his sheep which a slave-girl slaughtered without his command. So in that is what indicated that the ruling in what a man slaughters of cattle without its owner's permission is that it is a valid slaughter for it. So a speaker said: This is a hadith confused in chain; none narrated it as you mentioned from Nafi except Yahya ibn Said and Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah. As for others besides them of Nafi's narrators, they narrated it from Nafi with other than this chain, of chains the like of which proof does not stand with."
"3431","2994 - And he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub and Qatadah and Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi that Kab ibn Malik asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a slave-woman who slaughtered a sheep with a flint, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to eat it."
"3432","2995 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, from a man of the Ansar, from Muadh ibn Sad, or from Sad ibn Muadh, that he informed him that a slave-girl of Kab ibn Malik used to pasture sheep of his at Sal, then a sheep of them was stricken, so she reached it and slaughtered it with a stone. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about that, saying: There is no harm in it, so eat it."
"3433","2996 - And of what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Nafi who said: I heard a man of the Ansar narrating to Ibn Umar that a slave-woman of Kab ibn Malik used to pasture sheep of his at Sal, then a sheep of them was exposed, so she feared upon it it would die, so she slaughtered it with a flint. So she brought it to its people, then Kab asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, saying: Eat it."
"3434","2997 - And of what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed us, from Nafi that he heard a man of the Ansar informing Ibn Umar from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that a slave-girl of Kab ibn Malik, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3435","2998 - And of what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi who said: I heard a man of the Ansar narrating to Ibn Umar that a slave-girl of the family of Kab ibn Malik, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3436","2999 - And of what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Nafi narrated to me that he heard a man of the Ansar informing Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"3437","3000 - And of what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So what we narrated returned to eight narrating it from Nafi upon what we mentioned from them in these chains the like of which proof does not stand with, opposing Yahya ibn Said and Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah in what they narrated from Nafi upon it, and eight are more entitled to memory than two. This speaker said: Do we find in this sunnah a basis from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with an accepted chain necessitating what you go to in this meaning, of lawfulness of this slaughtered without its owner's command? Otherwise we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what prevents eating its like."
"3438","3001 - So he mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Thalabah ibn al-Hakam who said: The people obtained sheep in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and plundered them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Plunder is not proper, and he commanded concerning the pots, so they were overturned."
"3439","3002 - And of what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: My father and others narrated to me, from Simak ibn Harb, from Thalabah ibn al-Hakam who said: We obtained sheep on the day of Khaybar and plundered them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while their pots were boiling. So they said: It is plunder. So he said: Overturn the pots and what is in them, for plunder is not lawful."
"3440","3003 - And of what Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Dhuhli narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Zakariya ibn Adi narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his father who said: I witnessed the conquest of Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then when we defeated them we fell upon their baggage, taking what was therein of guarded goods, then no sooner did the pots boil than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded concerning the pots, so they were overturned. He said: So in these reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded overturning the pots with the meat therein of sheep since they were plunder. So in that is what indicated that what is slaughtered upon the like of this condition is not valid slaughter, nor lawful to eat. So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, was: that the reports we began mentioning in the first section of this chapter entered their chains of confusion what we mentioned therein, and that the reports we mentioned in the second section of it, of the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to overturn the pots with the meat therein of sheep since they were plunder, then it may be that was not because it was unlawful by plunder, but was a punishment for the plunderers, because that was in a time when punishments upon sins would be in the wealth of sinners, as was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the withholder of zakah: Whoever gives it seeking reward, his reward is for him, and whoever does not, we shall take it and half his wealth, a resolve of the resolves of our Lord, Mighty and Majestic; the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has nothing of it. And we shall mention that hereafter in this book of ours."
"3441","3004 - And it is what Yunus narrated to us, from the Muwatta of Abdillah ibn Wahb, after he narrated to us therein from Ibn Wahb, from Malik, with the hadith of Nafi, from a man of the Ansar, who informed concerning a slave-girl with what he informed, and of the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the family of Kab to eat it, and his informing them there is no harm in it. So he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah, meaning Ibn Zayd al-Laythi, narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, and he saw no harm in it. Abu Jafar said: So this is a hadith sound in chain, therein the unrestricted permission by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the family of Kab to eat this sheep, though it was slaughtered without his command. Abu Jafar said: And in the chapter also is another hadith, of the hadith of Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, from his father, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the sheep slaughtered without its owner's command and roasted, and the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to feed it to captives. And we shall mention that hereafter in this book of ours, if Allah wills. Abu Jafar said: So in these two hadiths is what indicated the unrestricted permission to eat the meat of the like of this, though it was slaughtered without its owner's command, with the saying of jurists of cities all with what agreed with what is in these two hadiths, opposing what this speaker said, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for guidance."
"3442","473 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what decides between differing jurists concerning the usurped sheep when slaughtered and roasted: does the usurped one have to take it while it is so or not? 3005 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi narrated to us, from his father - he said: I thought him of the Ansar - Abu Jafar said: There fell in my book from a man of the Ansar whose name I do not know, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at a funeral, then the messenger of a Qurayshi woman met him inviting him to food. So we sat the sitting of youths from their fathers. Then our fathers perceived the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in his hand a morsel. So he said: This sheep informs me it was taken without its lawfulness. So the woman stood, saying: O Messenger of Allah, it never ceased to please me you eat in my house, and I sent to al-Naqi, but no sheep was found therein, and my brother had bought a sheep yesterday, so I sent with it to its people with the price. So he said: Feed it to captives."
"3443","3006 - And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from a man of the Ansar who said: We went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the funeral of a man of the Ansar, then he mentioned this story itself in more speech than this speech. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded feeding the sheep to captives, and they are of those upon whom charity with its like is permissible, and he did not command withholding it for the one slaughtered while upon his ownership to take it while it is so. And in that is what indicated the removal of his ownership from it, and the occurrence of ownership of one who introduced therein what he introduced, of slaughter and roasting, upon it, as those say who say it of people of knowledge, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions, and with Allah is guidance."
"3444","474 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what a people attached to, that the slave has no divorce. 3007 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Umar ibn Muattib that Abu Hasan, freedman of Banu Nawfal, informed him that he sought the verdict of Ibn Abbas concerning an owned man under whom was an owned woman, then he divorced her two divorces, so she separated from him. Then they were freed after that. Is it proper for the man to propose to her? So Ibn Abbas said: Yes, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed with that, Ibn Abbas claimed. Abu Jafar said: So we examined this hadith in its chain to know whether this Abu al-Hasan upon whom it revolves is of those the like of this hadith is taken from the like of him."
"3445","So we found that Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Abu Hasan, freedman of Abdillah ibn Nawfal ibn Abd al-Muttalib, narrated to me, and he was of the most pleasing freedmen of Quraysh, and people of knowledge and righteousness of them, that he heard a woman of Abdillah seeking his verdict concerning a boy of hers, son of adultery, in a neck incumbent upon her. So Abdillah ibn Nawfal said to her: I do not see the freeing of a son of adultery discharging from you the neck upon you. Ibn Shihab said: And Abdillah ibn Nawfal informed me, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab: That I carry upon two mules in the path of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is dearer to me than that I free a son of adultery. And Abdillah ibn Nawfal was of the righteous Muslims, and of possessors of their knowledge, and Marwan ibn al-Hakam set him upon judiciary in his governorship. So we established thereby that this Abu al-Hasan is of those the like of this is taken from him. Then we sought whether Umar ibn Muattib has a condition necessitating for him the like of that, but we did not find it for him. So he returned of those proof is not made in the like of this with him. Then we examined the text of this hadith, finding it impossible, because the divorce by that owned one of his wife, the two divorces he divorced her in the state of his slavery and hers, is not free of one of two aspects: that it be operative, so its ruling be the ruling of prohibition of her until she marry another husband, since two divorces prohibit her upon him likewise; or that it be inoperative, because the divorce of the owned is nothing, upon what Abdillah ibn Abbas used to go to in the divorce of owned ones."
"3446","As Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah, from Busr ibn Said, from Abdillah ibn Abbas concerning a slave whom his master marries, then he divorces her, that it is not permitted except with his master's permission. And Abdillah ibn Abbas recited: And Allah struck a similitude: an owned slave able upon nothing. So I went to Abdillah ibn Umar and asked him. So he said: She is not lawful to him until she marry another husband."
"3447","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur - meaning Ibn ZadHan - narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The matter is to the master, whether he permitted him or did not permit him. And he recites this verse: And Allah struck a similitude: an owned slave able upon nothing."
"3448","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed us, from Abu Mabad, freedman of Ibn Abbas, that a boy of Ibn Abbas divorced his wife two divorces. So Ibn Abbas said to him: Return her, may you have no mother, for you have nothing of the matter. But he refused, saying: She is yours, so take her. Abu Jafar said: So if it were so, there would be no meaning to his taking her back, because she is his wife upon her state; that divorce did not prohibit her upon him. And in what we mentioned is what indicated the corruption of this hadith in its chain and in its text, and that it is of what acceptance upon Abdillah ibn Abbas is not obligatory, nor is it heeded."
"3449","3008 - And we found that Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Umar ibn Muattib, from al-Hasan, freedman of Banu Nawfal, thus he said, from Ibn Abbas concerning a slave who divorced his wife twice, then he freed her, may he marry her? He said: Yes. It was said: From whom? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave verdict with that."
"3450","3009 - And we found that Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Shayban al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Umar ibn Muattib, thus he said: The freedman of Banu Nawfal informed him that he sought the verdict of Ibn Abbas that he sought his verdict concerning an owned one under whom was an owned woman, then he divorced her one divorce, so she separated from him. Then they were freed after that. Is it proper for the man to propose to her? Ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed in that, and he did not add upon this anything. Abu Jafar said: So we wrote it, because therein he divorced her one divorce, to establish thereby the confusion of this hadith, and that it is not permissible to argue with it if it is so. Then we returned to what was reported in the divorce of the slave from other than Ibn Abbas of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3451","So we found that Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman - meaning freedman of the family of Talhah - from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abdillah ibn Utbah, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The slave marries two, and divorces two, and the slave-woman waits two menses. Then if she does not menstruate, a month and a half."
"3452","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Nufay - a mukatab of Umm Salamah - divorced a free woman twice, so he sought the verdict of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: She is prohibited to you."
"3453","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, from Sulayman ibn Yasar that a mukatab belonging to Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or a slave, under whom was a free woman, divorced her twice, then wanted to take her back, so the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to go to Uthman ibn Affan and ask him about that. So he went to him and met him at the stairs holding the hand of Zayd ibn Thabit, so he asked them both, and they both hastened to him saying: She is prohibited to you, she is prohibited to you."
"3454","And we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Uthman ibn Affan, then he mentioned the like of it. Yunus said: Ibn Shihab said: And Abu Salamah informed me from Uthman the like of it."
"3455","And we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Abu al-Zinad, from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Nufay, mukatab of Umm Salamah, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yunus, from Ibn Wahb, from Malik, from Abu al-Zinad, which we mentioned concerning that."
"3456","And we found that Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The sunnah is with women in divorce and waiting period. He said: So in what we narrated of these reports from Umar, Uthman, Ali, and Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them, was what contradicted what we narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, concerning that, and we found from Ibn Umar also therein what contradicts what we narrated from Ibn Abbas concerning that."
"3457","As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated to us, (h) and as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Marwan al-Uthmani narrated to us, and as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, they said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father who said: Whichever of them is enslaved, divorce is decreased by his slavery, and the waiting period after that is upon women. And what we narrated from Ibn Umar of this, we did not find for him upon it agreement from the Companions, nor from those after them. Then we examined the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Allah struck a similitude: an owned slave able upon nothing, whether his divorce is of those meanings he is not able upon or not? So we found the marrying by his master of him permits to him the private part of whom he married him to, being an owner of it, able upon it, apart from his master, and what he is not able upon is other than that of wealth which Allah entrusted to freemen apart from owned ones, not the private parts of women. So since the lawfulness of the private part was for him, not for his master, the prohibition of the private part was also for him, apart from his master. And we narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Madinans concerning that what we narrated from him in this chapter, and there was also narrated from him therein from the Kufans what agrees with that."
"3458","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ibn Awn who said: I heard Abu Salih saying: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying, asked about a man under whom was a slave-woman, then he divorced her twice, then he bought her, may he have intercourse with her? So he refused that. Then we returned to seeking the first of divorce which Umar and Ali set upon the ruling of divorced women, and Uthman and Zayd set upon the ruling of divorcing men. So we found the freeman permitted to marry four women, and set for him of divorce therein twelve divorces, and we found the owned one permitted to marry two, no more than them. So we understood thereby, since he was upon half of what the freeman is upon in their number, that he be in their divorce half of what the freeman is upon in that, so his divorce of them be six divorces. So by that is established what was reported from Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, therein. And I spoke to Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn al-Abbas in this chapter, taking upon him the saying of Uthman and Zayd therein, saying to him: Is divorce not found to be from the man, and the waiting period found to be from the woman? So it is intelligible therein that all of what is from each one of them is referred therein to his ruling. So he said to me: The Book of Allah repels what you said, because Allah said: O you who believe, when you marry believing women then divorce them before you touch them, you have no waiting period upon them you count. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, informed us that the waiting period is for men, not for women, and since it was for men, while it was upon the ruling of women, because it is of them, then the divorce which is of them in women is upon the ruling of women, not upon their ruling."
"3459","475 - Chapter explaining the difficulty of the ruling of safflower-dyed cloth: whether it is of perfume or not of perfume, in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3010 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi al-Basri narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah the daughter of Sirin, from Umm Atiyah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman does not mourn above three days except upon a husband, for she mourns upon him four months and ten, and she does not wear a safflower-dyed garment except a garment of asb (Yemeni striped cloth), nor apply kohl, nor touch perfume except small pieces of costus and nails. So in this hadith was what indicated that the mourning one does not wear a safflower-dyed garment, and in that was what indicated that safflower is of perfume. So a speaker said: She was not prohibited from that because it is of perfume, but she was prohibited from it because it is of adornment. So our answer to him concerning that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, was: that had she only been prohibited from it because it is of adornment, as mentioned, she would have been prohibited from the asb garment, because it is in adornment above the safflower-dyed garment, and in the permission of the asb garment to her in her mourning is what indicated that the prohibition of the safflower-dyed garment to her was not because it is adornment, but it is otherwise than that, and it is because it is dyed with perfume, and it is safflower. And in this is what strengthened the school of those who go in safflower that it is prohibited in ihram, and of those who used to go to that of people of knowledge was Abu Hanifah and his companions. And with Allah is guidance."
"3460","476 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the slain one whom Salamah ibn al-Akwa overtook until he killed him, apart from those present of people, not in the thick of war, and of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): His spoils all to him, meaning to Salamah. 3011 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, he said: My father Salamah ibn al-Akwa narrated to me, he said: We raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Hawazin, then while we were at the wide valley with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man came upon a red camel, so he made it kneel, then pulled a rope from its baggage, and tied the camel with it. Then he advanced and lunched with the people, looking at them, and among us was weakness and thinness of mounts, and some of us were walkers. So he went out hastening, came to his camel, released its tie, then made it kneel and sat upon it, and roused it, and the camel hastened with him. And a man followed him upon a gray she-camel, the head of the she-camel at the hip of the camel. Salamah said: So I drew the sword until I was at the hip of the camel, then I advanced until I took the camel's halter and made it kneel. Then when it placed its knees upon earth I drew my sword and struck the man's head, so it fell. So I came with the camel leading it, upon it its saddle and its weapon, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me saying: Who killed the man? He said: Ibn al-Akwa. He said: His spoils all to him."
"3461","3012 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Umays narrated to us, from Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa, from his father, who said: A spy of the polytheists came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was on a journey, so he sat speaking with his companions, then slipped away. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek him and kill him. So I preceded them to him and killed him, taking his spoils, so he gave them to me as extra share. Abu Jafar said: So in the first of these two hadiths is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Who killed the man? They said: Ibn al-Akwa. So he said: His spoils all to him. So this indicates that whoever kills a man of the enemy, entering the abode of Islam without safety, or captures him while he is so, shall have his spoils apart from those with him of people who did not kill him, as Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan say concerning the warrior when he enters the abode of Islam without safety, then a man of Muslims takes him, that he is his apart from them. Then once they said: Therein is the fifth, and once they said: There is no fifth therein. And they opposed Abu Hanifah in that, because he used to say: He is his and all Muslims, because with him he is spoils in the abode of Islam wherein he came, and of what indicated the correctness of what Abu Yusuf and Muhammad said in that is what there is no disagreement in, of what was established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in buried treasure found in the land of Islam, that had he taken it apart from the rest of Muslims except the fifth, then it is therein, because it is in the ruling of what was not spoils by the opening of the land wherein it was found. So its ruling was the ruling of what he spoiled and took when he found it, deserving it thereby apart from the rest of Muslims after the fifth therein."
"3462","477 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the wage of the hireling upon work: when its taking from its hirer upon him becomes obligatory. 3013 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Muhriz al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Aswad, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My nation was given five traits in Ramadan not given to anyone before them: The breath of the fasting one's mouth is better with Allah than the scent of musk, the angels seek forgiveness for them until they break fast, Allah adorns every day His Paradise saying: My righteous servants are about to cast from them burden and harm and come to you, the rebellious devils are chained therein, not reaching therein what they reach in other than it, and they are forgiven in the last night. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, is it the Night of Decree? He said: No, but the worker is only paid his wage at the completion of his work."
"3463","3014 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ammar the caller to prayer narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give the hireling his wage before his sweat dries."
"3464","3015 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three I am their opponent on the Day of Resurrection, and whom I oppose I defeat him: A man who gave by Me then betrayed, a man who sold a freeman and ate his price, and a man who hired a hireling, took in full from him, and did not pay him his wage. Abu Jafar said: And we mentioned in what preceded of us in this book of ours the hadith of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to stand upon his sacrificial camels, to give in charity their coverings and halters, saying: Do not give the butcher of them anything, and we give him from with us. He said: So in that was what indicated that he gives him his wage after his finishing his work, due to his saying: And do not give the butcher of them anything, and that after his finishing his work, and we give him at that from with us. And in what we narrated from Abu Hurayrah is what confirmed this meaning, disclosed it, and clarified to us that the hireling is only given his wage upon his work after his finishing his work. And Allah, Exalted, we ask for guidance."
"3465","478 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the food upon one invited to it its coming is obligatory. 3016 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Al-Zuhri informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman al-Araj informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worst food is the food of the wedding feast; the rich are invited to it and the poor are kept away, and whoever does not answer the invitation disobeyed Allah and His Messenger."
"3466","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to say: The worst food is the food of the wedding feast, the rich are invited to it and the poor are left, and whoever does not come to the invitation disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. Abu Jafar said: So Sufyan and Malik differed in this hadith: Sufyan narrated it all of it of the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Malik narrated it all of it of the speech of Abu Hurayrah, except what he mentioned therein concerning one who stays back from that, that he disobeyed Allah and His Messenger."
"3467","Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ata, who said: I heard Maymun ibn Maysarah, who said: Abu Hurayrah would be invited to food, going to it, and we would go with him, calling: The worst food is the food of the wedding feast, invited to it is one who refuses it, and prevented from it is one who comes to it. So Maymun ibn Maysarah agreed in what was reported of this hadith from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with Malik in what he narrated upon him from al-Zuhri, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah. And Ibn Uyaynah opposed in what he narrated upon him, from al-Zuhri, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah. Abu Jafar said: So we examined this hadith to stand upon its meaning intended thereby, Allah willing. So we found the food intended with what was mentioned to it therein is the wedding feast, and the wedding feast was a kind of foods, because in foods are other kinds besides it we shall mention in this chapter, Allah willing. And it is what I heard Ahmad ibn Abi Imran saying: The Arabs used to name the food a man feeds when a child is born to him: the food of khurs, and name the food of circumcision the food of idhar, saying: He was circumcised upon his child; and when a man builds a house or buys it and feeds, it is said: the food of wakirah, meaning of wakr. And when he comes from a journey and feeds, it is said: the food of naqiah. He said: And Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn Hatim, companion of al-Asmai, recited: He said: And qudar is the butcher, and quddam are comers, it is said: comer and comers as it is said: writer and writers. And the food of the mourning gathering is said to it: the food of hadimah. Ibn Abi Imran said to us: And al-Hasan ibn Amr al-Waili recited to me, of Umm Hakim the daughter of Abd al-Muttalib, to her father."
"3468","3017 - So we found that Abu Umayyah and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ruasi narrated to us, from his father, from Abd al-Karim ibn Salit, from Abu Buraydah, from his father, who said: When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, proposed to Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A wedding must have a feast. Sad said: Upon me a sheep, and so-and-so said: Upon me such and such of millet."
"3469","3018 - And we found that Ali ibn Shaybah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So in this hadith was the informing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that a wedding must have a feast."
"3470","3019 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a trace of yellowness upon me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you marry? So I said: Yes. So he said: Whom? I said: A woman of the Ansar. He said: How much did you give her? I said: The weight of a date-stone of gold, or a date-stone of gold. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Give a feast even with a sheep."
"3471","3020 - And we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a trace of yellowness upon him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him, and he informed him he married a woman of the Ansar. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How much did you give her? So he said: The weight of a date-stone of gold. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give a feast even with a sheep. He said: So in this hadith also was the command by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf when he married to give a feast."
"3472","3021 - And we found that Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abdillah ibn Uthman, from a one-eyed man of Thaqif called Zuhayr, Qatadah said: And he is said to be Maruf, Hammam said: meaning he praises him with good, Qatadah said: If his name is not Zuhayr ibn Uthman, I do not know what his name is. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The feast is a right, the second is kindness, and the third is showing off and repute. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith was the informing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the feast is a right, distinguishing between its ruling in the three days. So he made it in the first day praised upon it its people, because they did a right; made it in the second day kindness, because there may reach it in the second day who perhaps did not reach it in the first day, of those in whose reaching it is of reward for its people what is for them in that; and made it in the third day otherwise than that, because he made it showing off and repute. And it was known that one invited to the right, upon him to answer to it; one invited to kindness, for him to answer to it, not upon him to answer to it; and one invited to showing off and repute, upon him not to answer to it. And in that was what indicated that of foods invited to is what the invited one may not come to, and that of them is what the invited one must come to."
"3473","3022 - And Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, they said: Al-Layth informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanaj narrated to me, from Nafi that Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you invites his brother for a right, let him come to it, for a wedding invitation or its like."
"3474","3023 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to me, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So in this hadith was: When one of you invites his brother for a right, let him come. So the right was what was a right upon the inviter upon what we mentioned in the first, and what is in these two hadiths of Muhammad and Yazid of mention of that right, that it is for a wedding invitation or its like, may be of the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and may be of the speech of those after him of narrators of these two hadiths. And the hadith of Ibn Umar was narrated by a group from Nafi without mention of this meaning which is other than the wedding, among them Umar ibn Muhammad al-Umari."
"3475","3024 - As Yazid narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuayb, meaning Ibn Shabur, narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When you are invited, answer."
"3476","3025 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Answer the invitation when you are invited to it. And among them Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani."
"3477","3026 - As Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come to the invitation when you are invited. So it may be that invitation intended in these reports is the invitation mentioned in the first reports, so these reports agree and do not differ. So we looked whether anything was reported indicating it is that invitation as we mentioned."
"3478","3027 - So we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is invited to a wedding feast, let him come to it. So this hadith clarified that what must be come to of foods invited to in these hadiths of Ibn Umar is the wedding feast. And there was reported in this chapter from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, also, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3479","3028 - Of what Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is invited, let him answer; if he wills he eats, and if he wills he leaves."
"3480","3029 - And of what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3481","3030 - And what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, he heard Jabir saying: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When one of you invites his brother to food, let him accept it; then if he wishes he may eat, and if he wishes he may leave it. Abu Jafar said: So that could possibly mean the food mentioned in the first reports, not other than it. And there has been narrated from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this subject the like of this as well, and a precise statement not found in any other of these reports."
"3482","3031 - And it is what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Respond to the one who invites, do not refuse gifts, and do not beat people - or he said: Muslims; Abu Ghassan doubted. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the command to respond to the inviter, to accept the gift, and the prohibition of refusing it; so it is possible that this responding and this prohibited refusal are of one kind, and that what one is invited to is other than the wedding feast; and it is possible that each of the two is a kind other than the other kind, so that what one is invited to is the wedding feast whose attendance is obligatory, and the gift is unlike it. And there has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that as well."
"3483","3032 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is invited, let him accept; if he is not fasting, let him eat, and if he is fasting, let him pray. Hisham said: And prayer means supplication. And this hadith is like what we have narrated before it."
"3484","3033 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah, meaning al-Harrani, narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Ubaydullah ibn Talhah ibn Kurayz, from al-Hasan, he said: Uthman ibn Abi al-As was invited to a circumcision feast, and he refused to accept, and said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we would not attend circumcision feasts nor be invited to them. He said: That indicates that what they were invited to of foods in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which they would attend as obligatory upon them, was only a specific kind of foods, not all foods; and since the food of the wedding feast is commanded, whoever is invited to it is commanded to attend it; and since foods other than it are not commanded, one is not commanded to attend them."
"3485","3034 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum al-Maafiri informed us, from his father, that an anchorage at sea brought them together with Abu Ayyub al-Ansari; then when our lunch came, we sent to Abu Ayyub and to the people of his ship, and he said: You invited me while I am fasting, and it was due upon me to accept your invitation; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The Muslim owes his brother six qualities: when he invites him he should accept him, when he meets him he should greet him, when he sneezes he should say Yarhamukallah to him - or he thirsts and he gives him drink, Yunus doubted - and when he falls ill he should visit him, when he dies he should attend him, and when he seeks his advice he should advise him. Then someone said: In this hadith, from the words of Abu Ayyub there is what indicates that the invitation which it is the Muslim's right upon his brother to accept is like what he was invited to and accepted. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: it is possible that it is as he mentioned, and that the better course for people when invited to the like of it is not to stay away from it, and that the attendance of some of them drops what is upon others of it, and it is among the things borne by the community on behalf of the individuals, like attending funerals and burying the dead. And it is possible that it concerns what people ought to be upon in their travels with their brothers, of increase in keeping close ties, openness toward them, and generosity to them more than they are to them otherwise than in travel; so what Abu Ayyub did was for that. And what he did he did not report from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he only reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than that of what is in this hadith. And it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by the responding to invitation in this hadith the wedding feast we mentioned, not other than it."
"3486","3035 - Yunus and Sulayman ibn Shuayb together narrated to us, both said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us - so said Sulayman, and Yunus said: Bishr ibn Bakr informed us - he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The right of the Muslim upon his Muslim brother is five: he greets him when he meets him, he says Yarhamukallah to him when he sneezes, he accepts him when he invites him, he visits him when he is ill, and he attends his funeral when he dies. So it is also possible that the obligatory right in accepting invitation means the invitation that is a wedding feast, not other than it; nothing has become clear to us in anything we narrated of obligatory attendance to invited food except the food of the wedding feast which is weddings; and Allah, glory be to Him, we ask for success."
"3487","479 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on fine clothing and on coarse clothing. 3036 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar, from Abdullah ibn Thalabah, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik said to me: I heard your father narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he heard him saying: Shabbiness is part of faith, meaning austerity. Then someone said: You have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts what is in this hadith."
"3488","3037 - He mentioned what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Amr Muhammad ibn Amr al-Tannuri narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Fadalah, from Abu Raja al-Utaridi, he said: Imran ibn Husayn came out to us wearing a cloak of khazz I had never seen on him before nor after, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah bestows a blessing upon His servant, He loves to see the trace of His blessing upon him. Abu Jafar said: And Fudayl ibn Fadalah is a man from Qays; so claimed al-Bukhari."
"3489","3038 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from his father, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was disheveled and dusty, and he said: Do you have wealth? I said: Allah has given me all kinds of wealth; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah bestows a blessing upon His servant, He loves that it be seen upon him."
"3490","3039 - And what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas narrating from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When Allah gives you good or wealth, let it be seen upon you."
"3491","3040 - And what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: these two hadiths are in harmony, not conflicting. As for the hadith of Ibn Thalabah, it concerns shabbiness that does not reach with its owner the extreme shabbiness by which the possessor of blessing becomes indistinguishable from one without blessing. And the two hadiths of Abdullah ibn Masud and Imran ibn Husayn concern the blessing seen upon its owner with nothing in it of arrogance nor waste, nor blameworthy dress on its wearer; and praiseworthy dress is what is above the shabbiness below which there is no lesser shabbiness. And what is in the other two hadiths concerns dress by which its owner does not enter into the highest dress; so whoever does that enters into the meaning of Allah's saying: And those who, when they spend, do not waste nor stint, and it is between that, moderate. And like that is what the people of knowledge were upon, and what they command people regarding dress."
"3492","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan al-Thawri saying: Wear of clothes what does not make you notorious among the jurists, nor despised by it among the fools."
"3493","And as Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: al-Ashjai narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: It used to be said: Wear of clothes... then he mentioned these words the same. So it became clear, by Allah's praise and blessing, that there is no contradiction nor disagreement in anything we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and Allah we ask for success."
"3494","480 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his addressing Abu Abi al-Ahwas, whose name is disputed: one saying he is Awf ibn Malik, and one saying he is Malik ibn Awf; and al-Bukhari mentioned that he is Awf ibn Malik ibn Nadlah; and they do not differ that he is from Banu Jusham, by his saying to him: When Allah gives you wealth, let it be seen upon you. 3041 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from his father, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was unkempt, and he said: Do you have wealth? I said: Yes. He said: From which wealth? I said: From all wealth; from camels, horses, slaves, and sheep. He said: Then when Allah gives you wealth, let it be seen upon you. Then he said: Are the camels of your family born with sound ears, then you take to the razor and cut their ears and say: These are Buhur, and you slit them or slit their skins and say: These are Surum, and so make them forbidden upon yourself? He said: Yes. He said: What Allah has given you is lawful for you, and Allah's forearm is stronger, and Allah's razor is sharper. He said: And sometimes he said: Allah's forearm is mightier than your forearm, and Allah's razor is sharper than your razor."
"3495","3042 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Awf ibn Malik, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing worn-out garments, and he said: Do you have wealth? He said: Allah has given me of all wealth. He said: Then let it be seen upon you. Then he said: O Awf ibn Malik, are your camels not born with sound ears, then you take to some of them and slit their ears and say: These are Buhur; Allah has made no Bahirah; and you take to some of them and slit their ears and say: These are Surum? He said: Yes. He said: Do not do it; Allah's forearm is mightier than your forearm, and Allah's razor is sharper than your razor, and all that Allah has given you is lawful, so do not forbid anything of your wealth. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed Abu Abi al-Ahwas with what he addressed him in it, of his slitting the skins of his camels and cutting them, and of his saying at that what he used to say at it, and of his forbidding them so; and that is what cannot be from a Muslim, but is only from a polytheist; and he verified that."
"3496","3043 - What Ali ibn al-Husayn Abu Ubayd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas al-Jushami, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw rags upon me and said: Do you have wealth? I said: Yes. He said: From which wealth? He said: From all that Allah has given me; from sheep and camels. He said: Then let Allah's blessing and honor be seen upon you. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Are your camels born with complete ears? He said: And are they born except so? - and he had not become Muslim that day - He said: Perhaps you take your razor and cut the ears of some of them and say: These are Buhur, and slit the ears of others and say: These are Surum. He said: Yes. He said: Do not do it, for what Allah has given you is lawful for you, and Allah's razor is sharper, and Allah's forearm is stronger. He said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed this man with what he addressed him, while he had not become Muslim that day. So the meaning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to him: When Allah gives you wealth, let it be seen upon you, may mean that it be seen upon him, so that Allah's believing saints know that the world has no worth with Allah, and that had it been otherwise with Him, He would not have given the like of that of it to one who disbelieves in Him; and so they know it is not an abode of recompense, and that had it been an abode of recompense, whoever believes in Him and affirms His oneness would be worthier of that from Him; and that what He recompenses them for their oneness and worship He gives them in a dwelling other than the one they are in, which is the Hereafter. And of that is His saying: Had it not been that mankind would become one community - meaning upon one religion - We would have made for those who disbelieve in the Merciful roofs of silver for their houses, up to His saying: And all that is but enjoyment of worldly life, and the Hereafter with your Lord is for the God-fearing. He said: His recompense for the God-fearing for their piety and what they are upon for Him is in the Hereafter. And his saying to that man: When Allah gives you wealth, let it be seen upon you, means: so that he knows thereby what Allah has given him of what He withheld the like of from others who are upon what he is upon and from others besides, and that becomes a cause for his thanking Him by what he finds of his entering the religion to which He called him, and holding to what He created him for; for He said: I did not create jinn and men except to worship Me. If he does that, he has rendered thanks for the blessing Allah bestowed upon him, and is praiseworthy with Allah for that, and He is likely to increase him of that blessing in this world and store for him recompense for that in the Hereafter. And if he falls short of that and does not render to Allah what is due to Him in it, he is thereby ungrateful for His favors upon him, deserving punishment from Him along with his disbelief in Him and his deserving punishment for that; so what he deserves for ingratitude for His blessings of His punishment is added to His punishment for his disbelief and association with Him, and he is upon that of severer punishment and harder torment in the Hereafter than others of the disbelievers whom Allah did not give the like of that blessing in this world. This is the best we could manage of interpreting this hadith, and Allah knows best the reality in it; and Him we ask for success."
"3497","481 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his coming out to Makhramah, father of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, while wearing the qaba which he had hidden for him. 3044 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah."
"3498","3045 - And al-Rabi also narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam; al-Rabi said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, and Muhammad said: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, both said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed qabaa garments and did not give Makhramah anything; Makhramah said: O my son, go with me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He went with him and said: Enter and call him for me. So I called him for him, and he came out to him wearing a qaba, and said: I hid this for you. He looked at it and said: Makhramah is pleased. Abu Jafar said: So al-Layth narrated this hadith to most people; and he had narrated it in Iraq with an addition over what he had narrated of it before that."
"3499","3046 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) received qabaa garments, and that reached his father, and he said: O my son, it has reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) received qabaa garments and is distributing them; go with me to him. So we went and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his house, and he said: O my son, call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me. Al-Miswar said: I deemed that grave and said: Should I call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for you! He said: O my son, he is not a tyrant. So I called the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he came out wearing a qaba of brocade buttoned with gold, and said: O Makhramah, this I hid for you, and gave it to him. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing that qaba while it was of brocade buttoned with gold, and that was before the prohibition of wearing silk; and we shall mention what was narrated on the permissibility of wearing silk, and what was narrated on its abrogation and prohibition afterward, in our book, Allah willing."
"3500","3047 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Qabaa garments came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he distributed them among his companions; my father Makhramah said to me: Let us go, perhaps he will give us of them something. He came to the door and said: He is here. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard his voice and came out with a qaba, and it is as if I am looking at him showing my father the beauties of the qaba, saying: I hid this for you, I hid this for you. I said: For what did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do this with Makhramah? He said: He used to guard against his tongue. He said: And some people would reject this hadith, saying: Impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore that qaba while it was of what Allah granted him of fay, in which he has partners; for Allah made fay upon what He mentioned in His Book by His saying: What Allah granted His Messenger of the people of the towns is for Allah, the Messenger, the near kin, orphans, the poor, and the traveler. We reflected on what they said of that and what they denied of this hadith and negated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and found it corrupt; for the fay Allah granted His Messenger are two kinds: one the kind Allah mentioned in the verse I recited, and the other kind mentioned in the verse before it in the surah it is in, which is His saying: What Allah granted His Messenger of them, you did not press upon it horses nor camels. So what was of that fay was for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apart from all people, and was property, not fay of that kind; and it was for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apart from all people. He did not keep it solely for himself, but returned it to strengthening Islam, mending the hearts of those whose hearts he feared would be corrupted against them, though he professes what they profess except he does not have with him of strength of faith what they have; so that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) an increase in his merit, a grandeur of his station, magnifying Allah's rights upon him, and seeking harmony among his community and repelling harm feared from some of it upon the rest. So his distributing those qabaa garments among those he distributed them to was for that; and his wearing the mentioned qaba, wearing it in these hadiths while it was owned by him with no partner in it; for though he hid it for Makhramah, Makhramah did not own it thereby, but owned it only by taking it from him and his handing it to him; and Allah we ask for success."
"3501","482 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on waiting out captive women, the pregnant and others. 3048 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdullah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said; and Sharik, from Qays, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We took captives on the day of Awtas, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A pregnant woman is not to be had until she delivers, nor a non-pregnant until she menstruates once."
"3502","3049 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Qays ibn Wahb and al-Mujalid, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Jafar said: In what we narrated of this hadith is what indicates the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by waiting out those who menstruate of the non-pregnant, and the pregnant, not others among the women of that captivity. And we know it encompassed that among them were those not yet mature, and those who despaired of menstruation; and menstruation and pregnancy are absent in them. So this saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is proof that waiting out is upon other than those women his saying fell upon, and that waiting out is not obligatory for young girls who do not menstruate, nor for those in menopause, as narrated from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Salim ibn Abdullah on that."
"3503","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Talhah ibn Abi Said narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from al-Qasim and Salim: he asked them about a slave girl sold before menstruating: may her buyer have her? They said: One who knows that looks at her; if she has not menstruated, we see nothing upon him. Al-Layth said: If she is ten years old, she should not be had until her womb is waited out for three months, for it reached us that a ten-year-old girl became pregnant. He said: In this is what indicates al-Layth ibn Sad's school was that if her pregnancy is safe from, there is no waiting out in her; and this is a saying Abu Yusuf once said; and there is narrated from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, what indicates this was his school too, and more than that on the virgin, that she is not waited out."
"3504","Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The virgin is not waited out."
"3505","3050 - And what Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Yazid al-Suri narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade having captive women while pregnant until they deliver what is in their wombs or are waited out. Abu Jafar said: This with us does not contradict what we narrated before it in this chapter; for the meaning of or are waited out may mean: or are waited out of what we narrated before it; so the meaning of what was narrated on that from Ibn Abbas and from Abu Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), returns to one meaning; and Allah we ask for success."
"3506","483 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on what Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, did in dividing the fifth of what he was sent to divide of captives, and the servant girl in it falling to his family, and what he did in her of having her, and that reaching the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with no waiting out mentioned in it, and his not disapproving that against him. 3051 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Rahawayh, informed us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jalil ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: No one was more hateful to me than Ali ibn Abi Talib until I loved a man of Quraysh whom I love only for hating Ali; that man was sent over cavalry, and I accompanied him only out of hatred of Ali; he took captives and wrote to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to send one to fifth them; so he sent to us Ali, may Allah be pleased with him; and among the captives was a servant girl of the best captives; when he fifthed it, the girl fell in the fifth, then he fifthed and she fell to the Prophet's household, then he fifthed and she fell to Ali's family; he came to us with his head dripping, and we said: What is this? He said: Did you not see the servant girl fell in the fifth, then fell to the Prophet's household, then fell to Ali's family, and I had her. He wrote, and sent me confirming his letter to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what Ali said; I kept speaking against him, and he says: He told truth; and I speak and he says: He told truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held my hand and said: Do you hate Ali? I said: Yes. He said: Do not hate him; and if you love him, increase in love for him; by Him in whose hand is my soul, the share of Ali's family in the fifth is better than a servant girl. No one after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was dearer to me than Ali. Abdullah ibn Buraydah said: By Allah, there is in the hadith between me and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) none but my father."
"3507","3051M - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Said, he said: I carried the hadith of Ali ibn Suwayd, meaning Ibn Manjuf, from Ibn Buraydah on Ali; when I wrote it, part of it left me without doubt remaining in it; and Yahya narrated it to us from Abd al-Jalil ibn Atiyyah, from Ibn Buraydah. Abu Jafar said: This hadith returns to the narration of al-Nadr ibn Shumayl and Yahya ibn Said of it from Abd al-Jalil ibn Atiyyah. Then someone said: How may you accept this hadith when in it Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, divided between him and the people of the fifth what I mentioned its division in it while he is a partner in that, and it is not permitted for a man to divide for himself against himself and others. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: what is divided by authority of things of this kind may be by one who is a partner in that, as the Imam by imamate divides spoils among their people while he is of them; and when the Imam is so in what we mentioned, whoever divides it for that besides him stands in his place. So the soundness of this meaning of this hadith became clear by Allah's praise and blessing. Then this speaker returned asking us, saying: In this hadith is also what you may not accept from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, on the mentioned servant girl, of his having her; for she only fell to his family, and his family is other than him. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is that meant by his family is himself, upon him peace, in the sense she fell in his share; so there was from him in her what was; for Arabs make a man's family the man, and make his family his loins. And of it is what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he addressed Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa with when he came with his father's charity."
"3508","3052 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir, Abu Zayd the companion of al-Harawi, and Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa - and he was of the companions of the Tree - he said: When people brought their charity, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: O Allah, bless them. He said: My father brought him his charity, and he said: O Allah, bless the family of Abu Awfa. So that meant: O Allah, bless Abu Awfa. And of that is what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Abu Musa: This has been given a flute of the flutes of the family of Dawud, upon him peace, meaning: a flute of the flutes of Dawud, upon him peace; and family is connective; for the flutes were Dawud's, not others of his family nor besides them. And of that is grander than this, His saying: Admit the family of Pharaoh into the severest torment, not to exclude Pharaoh from them while he is included in them. As for other than these two meanings, of what is in this hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, having the mentioned servant girl with no waiting out from him in her, what we brought in the chapter before this chapter suffices us from speech on that in this chapter; and Allah we ask for success."
"3509","484 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on horse meat, of dislike and of permissibility, from the hadith of Jabir ibn Abdullah. 3053 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Amr ibn Dinar, he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed us horse meat, and forbade us donkey meat. This hadith mentioned Amr ibn Dinar's hearing from Jabir ibn Abdullah, and that was not heard in other than this narration."
"3510","3054 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. In that was no mention of Amr hearing it from Jabir."
"3511","3055 - And Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah said... then he mentioned this hadith. So we sought its reality: is it Amr hearing from Jabir, or not hearing from him?"
"3512","3056 - We found Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mukhabarah. Sufyan said: Everything I heard from Amr ibn Dinar - meaning of Jabir's hadith - he said to us: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah, except these two hadiths; I do not know whether between him and Jabir in them is someone or not. Then we sought it from the narration of other than Sufyan from Amr."
"3513","3057 - We found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith. In that was nothing indicating who establishes proof in the reality of this hadith; then we sought that also."
"3514","3058 - We found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi narrated to me, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the day of Khaybar domestic donkey meat, and permitted horse meat. This with us also was not of what we decisively hold that the reality in this hadith is Amr hearing it from Jabir, due to Muhammad ibn Muslim falling short of deserving the like of that; so we sought it from another's hadith."
"3515","3059 - We found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from a man, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: We had carried in our pots horse meat and donkey meat, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to eat horse meat, and forbade us eating donkey meat. So we stood thereby that the origin of this hadith is not Amr hearing it from Jabir, and that between him and him in it is a man; except it is possible that man is of those whose narration is accepted and proof stands by its like, and may be otherwise; so we sought that."
"3516","3060 - We found Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us. And we found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed us horse meat, and forbade us donkey meat. So this hadith became sound-chained from Amr's hadith; then we looked: did anyone narrate from Jabir ibn Abdullah agreeing with this meaning?"
"3517","3061 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: We used to eat horse meat in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3518","3062 - And we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abd al-Karim; and Waki, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Karim, then he mentioned the like of it. So Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn and Ata agreed from Jabir ibn Abdullah on the permissibility of horse meat."
"3519","3063 - And Yazid ibn Sinan also narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, he heard Jabir saying: We ate in Khaybar times horse meat and wild donkeys, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us eating domestic donkey. So what was narrated from Jabir on the lawfulness of horse meat returns to the narration of Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, Ata, and Abu al-Zubayr of that from him. Then someone said: There is narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what contradicts that."
"3520","3064 - He mentioned what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: On the day of Khaybar people suffered hunger, so they took domestic donkeys and slaughtered them and filled pots of them; that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us, so we overturned the pots that day while boiling; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade tame donkeys, horse and mule meat, every fanged beast, every clawed bird, and forbade the mujaththamah, the khalisah, and the nuhbah. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: the hadith folk weaken Ikrimah's hadith from Yahya, and do not make him therein proof; so said more than one of them. Had it been proof, the contradiction of Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, Ata ibn Abi Rabah, and Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir against him in that, from Yahya from Abu Salamah from Jabir, would be worthier than what Yahya narrated therein from Abu Salamah from Jabir; for three are worthier of retention than one; and Allah we ask for success."
"3521","485 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than Jabir ibn Abdullah's hadith on horse meat, of dislike and permissibility. 3065 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Himyari narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus al-Thalabi al-Kufi, known as al-Susi, narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his wife Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We slaughtered a horse in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ate it. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is Asma informing of what she informed in it of what they did in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); in that is proof for whoever permits horse meat in his permitting eating it. And there is narrated from Khalid ibn al-Walid from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prohibition of eating it."
"3522","3066 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us. And as Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi Abu Zurah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi and Khalid ibn Khali narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Salih ibn Yahya ibn al-Miqdam, from his father, from his grandfather, from Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating horse, mule, and donkey meat. In this hadith is the prohibition of eating horse meat. As for most of the reports narrated on horse meat, and the sound of them, is what was narrated on the permissibility of eating their meat of what we narrated in this chapter, and what we narrated in the chapter before it of our book. And if we return to what reflection requires in that, it is the prohibition of eating their meat; for we found cattle whose meat is permitted are cloven-hoofed and split-hoofed, and found domestic donkeys whose meat is forbidden and mules whose meat is forbidden are solid-hoofed; and horses, disputed on eating their meat, are solid-hoofed; so solid-hoofed disputed on eating their meat resemble solid-hoofed forbidden of eating their meat more than cloven and split-hoofed permitted of eating their meat. And Abu Hanifah and Malik ibn Anas held this saying."
"3523","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, he said: I dislike eating horse meat."
"3524","And as Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, he said: The best I heard on horses, mules, and donkeys is they are not eaten; for Allah said: And horses, mules, and donkeys for you to ride and as adornment; and He said on cattle: That you ride of them and of them you eat; and He said: And mention Allah's name on known days over what He provided them of cattle; eat of them and feed the wretched poor. Malik said: Allah mentioned horses, mules, and donkeys for riding and adornment, and mentioned cattle for riding and eating of them. Malik said: That is the matter with us. As for Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, they held the permissibility of eating their meat."
"3525","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, and he mentioned what we related from him also. We reflected on what was related from Malik of what he argued in disliking horse meat, that Allah only created them for riding and adornment: does that prevent eating their meat or not? We found Allah said in His Book: And they remain differing, except whom your Lord blessed; and for that He created them; yet that did not prevent Him having created them also for other than that, since Allah said: I did not create jinn and men except to worship Me. So we understood thereby they are created for what He mentioned creating them in each of these two verses. And when that is so, His saying: And horses, mules, and donkeys for you to ride and as adornment does not prevent His having created them for that and for other than it of what He permitted, of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feeding people their meat. And like that is what we found in the Messenger of Allah's sunnah indicating this meaning also."
"3526","3067 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that they heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: While a man was driving a cow carrying upon it, the cow turned to him and said: I was not created for this; I was created for plowing. People said: Glory be to Allah, in wonder, and were alarmed: A cow speaks! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I believe in it, and Abu Bakr and Umar. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the informing of the cow Allah made to speak with what He made it speak, to be of what believers believe; and what it uttered was truth, since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confirmed and believed it, and informed Abu Bakr and Umar believe in it. And when that is so and it was created for what it was created for in this hadith yet created with that for eating its meat for what Allah mentioned of what Malik recited on eaten cattle, like that are horses: created for what they were mentioned for in the verse he recited therein, of riding and adornment, and created for other than that of eating their meat which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed his companions. And what we narrated of Khalid ibn al-Walid's hadith is not of what opposes what we narrated in its contrary from Jabir ibn Abdullah in the chapter before this chapter; and Allah we ask for success."
"3527","486 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Nothing repels decree except supplication, and nothing increases life except righteousness. 3068 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yaqub al-Talaqani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Durays narrated to me, he said: Abu Mawdud narrated to us - Abu Jafar said: He is Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Sulayman, freedman of Hudhayl; with hadith folk he is trustworthy; of Basra folk; and he is other than Abu Mawdud al-Madini - from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing repels decree except supplication, and nothing increases life except righteousness."
"3528","3069 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Jad, from Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing increases life except righteousness, nothing repels decree except supplication, and a man is deprived of provision by the sin he commits."
"3529","3070 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is pleased that Allah extend his provision, or delay for him in his trace, let him join his kin."
"3530","3071 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Sirari, he narrated from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is pleased to be delayed in his trace, and expanded for him in his provision, let him join his kin."
"3531","3072 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of that. Then someone said: How do you accept this and ascribe it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while you narrate from him what we shall bring afterward in our book, Allah willing: what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when Allah wills to create a soul, He commands the angel with four words: its provision, its term, its deed, and wretched or happy - in Ibn Masud's hadith, and in Hudhayfah ibn Asid's hadith the like of that with addition: nothing is added to that nor decreased from it; and this is severe disagreement. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: this is of what has no disagreement; for it is possible that when Allah wills to create the soul, He makes its term, if it is dutiful, so, and if not dutiful, so less than that; and if supplication comes from it, so is repelled from it, and if no supplication, so befalls it; and if it does so, it is deprived so, and if not, it is provided so; and that is fixed in the record to which nothing is added nor decreased; and in that, by Allah's praise, is the harmony of these reports, their agreement, and the removal of contradiction from them; and Allah we ask for success."
"3532","487 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on what repels from man by his saying morning and evening: In Allah's name, with whose name nothing harms on earth nor in heaven, and He is the Hearing, Knowing. 3073 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed me, from Abu Mawdud - Abu Jafar said: He is al-Madini - from a man - Yunus said: I know him only as Muhammad ibn Kab - from Aban ibn Uthman - and he did not go beyond him with it - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: In Allah's name, with whose name nothing harms on earth nor in heaven, and He is the Hearing, Knowing, three times, no sudden calamity befalls him till night; and whoever says it in the evening is likewise. Abu Jafar said: So Yunus narrated it to us from Anas upon what we mentioned in this chain."
"3533","3074 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Abu Mawdud narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever says: In Allah's name, with whose name nothing harms on earth nor in heaven, and He is the Hearing, Knowing, and says it in the evening, no sudden calamity befalls him till morning; and if he says it in the morning, no sudden calamity befalls him till evening. And Aban was struck by paralysis, and it was said to him: Where is what you narrated to us? He said: By Allah, I neither lied nor was lied to, but when Allah willed for me what He willed, He made me forget that supplication."
"3534","3075 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb also narrated it to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Abu Mawdud, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, except he did not mention what is in it that Aban was struck by paralysis to the end of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: This hadith is narrated from other than Muhammad ibn Kab's path, from Aban ibn Uthman, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3535","3076 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, companion of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Aban ibn Uthman ibn Affan, he said: I heard Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No servant says in the morning of every day and evening of every night: In Allah's name, with whose name nothing harms on earth nor in heaven, and He is the Hearing, Knowing, three times, then anything harms him. He said: Aban had been struck by part of paralysis, and the man kept looking at him, and Aban said to him: Do not look; the hadith is as I narrated to you, but I did not say it that day for Allah's decree to proceed. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith and found the worthiest bearing to carry it upon is the meaning we carried upon it the reports we narrated in the chapter before this chapter; and in what we mentioned therein is sufficiency for us from speech in this chapter with the meaning we mentioned it is worthiest of; and Allah we ask for success."
"3536","488 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters, for every verse of it an outer and inner. 3077 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters; for every verse of it an outer and inner. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith; the best interpretation coming in it it bears is that the outer of it is what appears of its meaning, and the inner of it is what is hidden of its meaning; and that indicates people must seek its inner as they must seek its outer, to stand upon what in each is of what Allah made them worship with, and what in it is lawful and unlawful; and Allah we ask for success."
"3537","489 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his judging the custody of Hamzah's daughter, may Allah be pleased with her, to her maternal aunt Asma bint Umays, and not preventing her from that by the husband she has, Jaafar ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, since he was not her mahram kin. 3078 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi and Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, both said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from Hani, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged Hamzah's daughter to her maternal aunt, and said: The maternal aunt is in the mother's place; and that when Ali, Zayd, and Jaafar, may Allah be pleased with them, disputed over her."
"3538","3079 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hani and Hubayrah - the Shaykh said: Hubayrah ibn Yarim - from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that Hamzah's daughter followed them calling: O uncle, O uncle; Ali took her, held her hand, and said: Here is your cousin's daughter, take her. Ali, Zayd, and Jaafar disputed over her; Ali said: I took her, she is my cousin's daughter. Jaafar said: My cousin's daughter, and her maternal aunt is under me. Zayd said: My brother's daughter. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged her to her maternal aunt, and said: The maternal aunt is in the mother's place. He said to Ali: You are from me and I from you. He said to Jaafar: You resembled my creation and character. He said to Zayd: You are our brother and freedman. Ali said to him: O Messenger of Allah, will you not marry Hamzah's daughter? He said: She is my brother's daughter through suckling."
"3539","3080 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu Farwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he, Jaafar ibn Abi Talib, and Zayd ibn Harithah disputed over Hamzah's daughter to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her to Jaafar, because her maternal aunt is with him."
"3540","3081 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yahya al-Umawi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Najih and from Aban ibn Salih, from Ata, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali, Zayd, and Jaafar, may Allah be pleased with them, disputed over Hamzah's daughter; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged her to Jaafar, due to her maternal aunt Asma bint Umays."
"3541","3082 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar informed me, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Nafi ibn Ujayr, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, was struck, Zayd ibn Harithah went out until he brought Hamzah's daughter, and said: I am worthiest of her, she be with me; I undertook travel, she is my brother's daughter. Ali ibn Abi Talib said: I am worthiest of her, she be with me; she is my cousin, and with me is the Messenger of Allah's daughter. Jaafar ibn Abi Talib said: I am worthiest of her; mine is like your kinship, and with me is her maternal aunt; and the maternal aunt is a mother. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and said: I judge among you in that and in other than it. Ali said: I feared a Quran had descended among us for raising our voices. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for you, O Zayd, you are my freedman and hers. He said: I am pleased with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for you, O Ali, you are my elite and trustee; you are from me and I from you. As for you, O Jaafar, you resembled my creation and character; you are of my tree of which I am. I have judged the girl to be with her maternal aunt. They said: We are pleased with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3542","3083 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Nafi ibn Ujayr, from his father, from Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. He said: In this hadith's chain is an addition over Yunus's hadith chain, with Muhammad ibn Nafi ibn Ujayr adding it from his father from Ali; and in that is the necessity of connecting it to Ali, upon him peace."
"3543","3084 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban narrated to us, both said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib was struck, then he mentioned this hadith as those we narrated it from before him mentioned in this chapter. Then someone said: This is a hadith the people of knowledge all abandoned; for they do not judge custody to one with a husband not her mahram kin, of the boy or girl in custody; whence was it wide for them all to abandon this hadith, while it came with this recurrent coming! Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: they did not abandon this hadith nor oppose it, but took it and acted upon it from where its taking and acting was hidden to you; for the boy or girl need custody when they have among women none of their mahram kin free of husbands; their custody returns to their agnates. Hamzah's daughter, when her maternal aunt had a husband not her mahram kin, her custody returned to her agnates: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali, and Jaafar sons of Abu Talib. Her custody returned to them; she was with Jaafar with her maternal aunt; and her maternal aunt is only prevented from custody by her husband were he not of custody folk. When custody returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to Ali, and to him, it returned thereby to its ruling were her husband her mahram kin of Hamzah's daughter, in the sense not cutting her maternal aunt from her custody; for she is with one fit to be with in that state. Custody thereby returned to her, and her being married did not prevent her from it; for her husband, if custody does not return to her, returns to him and to whoever is like him among her agnates; and when it returns to him, he is no barrier to her custody, but her custody returns to her; for she argues him, saying to him: When I am only prevented by you, I am by preventing you from custody of my sister's daughter worthier, and by deserving that against you fitter. This is the meaning by which Asma bint Umays deserved custody of her sister's daughter, and the marriage she is in did not prevent her from that; and Allah we ask for success."
"3544","490 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated on the boy and girl when their parents dispute, which of them is worthier to be with. 3085 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ziyad ibn Sad, from Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah, from Abu Maymunah - and he is not his father - from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was brought a boy between two parents, and said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought a boy between his parents, and he said: O boy, this is your mother and this is your father, so choose."
"3545","3086 - And Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: I heard from Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah narrating it from Abu Maymunah, he said: A Persian man and a wife of his disputing over a son of theirs came to Abu Hurayrah, and the Persian said: O Abu Hurayrah, this is Busar, meaning a son. Abu Hurayrah said: I shall judge between you with what I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judge with: O boy, this is your father and this is your mother, choose whichever you wish. Then Abu Hurayrah said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and a man and woman came disputing over a son of theirs; the man said: O Messenger of Allah, my son draws from the well of Abu Inabah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is your father and this is your mother, choose whichever you wish. He said: In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gives that boy choice between his parents; and in that is attachment for whoever holds choice in the like of this against whoever does not hold choice therein, of those arguing with Hamzah's daughter's hadith we narrated in the chapter before this chapter; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give Hamzah's daughter choice among her agnates to choose whichever she wished. To this most Kufans held in abandoning choice therein; and many Hijaz folk apply choice in this for the hadith we narrated therein from Abu Hurayrah. Except upon them in that are demands of some opposing them therein that Ziyad's hadith did not encompass what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in that boy; and another not below him encompassed it, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir."
"3546","3087 - As Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Abdah ibn Abdullah ibn Zayd al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abu Tawbah al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya, he is Ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him - and he did not mention Abu Maymunah in its chain - he said: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My husband wants to come between me and my son, and he had divorced her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cast lots upon him. The man said: Who comes between me and my son! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the boy choice between his father and mother, and he chose his mother, and she went with him."
"3547","3088 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Shabbuwayh narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Mubarak, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Maymunah, from Abu Hurayrah - and he did not mention Hilal therein - he said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of hers, and her husband had divorced her, and his father wanted to take him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cast lots in him. The man said: Who comes between me and my son! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the boy: Choose whichever you wish. He chose the mother, and she went with him. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give that boy choice between his father and mother until he called his parents to cast lots upon him before that. Whoever gives choice without his calling those he gives choice between to cast lots upon the boy before choice abandons this hadith; and upon him in abandoning it is like what is upon one who does not give choice in abandoning choice in this hadith. And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also in the like of this what indicates choice was not from him a judgment with it, but was by the boy's parents choosing that."
"3548","3089 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Uthman al-Batti narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Salamah al-Ansari informed me: his grandfather became Muslim in the Messenger of Allah's time while his wife did not become Muslim, and he has from her a child; they disputed over their child to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said to them: If you wish, I give you choice. He seated the father on a side and the mother on a side, then gave the boy choice; he went toward his mother, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, guide him; the boy returned to his father. So Hushaym narrated this hadith from Abd al-Hamid; and others opposed him in its chain, narrating it adding over what Hushaym narrated upon him."
"3549","3090 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar al-Ansari, from his father, from his grandfather Rafi ibn Sinan, that he became Muslim while his wife refused to become Muslim; she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My daughter - she is weaned or near it - and I have reached my daughter. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sit, and said: Sit on a side; and he seated the girl between them, and said: Call her. The girl came to her mother, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, guide her; she went to her father and he took her. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded this girl's parents to call her; and this is of what indicates this is of the ruling in the like of her."
"3550","3091 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Uthman al-Batti, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Salamah, from his father, that a man became Muslim while his wife did not become Muslim; they disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over a boy of theirs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you let him choose? They said: Yes. His mother called him, and he went toward her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, guide him. His father called him, and he turned to him. In this hadith also is that the giving choice was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to that boy by his parents choosing that, not obligatory upon them therein."
"3551","3092 - And as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Shabbuwayh narrated to us, he said: I said to Abd al-Razzaq: Did Sufyan inform you, from Uthman al-Batti, from Abd al-Hamid al-Ansari, from his father, from his grandfather, that he became Muslim while his wife refused to become Muslim, and he came with a young son not mature; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated the mother here and the father here, then gave him choice, and said: O Allah, guide him, and he went to his father? Abd al-Razzaq said: Yes. In this hadith the boy was not mature and was young; in that is what indicates the mention of reaching in what we narrated before it did not mean reaching maturity, but meant reaching judgment therein with what must be judged in the like of it."
"3552","3093 - And as Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Uthman al-Batti narrated to us - and he was of high rank in knowledge - from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, he said: My father became Muslim while my mother refused to become Muslim; they disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was a boy. My father said: I am worthier of him. My mother said: I am worthier of him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish, I give him choice. My mother leapt out of tenderness to me and said: I am pleased. My father said: I am pleased. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called me and said: O boy, if you wish go to your father, and if you wish go to your mother. I turned toward my mother; when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw that, I heard him saying behind me: O Allah, guide him; and I turned to my father until I sat in his lap. In this hadith also is that the Prophet's giving that boy choice was only after his parents chose to give him choice between them. So by verifying what we narrated in this chapter, nothing of what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein may be left out nor abandoned; and what is applied in the like of this is calling the boy's parents to cast lots upon him: if they answer to that, lots are cast between them upon him; and if they refuse that then ask that the boy be given choice between them to choose one and be worthier with him than the other, that is done therein; and if there is no choice from them in that, one must return to what is in Hamzah's daughter's hadith we narrated in the chapter before this chapter, and it is applied therein, and judged for whom the judge sees worthier therein of those disputing to him therein. And Abd al-Hamid, holder of this hadith - Isa ibn Yunus clarified him to us in his narration of it from him - that he is Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar; and whatever others narrating it from him of those we mentioned in this chapter ascribed him to, Hushaym saying therein: Ibn Salamah, and Hammad ibn Salamah agreeing with him upon that, and Ali ibn Asim saying therein: Abd al-Hamid ibn Abi Salamah: whoever ascribed him to other than Jafar only ascribed him to his father's kunya, or to a father among his fathers named with that name he mentioned him with."
"3553","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Ikrimah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, disputed with his wife whom he divorced, to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, over her child; Abu Bakr said: She is worthier of him so long as she does not marry or the boy grows up; and said: She is more tender, compassionate, gentle, kind, and merciful. Abu Jafar said: Except it is possible his saying: or the boy grows up, does not mean a state in which he is given choice, but means a state in which he leaves custody and is independent of it, and is for his father apart from his mother; and Allah we ask for success."
"3554","491 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The Quran came down upon seven letters. 3094 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us; and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, both said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Qays al-Yashkuri Abu Hammam narrated to me, from Uthman ibn Hassan al-Amiri, from Fulfulah al-Jufi, he said: I hastened among those hastening to Abdullah ibn Masud on the codices; we entered upon him, and a man of the folk said: We did not come to you visiting, but we came when this news alarmed us. He said: The Quran was sent down upon your Prophet from seven doors upon seven letters; and the Book used to come down from one door upon one letter."
"3555","3095 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us; and Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, he said: Jarir, he is Ibn Abd al-Hamid, narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Wasil ibn Hayyan, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters; for every verse of it an outer and inner, and for every limit a lookout."
"3556","3096 - And Abu Umayyah and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, both said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed me, from Anas, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from Ubayy, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters."
"3557","3097 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed us, from Anas, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that Ubayy said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters."
"3558","3098 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Suqayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Hudhayfah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met Gabriel, upon him peace, and said: I was sent to a community among whom is the old shaykh, the old woman, the boy, the servant, and the worn shaykh who never read a book at all. He said: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters."
"3559","3099 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from Busr ibn Said: Abu Juhaym al-Ansari informed him, that two men differed over a Quran verse; this said: I received it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the other said: I received it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This Quran was sent down upon seven letters; do not dispute over the Quran, for disputing over it is disbelief."
"3560","3100 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, he said: I heard Umm Ayyub al-Ansariyyah; and once - Yunus the speaker - he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me, from his father, he said: I heard Umm Ayyub al-Ansariyyah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed with me, and I heard him saying: The Quran came down upon seven letters; whichever you read, you are right. So Yunus dictated it to us upon what we mentioned of the differing of what Ibn Uyaynah narrated upon him in each of these two times."
"3561","3101 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The Quran was sent down upon seven letters; recite, no harm, except do not join mention of mercy with torment, nor mention of torment with mercy. Abu Jafar said: Some folk held these seven letters mentioned in these reports are seven aspects, each aspect a part of the Quran unlike the other's course; and held each letter of these letters is a kind of the kinds, for Allah's saying: And of people who worship Allah upon a letter; if good befalls him he is reassured by it, and if trial befalls him he turns on his face, to the end of the verse. So the meaning of the letter upon which Allah is worshipped is a kind of the kinds upon which Allah is worshipped; of them praiseworthy with Him, and of them otherwise with Him: of those letters a restraining letter, a commanding letter, a lawful letter, an unlawful letter, a firm letter, an ambiguous letter, and a parables letter. I heard Ahmad ibn Abi Imran saying: This interpretation with me is corrupt; for Ubayy ibn Kab is narrated from that Gabriel came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Recite upon one letter; he asked more, and said: Recite upon two letters. We know the letter he taught him to recite upon is impossible to be unlawful and nothing else, or lawful and nothing else; for the Quran may not be recited upon it all lawful, nor upon it all unlawful. Abu Jafar said: It is as Ibn Abi Imran said, and of what the folk of this saying argued for their saying."
"3562","3102 - What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zurah Wahbullah ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid informed us, from Salamah ibn Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The first Book came down from one door upon one letter; and the Quran came down from seven doors upon seven letters: restraining, commanding, lawful, unlawful, firm, ambiguous, and parables. So permit its lawful, forbid its unlawful, do what you are commanded, desist what you are forbidden, take lesson by its parables, act by its firm, believe in its ambiguous, and say: We believe in Allah; all is from our Lord."
"3563","3103 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salamah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith and did not mention Abdullah ibn Masud therein. Abu Jafar said: Haywah and al-Layth differed over Uqayl in this hadith's chain; each narrated it from him upon what we mentioned in his narration of it from him. He said: The folk of chain knowledge reject this hadith for its break in chain, and because Abu Salamah in age cannot meet Abdullah ibn Masud nor take it from him. Others held, of what Ibn Abi Imran mentioned to us, that the meaning of seven letters is seven languages; for mentioned in the Quran is many a thing in differing Arab tongues, and of it what is mentioned in other than their tongues yet Arabized and entered their tongue, like Tur Sinin. So the Quran came down upon all those letters, part upon this letter and part upon the other letter; so it was said: The Quran came down upon seven letters, meaning: the whole Quran came down upon those seven letters. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this chapter to stand upon the reality therein, Allah willing. We found Allah said in His Book: We sent no messenger except in his folk's tongue to clarify to them. Allah informed us messengers are only sent in their folks' tongues, not others' tongues; and we understood thereby the tongue the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent with is his folk's tongue, the Quraysh, not other Arab tongues and others. His folk meant thereby are Quraysh, not others. And of that is Allah's saying to him: It is a reminder for you and your folk, meaning Quraysh, not others. And His saying: Your folk denied it while it is the truth, meaning deniers of it of Quraysh, not others. And His saying: Warn your nearest kin; so he called Quraysh belly by belly till he reached their last, not passing them to others, though they bore him as Quraysh bore him. We understood thereby his folk Allah sent him in their tongue are Quraysh apart from others. He would recite what came down upon him of the Quran in the tongue we mentioned, to its folk and to others of people of differing Arab tongues, and to others not Arabs entering his religion like Salman al-Farisi and others accompanying him, believing and confirming him. His tongue's folk were illiterate, writing only little of them in weak writing; preserving what he recites to them in its letters was hard upon them, nor could they write that and memorize it, for hardship in that upon them. When his tongue's folk are as we mentioned, whoever is not of his tongue after taking that from him in its letters is more emphatic, and their excuse therein wider; for whoever is upon a tongue then wants to shift to another tongue cannot do that except with severe training and heavy hardship. They needed to preserve what he recited to them of what Allah sent down upon him of the Quran, to recite it in their prayers and know thereby their religion's laws; so He widened for them to recite it in its meanings, though their words reciting it differ from their Prophet's words reciting it to them. He widened for them therein with what we mentioned. Proof of what we described is that Umar ibn al-Khattab and Hisham ibn Hakim ibn Hizam, both Qurashis whose tongue is the Messenger of Allah's tongue in which the Quran came down upon him, differed in what they recited of Surah al-Furqan, till they recited it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and of his saying to them is what was narrated in a hadith returning to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3564","3104 - It is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard Hisham ibn Hakim ibn Hizam reciting Surah al-Furqan upon other than what I recite it upon; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taught it to me. I almost rushed upon him, then gave him respite till he finished; then I seized him by his cloak and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: I heard this reciting Surah al-Furqan upon other than what you taught me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite. He recited the recitation I heard him recite. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: So was it sent down. Then he said to me: Recite. I recited, and he said: So was it sent down. This Quran was sent down upon seven letters; recite what is easy of it."
"3565","3105 - And what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"3566","3106 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"3567","3107 - And what Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read upon Malik, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"3568","3108 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ansari narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, both said: We heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: I heard Hisham ibn Hakim reciting Surah al-Furqan, then he mentioned it."
"3569","3109 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me: al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari informed him that they heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3570","3110 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari narrated to him that they heard Umar, then both mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: We understood thereby that Umar and Hisham's differing in this surah's recitation till the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them for their differing what he said to them of what is mentioned in this hadith, was only of the words each recited it with differing from the words the other recited it with. We understood thereby the seven letters he informed them the Quran came down with are the letters not differing in command nor prohibition nor lawful nor unlawful, like a man's saying to a man: Come, and his saying to him: Approach, and his saying to him: Draw near. Thereby the two sayings we began mentioning in this chapter are negated. And like that is what was narrated from Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning."
"3571","3111 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, from Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Nothing rankled in myself since I became Muslim except I recited a verse and another recited it; I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught it to me. My companion said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught it to me. We came to him, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, did you teach me such verse? He said: Yes. My companion said: Did you teach it to me so? He said: Yes. Gabriel and Michael, upon them peace, came to me; Gabriel sat on my right, Michael on my left. He said: Recite the Quran upon one letter. Michael said: Ask more. He said: Recite the Quran upon two letters, till it reached seven letters; and all sufficient, healing."
"3572","3112 - And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih al-Harithi narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Sulayman ibn Surad, that Ubayy ibn Kab said."
"3573","3113 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Sulayman ibn Surad, from Ubayy ibn Kab, he said: Ubayy recited a verse, Ibn Masud recited contrary to it, and another man recited contrary to it. We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I said to him: Did you not recite such verse so and so? Ibn Masud said: Did you not recite such verse so and so? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All of you are good, excellent. He said: We said: Not all of us is better nor more excellent. He said: He struck my chest and said: O Ubayy, I was taught the Quran; I said: Upon one letter, or upon two? The angel with me said to me: Upon two. I said: Upon two? He said to me: Upon two or upon three. The angel with me said to me: Upon three. I said: Upon three. So till it reached seven letters, none but healing, sufficient. You say: Forgiving, Merciful; or say: Hearing, Wise; or say: Knowing, Wise; or say: Mighty, Wise; whichever you say, it is so. Sulayman added in his hadith: So long as torment is not sealed with mercy, nor mercy with torment."
"3574","3114 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Musa, son of al-Suddi's daughter, narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Surad, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Two angels came to me; one said: Teach him upon one letter. He said: Upon one letter? He said: Increase him. He ended with me at seven letters."
"3575","3115 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Nasr al-Tammar narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd, he is Ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Surad, he said: Two angels came to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned its like. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what indicates the seven letters are the seven we mentioned, and they do not differ in meanings though differing in uttered words; and that was widening from Allah upon them for their need to that and want of it, though what came down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came down in single words. And of that is what was narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, of what Ibn Shihab carried upon the meaning we carried it upon."
"3576","3116 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah narrated to me that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gabriel taught me upon one letter; I kept asking more, and he increases me till it ended at seven letters. Ibn Shihab said: It reached me those seven letters are only in the matter one, not differing in lawful nor unlawful. Abu Jafar said: These seven for people in these letters were in their incapacity to take the Quran upon other than them of what they cannot, for what we mentioned before in this chapter. They were upon that till many writing among them grew, and till their tongues returned to the Messenger of Allah's tongue; so they strengthened thereby to preserve the Quran in its words it came down with. Then it was not wide for them then to recite it contrary to them. It became clear with what we mentioned that those seven letters were only in a special time for a necessity calling to that; then that necessity lifted, and the ruling of these seven letters lifted, and what the Quran is recited with returned to one letter. And there is narrated of Ubayy's hadith in the meaning we mentioned what is more than his hadith we narrated before this."
"3577","3117 - As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar and Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, both said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ubayy ibn Kab, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the pond of Banu Ghifar; Gabriel came to him and said: Allah commands you to recite, you and your community, upon one letter. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I ask Allah His pardon and forgiveness; my community cannot do that. Then he returned to him the second, and said: Allah commands you to recite the Quran upon two letters. He said: I ask Allah His pardon and forgiveness; my community cannot bear that. Then he came to him the third, and said to him the like of that; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him the like of that. Then he came to him the fourth, and said: Allah commands you and your community to recite the Quran upon seven letters; however they recite with them, they are right. And there is narrated from Abu Bakrah in this meaning also."
"3578","3118 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Zayd informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from Abu Bakrah, he said: Gabriel came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Recite upon one letter. He said: Michael said: Ask more. He said: Recite upon two letters. Michael said: Ask more, till it reached seven letters. He said: Recite it; all sufficient, healing, except you mix a mercy verse with a torment verse, or a torment verse with a mercy verse, upon the like of: Come, approach, advance, go, hasten, hurry. What is in these two hadiths also indicates what we mentioned of what we carried these reports' aspects upon; and of what indicates the recitation's return to one letter before them after it was before that upon the seven letters we mentioned is what Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, did of gathering the Quran and writing it in what he wrote it in."
"3579","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim and Kharijah, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, gathered the Quran in sheets; and he had asked Zayd ibn Thabit to look into that, and he refused him, till he sought help upon him with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he did. Those writings were with Abu Bakr till he died, then were with Hafsah, the Prophet's wife. Uthman sent to her, and she refused to hand them to him till he pledged to return them to her; she sent them to him, and Uthman copied them - these codices - then returned them to her. They remained with her till Marwan sent and took them and burned them."
"3580","And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Ibn Shihab, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, he said: When the Messenger of Allah's companions were killed at Yamamah, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: The Messenger of Allah's companions fell in crowds on Yamamah day; I fear they attend no battlefield but do that therein till killed, while they are the Quran's bearers; so the Quran is lost and forgotten. Gather and write it. Abu Bakr disliked it and said: I do what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do! Then Abu Bakr sent to Zayd ibn Thabit, Umar leaning, meaning like reclining. Abu Bakr said: This called me to a matter and I refused him; and you are the revelation's scribe. If you are with him, I follow you both; and if you agree with me, I do not do what he said. Abu Bakr related Umar's saying; I disliked it and said: We do what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do! Till Umar said a word, he said: What is upon you both were you to do? Abu Bakr commanded me, and I wrote it in leather pieces, shoulder-blade shards, and palm stalks - the Shaykh said: meaning fronds. When Abu Bakr perished and Umar had written all that in one sheet, it was with him; when he perished it was with Hafsah. Then Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman came in a raid he raided, the frontier of Armenia; he did not enter his house till he came to Uthman and said: O Commander of the Faithful, reach the people. Uthman said: What is that? He said: I raided Armenia; its folk of Iraq and Sham attended; Iraq folk recite Ubayy's recitation, bringing what Iraq folk never heard, and Iraq folk call them disbelievers; and Iraq folk recite Abdullah ibn Masud's recitation, bringing what Sham folk never heard, and Sham folk call them disbelievers. Zayd said: Uthman commanded me to write for him a codex, and said: I am placing with you an intelligent, eloquent man; whatever you agree in, write it; and whatever you differ in, raise it to me. He placed with him Aban ibn Said ibn al-As. When it reached: The sign of his kingship is the Tabut comes to you, Zayd said: I said: al-Tabuh, and Aban said: al-Tabut. We raised that to Uthman, and he wrote al-Tabut. Then I presented it, meaning the codex, another presentation, and found nothing in it. Uthman sent to Hafsah to give him the sheet, swearing to return it to her; she gave it, and I presented the codex upon it; they did not differ in anything. He returned it to her, and his soul was content, and commanded people to write codices. Abu Jafar said: We stood thereby that the Quran's gathering was from Abu Bakr and Umar, both rightly guided; and the Messenger of Allah's command to follow them preceded, and we narrated that in what preceded of our book. Uthman followed them upon that, an rightly guided Imam; and Zayd ibn Thabit followed them also, the Messenger's revelation scribe; he wrote the codex for Uthman with his hand. The Messenger of Allah's companions followed them upon that; so it became consensus. Transmission by consensus is the proof by whose like Islam was transmitted to us till we knew its laws, till we stood upon prayer counts and what besides of Islam's laws. It returned to that whoever disbelieves in a letter of it is disbeliever, lawful of blood if he does not return to what the community is upon. It differs from the ruling of reports transmitted by singles with what opposes something in the codex we mentioned; for whoever disbelieves in what single reports bring is not disbeliever as whoever disbelieves in what the community brought of what we mentioned is disbeliever. In what we mentioned is what indicates whoever ascribes something opposing what is in our codex to one of the Messenger's companions pays no heed to what he related; for he related what establishes no proof, opposing what proof is established with; and with Allah is success. In what we mentioned of what we narrated in Yunus's hadith from Nuaym of what returns to Kharijah ibn Zayd is that the codex written in Uthman's time's writer was Zayd ibn Thabit in Aban ibn Said's presence, by carrying out what they did therein upon their agreeing, and what they did in their differing. And there is narrated from other than Kharijah that the Messenger of Allah's companions were that codex's writers by Uthman's command."
"3581","As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, he said: A man of Banu Amir called Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, he said: They differed over the Quran in Uthman's time till boys and teachers fought; it reached Uthman, and he said: With me you lie about it and differ in it; whoever is far from me is severer in lying and more in error. To Muhammad's companions: Gather and write for people. He said: They wrote. He said: He narrated to me that when they disputed over a verse, they said: This the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught so-and-so; he sends to him while he is three miles from Madinah, and it is said: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) teach you so and so? He says: So and so. They write it, having left for it a place. This is in emphasis above what is in Kharijah's hadith; and Allah we ask for success."
"3582","492 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The Quran was sent down upon three letters. 3119 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq al-Basri and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, both said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Quran was sent down upon three letters. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith; some before us held these three letters are a saying said, a certainty believed, and a deed done; of those holding that was Ahmad ibn Salih. Worthier than what they said therein with us - Allah knows best - is it may be that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon what Ubayy ibn Kab narrated from him in the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter of what he related from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Gabriel sitting on his right and Michael on his left, and Gabriel saying to him: Recite the Quran upon one letter, and Michael saying to him: Ask more, and he saying: Recite the Quran upon two letters till it reached seven letters: it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was between launching a number of these letters to recite the Quran upon, teaching that to people and addressing them with it, to stand upon Allah's mercy to them and His widening upon them in what they recite the Quran upon; Samurah hears from him the letters he had then launched to recite the Quran upon, then three letters, no more; then he went on; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was launched to recite the Quran upon more than that to the completion of seven letters; Samurah did not hear that. He narrated what he heard, and fell short of what eluded him of them of what others heard of those we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. Each party narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what it heard from him of that; and whoever heard from him something of that more than another heard was worthier with that addition he heard than others falling short of it; and Allah we ask for success."
"3583","493 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated on agreeing letters in script, differing in utterance. 3120 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah, Abu Muawiyah, and Waki informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Zibyan, he said: Ibn Abbas said to me: Upon which of the two recitations do you recite? I said: Upon the first recitation, Ibn Masud's recitation. He said: Rather Ibn Masud's recitation is the last; Gabriel would present the Quran to Allah's Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) every Ramadan; in the year he died in, he presented it twice; Abdullah witnessed what was abrogated of it and what was changed."
"3584","3121 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, adding: That is the last recitation."
"3585","3122 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, that he said to his companions: Which of the two recitations do you see last? They said: Zayd's recitation. He said: No; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would present recitation to Gabriel every year; in the year he was taken in, he presented it to him twice; Ibn Masud witnessed it, and Abdullah's recitation was last. He said: Then we found the reciters differed in things they recite the Quran upon of what is agreeing in script yet differing in their utterances with them, of them His saying: When you strike in Allah's path, verify; and in others' recitation of them: Be firm. And of them His saying: O believers, if a corrupt one comes to you with news, verify, in some's recitation; and in others' recitation: Be firm. And of them His saying: Those who believe and do righteous deeds, We shall settle them of Paradise in chambers, in some's recitation; and in others' recitation of them: We shall lodge them of Paradise in chambers. And of them His saying: Look at the bones how We spread them, in some's recitation; and in others' recitation of them: We raise them. And like that in the Quran as the reciters recited them, differing in them, not censuring one another in opposing him therein. That was from them after standing upon what the codices were written upon whose writing those we mentioned undertook in what preceded of our book, by the command of those commanding that of the rightly guided caliphs, and with the presence of others of the Messenger of Allah's companions transmitting to us from him Islam, its laws and rulings established as proof upon us. Whoever leaves something of them to its contrary is renegade; whoever denies something of them is thereby disbeliever; we seek his repentance, and if he returns to Islam and affirming what he denied and adhering to what adherence was upon him, we accept that from him; and if he persists upon what he turned to and does not return to what we called him to, we kill him as we kill other apostates. The letters we mentioned their differing in reciting them only reach their realities were the written codices therein used with their dotting or voweling, till every letter of them is distinct from others like it in script, differing in utterance. But those writing them, Allah's pleasure upon them, left that out of dislike to mix with Allah's Book other than it, till many of them disliked writing surah openings, tenths, and fifths. Their views, Allah's pleasure upon them, are proof; saying with what they held therein is obligatory; leaving it unpraiseworthy. Then their differing in words with these letters may be that one attended the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting with them, and took them from him as he heard him recite with them; then Gabriel presented the Quran to him and changed some of them; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to people the recitation to which Gabriel returned what he recited before that to what he recited to him after it. Some of his companions attended that, and some were absent from it. Whoever attended that recited what he recited of those letters upon the second recitation, and whoever attended the first recitation and was absent from the second did not know that, and adhered to the first recitation. That was from him like what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in rulings of what Allah abrogated after that upon his tongue with what He abrogated it with; and of what some stood upon the first ruling and the second ruling, and turned to the second ruling; and some were absent from the second ruling of those attending the first ruling and knowing it, and held to the first ruling. Each party was upon its obligation and what is counted with it. Like those letters we mentioned and their differing therein of the Quran is upon this meaning; and each party upon what it is of them is praiseworthy. All recitations are from Allah; one reciting something of them, opposing others, must not be censured; and Allah we ask for success."
"3586","494 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what the reciters differed in, some adding over others therein what others fell short of. 3123 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja al-Ghudani narrated to us; and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, both said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ubayy ibn Kab narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrating of the story of Moses and al-Khidr, upon them peace, that while they walked upon the shore, al-Khidr saw a boy playing with boys; al-Khidr took him by his head and pulled it with his hand and killed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you kill a pure soul without a soul; you have come to a heinous thing. Then he drove the hadith till he ended of it to al-Khidr asking Moses, upon them peace, of what he did of what he disapproved against him, and to al-Khidr saying to him: As for the boy, he was disbeliever, and his parents were believers. He said: In this hadith: Did you kill a pure soul; and there is narrated from this aspect contrary to this letter, of Abu Ishaq's narration from Said ibn Jubayr from Ibn Abbas from Ubayy also."
"3587","3124 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: I heard my father saying: Raqabah narrated to me, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Ubayy ibn Kab narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like of it, except he mentioned in place of pure: growing."
"3588","3125 - And Imran ibn Musa al-Tai Abu al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father mentioning from Raqabah, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The boy al-Khidr killed was stamped disbeliever; had he reached, he would have burdened his parents with transgression and disbelief. Abu Jafar said: Abu Ishaq is differed upon in this hadith in pure and growing, upon what we mentioned from him in each of these two narrations. This hadith is also narrated from Amr ibn Dinar, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ubayy, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with growing, not pure."
"3589","3126 - As Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Jubayr narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ubayy ibn Kab narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith and said therein in place of pure in the first hadith: growing. Abu Jafar said: This letter the reciters differed in reciting it; some recited with pure. Of those reciting so of them, in what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted me from Abu Ubayd: Asim, al-Amash, Hamzah, and al-Kisai. Of those reciting of them: growing, in what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted me from Abu Ubayd also: Abu Jafar, Shaybah, Nafi, Abdullah ibn Kathir, and Abu Amr. Abu Ubayd said: Recitation with us is: growing; for Abu Amr would distinguish between them in interpretation, saying: The growing is that never sinning at all, and the pure is that sinning then forgiven; and al-Khidr only killed him young, not reaching puberty. Abu Ubayd said in this permission: al-Kisai saw them two dialects in one meaning. What al-Kisai said therein with us is worthier than what Abu Amr said therein of what Abu Ubayd agreed with him upon. Then we return saying to Abu Ubayd, saying to him: As for this slain though named boy, he may be named boy while mature. As for what is in it of his saying: Had he reached he would burden them with transgression and disbelief: that reaching may be puberty, and may be other than it, of knowledge of blameworthy things by which he burdens his parents with transgression and disbelief. In the verse is what indicates he was mature: Allah's saying relating from His Prophet Moses, upon him peace, in addressing His Prophet al-Khidr, upon him peace: Did you kill a pure soul without a soul, meaning: had it killed a soul, it would deserve killing for it; so that is only after its maturity. Its purity is strengthened by its cleanness. That is strengthened by Allah's saying in Mary's story: To grant you a pure boy, meaning: clean. So its being described pure is without sin from him before that till Allah forgave him. In what we mentioned of that is what necessitates the corruption of what Abu Amr said in distinguishing between the pure and growing, and establishing what al-Kisai said: they are two dialects in one meaning. Arabs do the like, saying: al-Qasi and al-Qasiyy; one of the desert language folk recited to me in addressing his wife on a child she bore and he denied: meaning by al-Qasiyy: al-Qasi, and by al-Aliyy: al-Ali. Then someone said: In what you mentioned of these hadiths is an added letter in script: the alif found in growing, missing in pure; how was that allowed so in the codices you mentioned? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: what we mentioned of differing in growing and pure is not relating from the Quran, but relating from Moses' speech to al-Khidr with what he spoke to him of that. Moses' tongue was other than our Prophet's tongue in which the Quran came down. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in these hadiths of growing and pure is relating of what Moses did of addressing al-Khidr therein. Relatings in tongues from tongues before that in other than those tongues may be related in differing words. And of that is His saying in His Book in what He related from His Prophet Zakariyya, upon him peace, of His answering him when he asked Him to make for him a sign, saying in one place of His Book: Your sign is you speak not to people three days except gesture; and saying in another place of it: Your sign is you speak not to people three nights sound; informing of one meaning mentioned in one place with nights whose days enter in them, and in the other place with days whose nights enter in them. Like that is His relating from Moses in describing the slain boy with the state he was upon with him that he is pure in the meaning of growing, and that he is growing in the meaning of pure. Then the return after that in recitation is what is found in the codices of them: in some affirming the alif, in some dropping the alif. That indicates that is wide, and what is recited of those two words is wide, uncensured whoever inclines to one of the two words and leaves the other; and Allah we ask for success."
"3590","495 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying on the believer: he is unwary, noble; and on the wicked: he is cunning, base. 3127 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Furafisah, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir or another, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer is unwary, noble; and the wicked is cunning, base."
"3591","3128 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Mubaraki narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Hajjaj ibn Furafisah, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with no doubt he mentioned in its chain."
"3592","3129 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Janab narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, with no doubt he mentioned in anything of its chain. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith to stand upon what is meant with it, Allah willing. We found al-ghirr in Arab speech is one with no guile with him, no inner differing from his outer. Whoever is so, Muslims are safe from his tongue and hand; and it is the believers' quality. We found the wicked's outer contrary to his inner; for his inner is what is disliked, and his outer contradicts that, like the hypocrite showing something not disliked of him, the Islam its folk praise him upon, hiding contrary to it, the disbelief Muslims blame him upon. Like that is the cunning one showing the meaning praiseworthy of him, till Muslims praise him upon that, hiding its opposite of what Muslims blame him upon. He is the wicked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described with what he described him in this hadith, differing between him and the believer he described with what he described him in this hadith; and Allah we ask for success."
"3593","496 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Qurashi has twice a non-Qurashi man's strength. 3130 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Azdi al-Jizi and Sulayman ibn Shuayb ibn Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us, both said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zahir, from Jubayr ibn Mutim, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Qurashi has twice a non-Qurashi man's strength. Ibn Shihab said: Meant thereby is only nobility of opinion. He said: We reflected on this; its meaning with us - Allah knows best - is that upon the Qurashi of opinion, not upon others than opinion folk though Qurashi. For when a thing is described with a man of numbered folk, the quality may be ascribed to all those folk, though meant thereby is special of them. Like that is Allah's saying to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): It is a reminder for you and your folk, meaning his folk following him, believing in him, apart from others of his folk opposing him, disbelieving in him. Like that is His saying also to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Your folk denied it while it is the truth, meaning his folk denying him, opposing him, apart from his folk following him, believing in him. Like that is what he did in his qunut in prayer, of his supplicating against Mudar: Tighten Your grip, meaning opposing Mudar, not following Mudar. This is wide in speech. In Allah's Book is a place the reciters differed in reciting it: His saying: O believers, be Allah's helpers. Asim, Hamzah, and al-Kisai's recitation, in what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted me from Abu Ubayd: Allah's helpers; and Abu Jafar, Shaybah, Nafi, and Abu Amr's recitation: helpers to Allah, with nunation. Abu Ubayd said in this permission: With us it is: Allah's helpers, by annexation not nunation, for their agreeing upon what after that indicates it: His saying: The disciples said: We are Allah's helpers; He did not say: helpers to Allah."
"3594","Abu Ubayd Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam and Abd al-Aziz ibn Yahya al-Makki differed over this letter's recitation. Abu Ubayd said what we related from him in what Ali permitted us from him; and al-Makki said what we related from Abu Jafar and Nafi therein. He said: Then al-Makki argued therein against Abu Ubayd, saying: When we recite it: Allah's helpers, by annexation, we thereby deny Allah has helpers besides them. Abu Ubayd argued against him therein, saying: It is allowed in a thing when abundant to annex to all of it what is of part of it; so thereby it is allowed if said to part of Allah's helpers: they are Allah's helpers, though meant thereby is part of Allah's helpers. He said: Enters therein what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter of this meaning, of what we are free from repeating in this chapter. What Abu Ubayd chose of what we mentioned from him is established with what we mentioned; and Allah we ask for success."
"3595","497 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Look to Quraysh, hear of their speech, and leave their deed. 3131 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Muhriz Abu Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Amir ibn Shahr, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Look to Quraysh; hear of their speech, and leave their deed. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith; its meaning with us - Allah knows best - is that meant of Quraysh commanded to hear of their speech are those of speech that must be heard, not others than opinion-speech that must be heard. Likewise his saying: and leave their deed, is also upon those of them of blameworthy deed, not others of praiseworthy deed; and Allah we ask for success."
"3596","498 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in choosing of what was recited upon him Allah's saying: Allah who created you of weakness, or of weakness, upon what was recited upon him of these two letters. 3132 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us; and Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, both said: al-Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Atiyyah al-Awfi, he said: I recited upon Abdullah ibn Umar: Allah who created you of weakness, then made after weakness strength, then made after strength weakness. He returned upon me: Allah who created you of weakness, then made after weakness strength, then made after strength weakness. Then he said to me: I recited upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as you recited upon me, and he returned upon me as I returned upon you. Abu Jafar said: This is a hadith we know no other narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter besides it. In it is his returning upon Abdullah ibn Umar: weakness, in place of his reciting weakness. Though reciters differed therein: some's recitation upon weakness, some's upon weakness. With us the worthier therein is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, though wide for people to recite the other recitation; for impossible with us they recited it except from where it was allowed them to recite it; and because many of them recited this letter upon what he recited it upon who recited it weakness. It may be the differing therein came from the aspect we mentioned in what preceded of these chapters of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite to people, and they take it from him as he recites it to them; then he presents the Quran to Gabriel, and changes of that what He changes. One of these two meanings may have met change, and the other meaning is what was made in the first meaning's place, though they do not narrate it textually from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Thereby with us reciting with each of the two letters is wide. Except what distinguished of these two meanings the other meaning of them with relating of who related it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his returning it upon who recited upon him the other letter of those two letters, is worthier by choice. And Allah we ask for success. The recitation folk differed in this letter: some recited it with dammah. Of those reciting so of them: Abu Jafar, Shaybah, Nafi, Abdullah ibn Abi Ishaq, Abu Amr, and al-Kisai. Some recited it with fathah. Of those reciting so of them: Yahya ibn Waththab, Asim, and al-Amash. Likewise Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us from Abu Ubayd; and he mentioned to us from Abu Ubayd his choosing the first recitation: of weakness, following the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with whoever followed him upon it; and with Allah is success."
"3597","499 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his commanding the finder to witness upon what he found, and what is meant thereby. 3133 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir al-Dubai narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif, meaning Ibn Abdullah, from Iyad ibn Himar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever picks up a find, let him witness one just, or he said: two just; then let him not conceal nor alter. If its owner comes, he is worthier of it; otherwise it is Allah's wealth He gives whom He wills. Abu Jafar said: This hadith is narrated from other than this aspect upon what we mentioned, upon doubt of some narrators in what the finder is commanded therein, of witnessing one just or two just, not upon the Messenger of Allah's giving him choice to witness upon that whichever of those two kinds he wills. It is a hadith circling upon Khalid al-Hadhdha; its narrators from him differed therein: Shubah narrated it from him upon what we mentioned, and Hammad ibn Salamah narrated it upon him contrary to that."
"3598","3134 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah, from Iyad ibn Himar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the find, and said: It is announced, not hidden nor concealed. If its owner comes, otherwise it is Allah's wealth He gives whom He wills. Abu Jafar said: Shubah and Hammad differed in this hadith's chain we mentioned: Shubah mentioned it from Khalid from Yazid from Mutarrif; Hammad mentioned it from Khalid from Abu Qilabah from Mutarrif. They differed in its text: Shubah mentioned therein witnessing, and Hammad did not mention it. Hammad also narrated it from another path than this path returning to Mutarrif, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3599","3135 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Said, from Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it, I mean Iyad ibn Himar's hadith we began mentioning in this chapter. So we needed to stand upon the retention of what is in this hadith, of one just or two just, what it is?"
"3600","3136 - We found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Iyad ibn Himar al-Mujashii, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever picks up a find, let him witness two just; let him not conceal nor hide. If its lord comes, he is worthier of it; otherwise it is Allah's wealth He gives whom He wills."
